%requires qore >= 1.0
# need mime definitions
%requires Mime >= 1.0
%requires Util >= 1.0
%requires(reexport) HttpServerUtil >= 1.0
%requires Logger
%requires AsyncSocketIo
%requires HttpServerAsyncIo

%try-module sysconf
%define NO_SYSCONF
%endtry

%modern
%allow-weak-references
# the request contexts' references to the async I/O controllers are not followed by the collector; see the "@="
# comments where they are assigned
%allow-opaque-references
%enable-debug

module HttpServer {
    version = "1.7";
    desc = "HttpServer module for a multi-threaded HTTP server";
    author = "David Nichols <david@qore.org>";
    url = "http://qore.org";
    license = "MIT";
}

/** @mainpage HttpServer Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section httpserverintro HttpServer Introduction

    The %HttpServer module provides a multi-threaded HTTP server to Qore programs.

    The %HttpServer implemented is designed for serving REST, RPC-style, and file-system-based requests as well as
    other types of traffic with an appropriate handler (for example handlers requiring protocol changes such as with
    the WebSocket protocol are supported as well).

    The HttpServer module uses async I/O for all connection handling and supports HTTP/1.x, HTTP/2, and HTTP/3.
    Async I/O uses a non-blocking I/O controller to accept connections and read headers efficiently, then dispatches
    request processing to the thread pool. See the @ref httpserverasynciointro "HttpServerAsyncIo" module for
    additional details.

    To use this module, use \c "%requires HttpServer" in your code.  See examples/httpserver.qr for an example program
    using this module

    All the public symbols in the module are defined in the HttpServer namespace.

    The main classes is (see @ref httpserverutilintro "HttpServerUtil" for supporting definitions; note that the
    @ref httpserverutilintro "HttpServerUtil" module's definitions are also imported into @ref Qore::Program "Program"
    objects @ref requires "requiring" this module):
    - @ref HttpServer::HttpServer "HttpServer": this class implements the main interface to the HTTP server provided
      by this module

    See also:
    - @ref resthandlerintro "RestHandler": a module providing a handler framework for this
      module for implementing server-side REST services
    - @ref websockethandlerintro "WebSocketHandler": a module providing a handler framework
      for this module for implementing server-side WebSocket services
    - @ref webutilintro "WebUtil": a module providing higher-level HTTP services using this
      module as a base as well as providing support for dynamic template-based HTML rendering (ie rendering qhtml
      files which are a mix of HTML and Qore code)
    - @ref jsonrpchandlerintro "JsonRpcHandler":
      provides infrastructure for implementing JSON-RPC server-side services using the HttpServer module
    - @ref yamlrpchandlerintro "YamlRpcHandler":
      provides infrastructure for implementing YAML-RPC server-side services using the HttpServer module
    - <a href="https://docs.qore.org/modules/xml/current/XmlRpcHandler/html/index.html#xmlrpchandlerintro">XmlRpcHandler</a>:
      provides infrastructure for implementing XML-RPC server-side services using the HttpServer module
    - <a href="https://docs.qore.org/modules/xml/current/SoapHandler/html/index.html#soaphandlerintro">SoapHandler</a>: provides
      infrastructure for implementing SOAP server-side services using the HttpServer module

    @section httpserver_dispatch Choosing How a Request Is Dispatched

    A request reaches a handler in one of three ways, from the most general to the cheapest:

    |!Way|!What it does|!What it skips|!Cost of a trivial response
    |a handler's \c handleRequest()|runs on a handler pool thread with the full request context, authentication, logging, \
        request body handling and streaming|nothing|the baseline (about 7.8 million user-space instructions of server CPU \
        per request on a Linux x86_64 release build)
    |an inline handler (@ref HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler::canHandleInline() "canHandleInline()" returns \
        @ref True "True")|runs on the async callback dispatcher worker, in the thread that read the request headers|the \
        hand-off to the handler pool, the request context, authentication and logging|about a quarter of the baseline \
        (about 1.9 million instructions)
    |a fast route (@ref HttpServer::HttpServer::addFastRoute() "addFastRoute()")|is answered on the async I/O thread \
        without calling Qore code|every handler, and so authentication, logging and request preprocessing|a few \
        microseconds of server CPU per request

    The example program <tt>examples/fast-routes.qr</tt> serves one request each of the three ways.

    Use the first for everything that needs the request, the context or authentication, that reads a request body, that
    streams, or that can block.  Use the second for a handler that only builds a response in memory from the request
    headers, never blocks and never waits (a health or readiness check, a static response, a redirect): a handler
    that waits stalls the callback dispatcher worker for every other connection while it waits.  An inline handler
    gets only the connection's peer information in its context, receives no request body (a request with a body takes
    the normal path), and must return its message body as a string or binary value.  Use the third for a path whose
    answer depends on nothing but a few values that the program updates (see
    @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::addFastRoute() "addFastRoute()").

    @section httpserver_after_send Running Code After the Response Has Been Sent

    A handler returns its response; the server sends it afterwards.  To run code once the response has been sent,
    for example to send a reply to a separate address only after the client has received the response to its
    request, set the \c after_send key of the @ref HttpServer::HttpResponseInfo "HttpResponseInfo" hash.  The
    callback is called exactly once with the request context and @ref True "True" if the response has been completely
    sent, or @ref False "False" if it could not be sent:

    @code{.py}
# accepts a request with 202 Accepted and sends the reply to the address in the request's "Reply-To" header once
# the client has been sent the 202 response
class DecoupledReplyHandler inherits AbstractHttpRequestHandler {
    hash<HttpResponseInfo> handleRequest(hash<auto> cx, hash<auto> hdr, *data body) {
        string reply_to = hdr."reply-to";
        string reply = sprintf("processed %d bytes", body.size());
        return <HttpResponseInfo>{
            "code": 202,
            "after_send": sub (hash<auto> cx, bool sent) {
                if (sent) {
                    HTTPClient client({"url": reply_to});
                    client.post(NOTHING, reply, {"Content-Type": MimeTypeText});
                }
            },
        };
    }
}
    @endcode

    See @ref HttpServer::HttpResponseInfo::after_send "HttpResponseInfo::after_send" for when a response counts as
    sent with each HTTP version and on which thread the callback runs.

    @section httpserverhttp3 HTTP/3 (QUIC) Support

    When a TLS listener is added with \c enable_h3 (or when
    \c enable_h3 defaults to \c True), the server automatically creates a paired QUIC/UDP
    listener on the \b same port as the TCP listener.  This follows the standard HTTP/3
    deployment model where TCP (HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/2) and UDP (HTTP/3 over QUIC) share the
    same port number; browsers discover HTTP/3 availability via the \c Alt-Svc response header.

    @par Paired Listener Architecture
    - Each TCP/TLS listener with HTTP/3 enabled gets a paired QUIC listener
    - The QUIC listener binds to the same port on UDP
    - Handlers added via
      @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::addHandlerToListenerID() "addHandlerToListenerID()" are automatically propagated to the paired QUIC listener
      (with a \c "-h3" suffix on the handler name)
    - Handler removal via
      @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::removeHandlerFromListenerID() "removeHandlerFromListenerID()" also removes the propagated handler from the paired
      QUIC listener
    - Stopping a TCP listener also stops its paired QUIC listener
    - The \c Alt-Svc header is set per-listener so each TCP listener only advertises its own
      QUIC counterpart

    @par Configuration
    - \c enable_h3: set to \c True in @ref HttpServer::HttpListenerOptionInfo to enable HTTP/3 for a
      listener (defaults to \c True for TLS listeners when QUIC support is available)
    - \c alt_svc_max_age: the \c max-age value for the \c Alt-Svc header in seconds
      (default: 86400)

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
%requires HttpServer

HttpServer server();
server.setDefaultHandler("api", my_handler);
# HTTPS listener with automatic HTTP/3 — QUIC binds to the same port on UDP
server.addListener({"service": 8443, "cert": cert, "key": key});
    @endcode

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
#!/usr/bin/env qore

%requires HttpServer
%requires Mime

class MyHandler inherits AbstractHttpRequestHandler {
    hash<HttpResponseInfo> handleRequest(hash<auto> cx, hash<auto> hdr, *data body) {
        # NOTE: change "%y" to "%Y" to get a more readable multi-line output format for container values
        log("request received on %s from %s: context: %y hdr: %y body size: %d byte%s", cx."peer-info".desc,
            cx."socket-info".desc, http_mask_value(cx), http_mask_value(hdr), body.size(), body.size() == 1 ? "" : "s");
        return {
            "code": 200,
            "body": sprintf("<title>Test Page</title><body><h1>Qore HTTP server v%s</h1><p>%y on %s PID %s TID %d "
                "connection %d</p><p>Qore %s</p></body>", HttpServer::Version, now_us(), gethostname(), getpid(),
                gettid(), cx.id, Qore::VersionString),
            "close": True,
        };
    }
}

sub log(string str, ...) {
    printf("%y: %s\n", now_us(), vsprintf(str, argv));
}

const MyHttpPort = 19001;

# create our handler object
MyHandler myHandler();

# create the http server object
HttpServer hs(\log(), \log());

# add our handler to the server
hs.setHandler("my-handler", "/", MimeTypeHtml, myHandler);

# set our handler as the default handler
hs.setDefaultHandler("my-handler", myHandler);

# start a listener
hash<auto> lh = hs.addListener(MyHttpPort);

# output a log message
log("started listener on %s", lh.desc);
    @endcode

    See @subpage http_relnotes "HttpServer Module Release Notes" for the changes in each version.
*/
/** @page http_relnotes HttpServer Module Release Notes


    @section http1_7 HttpServer 1.7
    - every Server-Sent Events stream bounds the data held for its client (the \c sse_max_buffer_size listener
      option, default 1 MiB): queueing an event never waits for a client that stops reading, on HTTP/1.x, HTTP/2 and
      HTTP/3 alike, and a client whose data would exceed the bound is dropped, with a warning in the listener's log
      and the end of its stream reported as for any other end (see @ref httpserverasyncio_sse_buffer)
    - an HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 extended CONNECT (RFC 8441, RFC 9220) that a handler answers with an ordinary
      (non-streaming) 2xx response, such as a catch-all handler serving a page, is refused with @c 501 instead of
      being sent as a response that promises a tunnel and ends the stream at once (issue #5667); see
      @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::refuseNonTunnelExtendedConnectResponse() "refuseNonTunnelExtendedConnectResponse()"
    - added @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::addFastRoute() "fast routes": a path that is answered on the async I/O thread,
      without a hand-off to a worker thread and without calling a handler, for the requests that qualify
      (@ref HttpServer::HttpServer::addFastRoute(), @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::removeFastRoute(),
      @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::getFastRouteInfo(), @ref Qore::FastCell "FastCell" and
      @ref Qore::HttpFastRoutes "HttpFastRoutes"); a request served by a fast route costs about 10 to 20 microseconds
      of server CPU instead of milliseconds
    - fixed logging sensitive data: logged message headers and bodies are masked as with
      @ref HttpServer::http_mask_value() "http_mask_value()", and a binary body sent is logged only by its size
    - fixed @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::setListenerLogOptions() "setListenerLogOptions()": logging response bodies
      was set by @ref HttpServer::LLO_RECV_BODY "LLO_RECV_BODY" instead of @ref HttpServer::LLO_SEND_BODY "LLO_SEND_BODY"
    - request and response log messages are masked and formatted only if they will be logged; added
      @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::isLogInfoEnabled() "HttpServer::isLogInfoEnabled()"; whether the request line,
      the request body and the response of a request are logged at the \c INFO level is decided once when the request
      starts (for each request of a persistent session too), so a change of the log level applies to them from the
      next request
    - fixed the \c hdr option of @ref HttpServer::HttpServerOptionInfo "HttpServerOptionInfo" with values that all
      have the same type, such as a literal <tt>{"X-Instance": 1}</tt>: responses failed with status 500
      (\c RUNTIME-TYPE-ERROR); @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::sendHttpError() "sendHttpError()" also accepts extra
      headers whose values all have the same type
    - a request whose path contains an escaped NUL character (\c %00), which names no resource, is answered with
      status 400 (Bad Request) before a handler is selected, over HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3; before, the handler
      was selected by the path cut at the NUL character, so that \c "/a%00b" was handled by the handler of \c "/a",
      or the decoded path reached the default handler
    - an HTTP/1.x request with a NUL character in a header line is answered with status 400 (Bad Request), and the
      connection is closed, as a request with one in its request line already was; before, the request was handled
      without the header lines from the NUL character on
    - an HTTP/1.x request with a field line that RFC 9112 does not allow, such as a bare CR, whitespace before the
      colon (<tt>"Host : x"</tt>), an obsolete line folding or a line without a colon, is answered with status 400
      (Bad Request), and the connection is closed, as RFC 9112 requires; before, the request was handled, and an
      obsolete line folding or a line without a colon ended the header, so that the header lines after it, such as
      \c Content-Length, were lost
    - @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::stop() "HttpServer::stop()" and stopping a listener close the dedicated
      connections, such as WebSocket connections, with their protocol's closing handshake, all at the same time: the
      stop waits at most the handlers' close timeout in total for the clients to answer the server's close frame
      (code 1001, going away); before, a WebSocket connection was closed as soon as the server's close frame was sent
    - response bodies are compressed on the fly with levels suited to compressing them for each request, set with the
      new \c compression_levels server option and
      @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::setCompressionLevels() "setCompressionLevels()" (defaults: Brotli quality 5, Zstandard level 3, gzip and deflate level 6, bzip2 level
      3; see @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::DefaultCompressionLevels "DefaultCompressionLevels")
    - among the content encodings that a client accepts with the same weight, the cheapest one to compress is used
      (Zstandard, then Brotli, gzip, deflate, and bzip2; see
      @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::ContentEncodingPreference "ContentEncodingPreference"); weights and exclusions in \c Accept-Encoding are honored
    - generated response charset parameters use canonical encoding names such as \c utf-8,
      including default content types and error responses
    - text is sent in the character encoding that the response declares: a string or a
      @ref Qore::StringInputStream "StringInputStream" in another encoding is converted to the charset of the
      \c Content-Type, or to the response encoding; a string was sent in its own encoding and labeled with the
      response encoding, and a stream was converted to the socket encoding
    - fixed a response with a @ref Qore::StringInputStream "StringInputStream" body closing the connection without
      a response
    - a response body without a \c Content-Type is labeled by its data: a string as \c text/html in the response
      encoding as before, binary data (including a binary stream) as \c application/octet-stream without a charset,
      and a body produced by a \c send_callback without a \c Content-Type (RFC 9110 section 8.3); binary data was
      labeled as HTML text in the response encoding, and a stream body had no type; error responses are labeled
      the same way, and no charset is added to the type of a binary error message
    - request bodies are decoded by the rules shared by all HTTP clients and servers (see
      @ref Qore::decode_http_body() "decode_http_body()") whether they are read from the socket, buffered, or
      received over HTTP/2 or HTTP/3; the default text encoding
      (@ref HttpServer::HttpServer::setDefaultTextEncoding() "setDefaultTextEncoding()") is the assumed encoding for text without an encoding declaration, and no longer
      replaces the encoding of a JSON body or one declared in the body
    - every request handler receives the effective maximum request body size of the listener
      (@ref HttpServer::HttpListener::getMaxRequestBodySize() "HttpListener::getMaxRequestBodySize()") in
      \c cx."header-info".max_request_body_size (\c 0 = no limit), including handlers on an automatically created
      HTTP/3 listener; a handler that reads the request body as a stream enforces the total with it, since the
      server limits only the data it buffers ahead of such a handler
    - a request body with a \c Content-Encoding is decompressed only up to a maximum size, and a larger body receives
      HTTP 413 (Content Too Large); decompression stops as soon as the limit is exceeded, so a small compressed body
      that expands to a huge one (a "decompression bomb") can no longer exhaust the memory of the server.  The limit
      is the new \c max_decoded_body_size server option
      (@ref HttpServer::HttpServerOptionInfo::max_decoded_body_size "HttpServerOptionInfo::max_decoded_body_size",
      @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::setMaxDecodedBodySize() "HttpServer::setMaxDecodedBodySize()"), listener option,
      or handler setting, the \c max_request_body_size of the listener when none is set, and otherwise
      @ref HttpServer::DefaultMaxDecodedBodySize "DefaultMaxDecodedBodySize" (128 MiB)
    - a listener added with a service or port and no address listens on all interfaces in both address families
      with one dual-stack socket, so a client reaches it through any address of the host, including <tt>::1</tt>, which
      \c "localhost" resolves to first; it was bound on the IPv4 wildcard address only, so such a client could
      reach an unrelated IPv6 service holding the same port number instead.  IPv4 clients are reported with their
      IPv4 addresses in the \c "peer-info" of the request context
    - fixed sending two conflicting response header fields when a handler or the server configuration used a
      name in another case than the server, as with \c "content-type": the server added its own
      \c Content-Type, \c Date, \c Server, or \c Connection field, and a configured header was not replaced by
      the handler's; header names are now matched case-insensitively, also for the configured headers that a
      subclass sets or changes after the constructor, such as security headers like \c X-Frame-Options (see
      @ref HttpServer::http_canonicalize_reply_header_names() "http_canonicalize_reply_header_names()")
    - a multipart request body is delivered to the handler as binary data, since the request charset describes
      neither the container nor the parts inside it, each of which carries its own encoding
    - response compression honors Accept-Encoding quality values, exclusions, aliases, wildcard preferences,
      and explicit identity preferences consistently across HTTP/1, HTTP/2, and HTTP/3
    - @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::waitStop() "waitStop()" accepts an optional timeout and returns whether all
      listeners stopped, so a server shutdown can be bounded instead of blocking indefinitely on a request
      handler that never returns; the no-argument call is unchanged
    - new @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::getRunningListenerCount() "getRunningListenerCount()" method, so callers
      can report what is still in flight after a bounded wait times out
    - @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::stop() "stop()" lets the requests in progress finish before it closes the
      connections (HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 \c GOAWAY, HTTP/1.1 \c Connection: \c close), waiting at most the new
      \c drain_timeout option (the \c quic_drain_timeout option is a deprecated alias);
      @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::stopNoWait() "stopNoWait()" does the same in the background, and the new
      @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::stopImmediate() "stopImmediate()" stops at once, as \c stop() did before
    - fixed keeping the connection open after the response to an HTTP/1.0 request without the \c keep-alive
      connection option, or to a request with the \c close option (RFC 9112 section 9.3): a handler can close a
      connection, but a handler response can no longer keep open a connection that the request requires to close
    - fixed sending chunked transfer encoding in responses to HTTP/1.0 requests (RFC 9112 section 6.1): a message
      body of known length is sent with a \c Content-Length, and a streamed message body of unknown length is
      delimited by closing the connection (RFC 9112 section 6.3);
      @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::sendHttpError() "sendHttpError()" sends a streamed error message to an HTTP/1.0
      client with a \c Content-Length, and sends no message body in a response to a \c HEAD request
    - fixed the \c Content-Length of a request (RFC 9110 section 8.6): the value is the digits and nothing else
      (@ref HttpServer::http_parse_content_length()); a value such as \c +0, \c -1 or \c 0x0 is refused with
      \c 400 instead of making the server wait for a message body until the read timed out, and \c 00 is a message
      body of length 0
    - fixed the response to a \c HEAD request (RFC 9110 section 9.3.2): it has the headers of the response to a
      \c GET request, including the character encoding of the \c Content-Type and the \c Content-Encoding, no message
      body, and a \c Content-Length only if it is the length of the \c GET response's message body (it was always
      \c 0), and an error response to a \c HEAD request has no message body
    - new @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::acceptsHttpMethod() "acceptsHttpMethod()" method
    - the time allowed for an I/O operation of a connection, which was always 30 seconds, can be set with the new
      \c io_operation_timeout option in @ref HttpServer::HttpServerOptionInfo "HttpServerOptionInfo"
    - fixed an error response that the server sends for a request, for example \c 400 for an invalid
      \c Content-Length or \c 413 for a body that is too large: a second response (\c 500, "returned without sending a
      response") followed it on the same connection
    - an HTTP/1.x connection that the server closes after a response is closed with a lingering close (see the new
      \c lingering_close_timeout option in @ref HttpServer::HttpServerOptionInfo "HttpServerOptionInfo"), so a
      client that is still sending data the server did not read, such as the rest of a request body answered with
      an error, receives the response instead of a connection reset; the new
      @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::closeConnectionAfterResponse() "closeConnectionAfterResponse()" method closes a
      connection in this way after a response sent by a handler thread
    - allow the listener class to be overridden and added \c "listener-name" to the context hash
    - persistent (dedicated-thread) handler sessions are stream-scoped on HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, which carry many
      independent streams on one connection: a handler that calls \c AbstractHttpRequestHandler::setPersistent()
      on such a connection is released after its response, and its thread-local context is torn down; a session
      across requests on HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 must be a single bidirectional streaming request.  HTTP/1.x sessions are
      connection-scoped
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5351">issue 5351</a>)
    - fixed request path decoding so the resource path is URL-decoded while the query string remains encoded until
      parsed with @ref HttpServer::parse_uri_query() "parse_uri_query()", preventing encoded query separators such as
      \c "%26", \c "%3D", and \c "%3B" from corrupting query parameters
    - added HTTP/3 (QUIC) support with automatic listener creation for TLS listeners
    - added per-listener \c Alt-Svc header support for advertising HTTP/3 availability; each TCP
      listener only advertises its own QUIC counterpart to prevent browsers from confusing origins
    - QUIC listeners inherit all configuration from their parent TCP listener (\c additional_info)
      including cookie names, cookie domains, OIDC flags, and public URLs
    - added \c enable_h3 and \c alt_svc_max_age configuration options
    - added maximum HTTP request body size enforcement (\c max_request_body_size)
    - added \c sync option to allow non-blocking listener addition
    - added Brotli (\c br) and Zstandard (\c zstd) Content-Encoding support
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5071">issue 5071</a>)
      - responses can be compressed with Brotli or Zstd when clients send appropriate \c Accept-Encoding headers
      - requires Qore built with Brotli and/or Zstd support
    - return HTTP 204 when a handler returns code 200 with no response body
    - request contexts get numeric \c "peer-info" without a reverse DNS lookup; with the
      @ref HttpServer::HttpServerOptionInfo::peer_hostname_lookup "peer_hostname_lookup" option the lookup is made in
      parallel and is available in the optional \c "peer-info-future" key for handlers that need it
    - added @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::addListenerAsync() "addListenerAsync()" and
      @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::addListenersAsync() "addListenersAsync()" methods for Future-based listener setup
    - fixed a shutdown crash when HttpServer instances created with the deprecated logger callback constructor were
      torn down while async logging was still active
    - introduced async I/O for HttpServer (now the only mode): a single I/O thread accepts connections and handles
      TLS and the reading of headers, and requests are dispatched to a thread pool
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5019">issue 5019</a>)
    - @ref Qore::InputStream "InputStream" response bodies are streamed incrementally, without buffering the whole
      body, with backpressure over HTTP/1.x, HTTP/2 and HTTP/3
    - added the \c after_send response callback, called once after the response has been sent or has failed to be
      sent (see @ref httpserver_after_send)

    @section http1_4 HttpServer 1.4
    - allow authentication methods to set the call context
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4970">issue 4970</a>)

    @section http1_3 HttpServer 1.3
    - fixed a bug where idle connections were never disconnected
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4878">issue 4878</a>)

    @section http1_2 HttpServer 1.2
    - allow handlers to remove thread-local auth information after requests have been handled

    @section http1_1_2 HttpServer 1.1.2
    - allow context info to be cleared after handling each request to ensure that context info (like thread-local
      data) does not leak into other connections on the same thread
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4802">issue 4802</a>)

    @section http1_1_1 HttpServer 1.1.1
    - fixed a bug reloading X.509 certificates on running listeners
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4720">issue 4720</a>)

    @section http1_1 HttpServer 1.1
    - implemented support for reloading X.509 certificate information without manually restarting listeners
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4574">issue 4574</a>)

    @section http1_0_12 HttpServer 1.0.12
    - fixed a bug where incorrect URI decoding was applied to URLs in received HTTP messages instead of URL decoding
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4363">issue 4363</a>)

    @section http1_0_2 HttpServer 1.0.2
    - @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::addListeners() "HttpServer::addListeners()" has no mechanism for returning errors
      to the caller
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4312">issue 4312</a>)

    @section http1_0_1 HttpServer 1.0.1
    - allow dynamic handlers to be removed without waiting for all connections to terminate
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4273">issue 4273</a>)
    - allow for full logging of authentication errors if HTTP server debugging is enabled
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4217">issue 4217</a>)

    @section http1_0 HttpServer 1.0
    - allow dynamic handlers to be disabled before being removed
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4173">issue 4173</a>)
    - added support for the Logger module for logging
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4157">issue 4157</a>)
    - removed deprecated APIs

    @section http0911 HttpServer 0.9.11
    - fixed a bug shutting down dedicated socket I/O handlers
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4130">issue 4130</a>)
    - fixed a bug where invalid HTTP responses were send with error messages and chunked transfer encoding
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4124">issue 4124</a>)
    - fixed a bug where it was not possible for an HTTP stream handler to support switching protocols
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/4111">issue 4111</a>)

    @section http095 HttpServer 0.9.5
    - fixed a bug where the HTTP server would not always stop the ThreadPool which caused process shutdowns to hang
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/3999">issue 3999</a>)

    @section http094 HttpServer 0.9.4
    - added support for sending chunked replies from an @ref Qore::InputStream "InputStream"
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/3702">issue 3702</a>)

    @section http03131 HttpServer 0.3.13.1
    - enable remote certificates to be retrieved in listeners and handlers by default; added new API entry points for
      listeners and handlers with more flexible and saner parameters, deprecated old complex methods
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/3512">issue 3512</a>)
    - shut down dedicated socket conenctions last in order to allow for effective keep-alive implementations with
      WebSocket for example
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/3488">issue 3488</a>)
    - fixed certificate and key errors for HTTPS listeners to generate user-friendly exceptions
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/3397">issue 3397</a>)

    @section http0313 HttpServer 0.3.13
    - added the \c "header-info" hash to the context argument when calling handlers
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/3026">issue 3026</a>)
    - added support for adding new HTTP methods to the server with the
      @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::addHttpMethod() "HttpServer::addHttpMethod()" method
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/2805">issue 2805</a>)

    @section http03121 HttpServer 0.3.12.1
    - fixed \c HEAD and other responses that cannot have a message body
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/3116">bug 3116</a>)

    @section http0312 HttpServer 0.3.12
    - added a minimal substring of string bodies received to the log message when logging HTTP requests
    - added logic to allow sensitive data to be masked in log messages (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/1086">issue 1086</a>)
    - added support for the HTTP \c "PATCH" method

    @section http03111 HttpServer 0.3.11.1
    - fixed a bug where @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::addListener() would not accept port 0 meaning bind on any random open port (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/1284">bug 1284</a>)

    @section http0311 HttpServer 0.3.11
    - fixed a bug setting the response encoding in @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::setReplyHeaders() where the Socket encoding was not set properly and therefore the encoding in the \c Content-Type in the response header did not necessarily match the encoding of the response
    - added support for private keys with passwords
    - added the @ref HttpServer::PermissiveAuthenticator "PermissiveAuthenticator" class
    - moved base code to the @ref httpserverutilintro "HttpServerUtil" module (imported with the \c reexport option) and santized APIs, made HttpListener class private/internal again
    - fixed a socket / connection performance problem with HTTPS listeners where the SSL connection was being negotiated inline with the accept instead of in the connection thread, thereby blocking new connections from being accepted
    - new methods implemented in HttpServer:
      - @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::setListenerLogOptions()
      - @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::setListenerLogOptionsID()
    - performance improvement of matching handlers to request URIs (not used for RegExp matching)
    - changed the order of path-based handler matching:
      - the old implementation selects the first (in order of their registration) handler whose path matches the request URI, regexp and path matching have the same priority
      - the new implementation has two stages:
        - first, handlers without regexp are considered - if there are some handler with their path matching the request, the one with the longest path is selected
        - otherwise, the first (in order of their registration) handler with regexp that matches the request URI is chosen
      - if no path matches the request, the same logic as before applies
    - added @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::addListeners() "HttpServer::addListeners(string, hash, *code, *code, *code, *string, int)"
    - fixed a bug in @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::addListener() with an integer argument; a UNIX socket was opened instead of a wildcard listener on the given port
    - added the @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::listenerStarted() method to allow for reporting when listeners are actually running since they are started asynchronously
    - added the \c "ssl" key to the listener socket info hash
    - fixed typos causing bugs in HTTP error logging (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/308">bug 308</a>)
    - fixed bugs handling persistent connections

    @section http0310 HttpServer 0.3.10
    - if an error occurs receiving a message with chunked transfer encoding, send the response immediately before reading the rest of the chunked transfer

    @section http039 HttpServer 0.3.9
    - when binding a wildcard address with @ref Qore::AF_UNSPEC "AF_UNSPEC" on Windows with \c HttpServer::HttpServer::addListeners()
      and both IPv6 and IPv4 addresses are returned, bind both addresses since Windows doesn't direct the IPv4 requests to the wildcard-bound IPv6 listener

    @section http038 HttpServer 0.3.8
    - moved more message handling code to HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler so that chunked transfers can be efficiently supported by handlers
    - added a minimum body size threshold for \c Content-Encoding compression to HttpServer
    - added the following classes to facilitate handling chunked requests and responses:
      - HttpServer::AbstractStreamRequestHandler
      - HttpServer::AbstractStreamRequest
    - added support for persistent connections to a given handler to the HTTP server

    @section http037 HttpServer 0.3.7
    - fixed a bug in parsing arguments in @ref HttpServer::parse_uri_query() "parse_uri_query()"
    - updated @ref HttpServer::parse_uri_query() "parse_uri_query()" to accept both \c "&" and \c ";" as argument separators
    - added support for the \c PUT and \c DELETE methods and for optional message bodies independent of the HTTP method
    - fixed handler matching to use a score for each handler and to check dynamic handlers also for a match if the fixed handlers don't provide a perfect match
    - fixed response handling to not overwrite the \c Connection header if present from the handler
    - fixed response handling to only return error reponses with codes >= 400
    - implemented the  @ref HttpServer::AbstractHttpSocketHandler "AbstractHttpSocketHandler" handler class to support dedicated socket connections when switching protocols in handlers
    - do not add HTML to the msg body from explicit error responses from handlers
    - improved HTTP server log messages
    - added "family" argument to HttpServer::add*Listener*() methods
    - turned of \c TCP_NODELAY by default and use socket shutdown when closing sockets instead
    - set the listen backlog queue size to 100 by default (previously was hardcoded internally in %Qore to 5)
    - use the new @ref Qore::Thread::ThreadPool "ThreadPool" class introduced in %Qore 0.8.8 to pre-allocate threads to reduce latency for new socket connections
    - improved handler matching algorithm
    - implemented many performance improvements
    - implemented support for simple string path matching when finding a handler for a request
    - added static @ref HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler::redirect() to help in generating 301 Moved Permanently messages

    @section http036 HttpServer 0.3.6
    - fixed a race condition in tracking active requests in dynamic HTTP handlers and removing dynamic HTTP handlers

    @section http035 HttpServer 0.3.5
    - fixed bugs in regexes in the @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::addListeners() "HttpServer::addListeners()" and
      \c HttpServer::addListenersWithHandler() methods
    - updated module example code in summary
    - added \c "listener-id" to request context hash

    @section http034 HttpServer 0.3.4
    - added public @ref HttpServer::parse_uri_query() "parse_uri_query()" function

    @section http033 HttpServer 0.3.3
    - updated to a user module
    - added support for custom redirects from user handlers
    - use the @ref mimeintro "Mime" module for mime definitions

    @section http032 HttpServer 0.3.2
    - added support for listeners with specific handler lists
    - added the ability to manage dynamic content handlers
    - implemented content-encoding handling flags in @ref HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler "AbstractHttpRequestHandler"
    - implemented support for \c "identity" encoding (if anyone ever sends it)

    @section http031 HttpServer 0.3.1
    - added IPv6 support in qore 0.8.2

    @section http030 HttpServer 0.3.0
    - added the ability to start and stop listeners on demand

    @section http029 HttpServer 0.2.9
    - updates for new SSL and timeout behavior with with @ref Qore::Socket "Socket" class with qore 0.8.1+
    - set socket encoding to UTF-8 by default
    - add \c "charset=utf-8" to \c Content-Type header if not already present
    - add \c "text/html" to \c Content-Type header if no content-type is given by the handler
    - fixed setting X.509 certificate and private key for HTTPS listeners
    - require qore >= 0.8.1 for new Socket features

    @section http028 HttpServer 0.2.8
    - converted to hard typing for use with %require-types
    - require qore >= 0.8.0 for new @ref Qore::Socket "Socket" features

    @section http027 HttpServer 0.2.7
    - set \c TCP_NODELAY on all sockets to ensure that clients get all data before closing the socket, especially in case of errors
    - require qore >= 0.7.4 for new @ref Qore::Socket "Socket" features

    @section http026 HttpServer 0.2.6
    - minor fixes for SOAP support
    - improved handler matching

    @section http025 HttpServer 0.2.5
    - minor fixes for SOAP support

    @section http024 HttpServer 0.2.4
    - improved \c Content-Type handling
    - improved URL/path support

    @section http023 HttpServer 0.2.3
    - bzip2 \c Content-Encoding support

    @section http022 HttpServer 0.2.2
    - basic authentication

    @section http021 HttpServer 0.2.1
    - implementing logic to handle \c "deflate" and \c "gzip" content-encoding
    - chunked content-encoding supported for POSTs
    - Date: header always sent as per HTTP 1.1 spec

    @section http020 HttpServer 0.2.0
    - modular/multiple listener support added
    - https support added
*/

#! the main namespace for the HttpServer module
/** To use this module, use \c "%requires HttpServer" in your code.

    See examples/httpserver.qr for an example program using this module

    All the public symbols in the module are defined in this namespace
 */
public namespace HttpServer {
#! returns a multi-line string from the exception hash argument suitable for logging or output on the console
/** @param ex the exception hash to process

    @return a multi-line string from the exception hash argument suitable for logging or output on the console

    @note reexported from the Util module
*/
public string sub get_exception_string(hash<auto> ex) {
    return Util::get_exception_string(ex);
}

#! Hash for HttpServer options
public hashdecl HttpServerOptionInfo {
    #! HTTP server name
    /** Empty string = use HttpServer::HttpServerString default; actual substitution
        happens in HttpServer::init().  Direct use of a constant ref here would
        break AOT serialisation (the writer has no encoding for
        RuntimeConstantRefNode in hashdecl member defaults).
    */
    string name = "";

    #! Debug flag
    bool debug = False;

    #! Do not allow the last listener to be stopped
    bool protect_last_listener = False;

    #! Headers to add in responses
    /** Empty hash = use the built-in X-Powered-By header default; actual
        substitution happens in HttpServer::init().  See <tt>name</tt> member for
        the AOT-serialisation constraint.
    */
    hash<auto> hdr = {};

    #! Idle disconnect time; the default is 75 seconds
    /** 0s = use HttpServer::DefaultTtl default; actual substitution happens
        in HttpServer::init().  See <tt>name</tt> member for the AOT-serialisation
        constraint.
    */
    date ttl = 0s;

    #! HTTP server logger
    /** The process-wide async I/O controller (see @ref AsyncSocketIo::getGlobalAsyncIoController()) also logs through
        this logger while the server runs (see
        @ref Qore::AsyncIoController::setLogger() "AsyncIoController::setLogger()"); when the server stops, the
        controller gets back the logger it had before, unless its logger has been replaced since.  The controller has
        one logger for the whole process: with more than one server running, it logs through the logger of the server
        created last of them.
    */
    *LoggerInterface logger;

    #! Idle thread count for the connection thread pool
    /** 0 means auto-detect: 2 × CPU count (or HttpServer::DefaultIdleThreads on
        systems without sysconf(SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN)).  The auto-detect computation
        lives in HttpServer::init() rather than in the member initializer because
        an initializer expression like \c "sysconf(SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN) * 2" serialises
        as \c VT_NOTHING in the AOT binary format (the writer has no specialised
        encoding for a multiplication of a function-call result) — which yields
        \c idle_threads=0 after an AOT-compiled load.  Using 0 as a sentinel keeps
        the source-parse and AOT-parse paths identical.
    */
    int idle_threads = 0;

    #! Maximum number of handler threads for the async I/O thread pool (0 = unlimited)
    /** Controls the maximum number of concurrent threads that can be used for request processing.
        Set to 0 (default) for unlimited.

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    int max_handler_threads = 0;

    #! Minimum idle handler threads kept alive in the async I/O thread pool
    /**
        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    int min_idle_handler_threads = 0;

    #! Maximum idle handler threads in the async I/O thread pool
    /**
        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    int max_idle_handler_threads = 0;

    #! Maximum HTTP request body size in bytes (0 = unlimited)
    /** Requests with Content-Length exceeding this value, or chunked, HTTP/2, or HTTP/3 bodies
        that grow beyond this value, receive HTTP 413 (Content Too Large).  An HTTP/1.x connection
        is closed after the response; on HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 only the request's stream ends, and the
        rest of the body is discarded.  An HTTP/2 client that is still sending the body when the
        response has been sent is asked to stop with \c RST_STREAM(NO_ERROR) (RFC 9113 section 8.1), and an
        HTTP/3 client with \c STOP_SENDING(H3_NO_ERROR) (RFC 9114 section 4.1.2).

        A handler that reads the request body as a stream (see
        @ref HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler::wantsBodyStreaming() "wantsBodyStreaming()") enforces a limit on
        the total body size itself; for such a handler, this value limits the data that the server buffers for it,
        and a read of a body that exceeded it fails with \c HTTP-BODY-TOO-LARGE after the buffered data.

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    int max_request_body_size = 0;

    #! Maximum size in bytes of a decompressed request body
    /** A request body with a \c Content-Encoding is decompressed before it is passed to the handler; a body whose
        decompressed size exceeds this value receives HTTP 413 (Content Too Large) and the connection is closed.
        Decompression stops as soon as the limit is exceeded, so a small compressed body that expands to a huge one
        (a "decompression bomb") never allocates more than the limit.

        - a positive value is the limit
        - \c 0 (the default) uses the effective \c max_request_body_size of the listener, or
          @ref HttpServer::DefaultMaxDecodedBodySize "DefaultMaxDecodedBodySize" if there is no such limit
        - a negative value means no limit

        A listener can override the limit with
        @ref HttpServer::HttpListenerOptionInfo::max_decoded_body_size "HttpListenerOptionInfo::max_decoded_body_size",
        and a handler with
        @ref HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler::max_decoded_body_size "AbstractHttpRequestHandler::max_decoded_body_size".

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    int max_decoded_body_size = 0;

    #! The compression level of each content encoding for response bodies compressed on the fly
    /** A response body is compressed for each request in which the client accepts a supported content encoding
        (unless the handler sets the \c Content-Encoding or \c no_content_encoding), so the levels trade a little
        compression for speed; see @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::DefaultCompressionLevels "DefaultCompressionLevels"
        for the defaults.  The keys are content encodings: \c "br" (quality 0 - 11), \c "zstd" (level 1 -
        @ref Qore::ZSTD_MAX_LEVEL "ZSTD_MAX_LEVEL"), \c "gzip" and \c "deflate" (level 1 - 9), and \c "bzip2"
        (level 1 - 9); an encoding that is not given has its default level, and an unknown encoding or a level out of
        range raises an \c HTTP-SERVER-COMPRESSION-LEVEL-ERROR exception.

        The levels also apply to static files that @ref WebUtil::FileHandler "FileHandler" compresses on the fly;
        precompressed files are served as they are.

        @see @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::setCompressionLevels() "HttpServer::setCompressionLevels()"

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    hash<string, int> compression_levels = {};

    #! Enable automatic HTTP/3 (QUIC) listener creation for TLS listeners
    /** When \c True (the default), QUIC listeners are automatically created alongside TLS TCP listeners.
        The server advertises HTTP/3 availability via the \c Alt-Svc response header.

        Set to \c False to disable automatic QUIC listener creation server-wide.

        @note Individual listeners can override this via the \c enable_h3 field in
        @ref HttpServer::HttpListenerOptionInfo.

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    bool enable_h3 = True;

    #! Default max-age value for the Alt-Svc header (seconds)
    /** Controls how long clients should cache the HTTP/3 Alt-Svc advertisement.

        Default: 3600 (1 hour).

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    int alt_svc_max_age = 3600;

    #! The maximum time that @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::stop() "HttpServer::stop()" waits for the requests in progress to finish
    /** When the server stops, it accepts no new connections and tells its clients that it is closing their
        connections (HTTP/2 and HTTP/3: \c GOAWAY; HTTP/1.1: \c Connection: \c close), then waits at most this long
        for the requests that it has accepted to be answered before it closes whatever is left (see
        @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::stop() "HttpServer::stop()").

        The default is 5 seconds; zero or a negative value means that the server does not wait, as with
        @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::stopImmediate() "HttpServer::stopImmediate()".

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    *timeout drain_timeout;

    #! Deprecated: use \c drain_timeout instead
    /** Used as the \c drain_timeout option when that option is not set.

        @deprecated use \c drain_timeout, which applies to every HTTP version

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    *timeout quic_drain_timeout;

    #! The maximum time to wait for an HTTP/1.x client to close its side of the connection after the last response
    /** When the server closes an HTTP/1.x connection after a response, the client may still be sending data that
        the server did not read: the rest of a request body answered with an error, a pipelined request, or the
        remainder of an HTTP/2 connection preface.  Closing a connection with unread data makes the system reset it,
        and the reset can make the client discard the response before reading it.  The server therefore performs a
        "lingering close": it shuts down its sending side, so the client receives the end of the stream after the
        response, and discards what the client sends until the client closes its side or this timeout expires.

        A client that reads the response and closes the connection ends the wait at once.  0 closes the connection
        immediately after the response, without waiting.

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    timeout lingering_close_timeout = 5s;

    #! The time allowed for an I/O operation of a connection
    /** This is the time allowed to read a request header once a request has started to arrive, to read the first
        request of a new connection, and to send a response.  An idle keep-alive connection is waited for the idle
        time (\c ttl) plus this time, which covers reading the header of the next request: a connection that sends
        nothing for longer is closed.  A connection whose requests are answered by fast routes renews its timeout
        with each request.

        Must be greater than zero.

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    timeout io_operation_timeout = 30s;

    #! Resolves the host name of each client address, available to handlers in the request context
    /** When @ref True "True", the reverse DNS lookup of the address of each accepted connection is started when the
        connection is accepted and is available to handlers in the \c "peer-info-future" key of the request context
        (a @ref Qore::Thread::Future "Future"); the lookup does not delay the request.  The default is
        @ref False "False": the lookup costs a resolver channel and a query for every connection, which a server whose
        handlers do not use the host name should not pay, and the key is not set.

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    bool peer_hostname_lookup = False;

    #! Enable HTTP/3 headers-only dispatch mode
    /** When @ref True "True", HTTP/3 (and HTTP/2) requests are dispatched to handlers as
        soon as headers arrive, before the full body is received.  Handlers that override
        @ref AbstractHttpRequestHandler::wantsBodyStreaming() "wantsBodyStreaming()" and
        return @ref True "True" can read body data incrementally via
        @ref Qore::Socket::readQuicStreamDataBlock() (HTTP/3) or
        HttpConnectionInfo::header_info::register_body_queue (HTTP/2).

        Handlers that do @b not override \c wantsBodyStreaming() are unaffected — the
        framework automatically buffers the full body before calling \c handleRequest(),
        maintaining backward compatibility.

        This enables bidirectional streaming for gRPC-style, Connect protocol, and
        chunked upload use cases where processing should begin before the full body arrives.

        Default: @ref True "True" (since HttpServer 1.6).  This brings H3 to parity
        with the H2 server, which dispatches handlers on HEADERS unconditionally.
        Set explicitly to @ref False "False" to restore the legacy behavior of
        buffering the full request body server-side before handler dispatch — this
        is rarely useful and trades latency, memory, and early-reject semantics for
        no functional benefit.

        @par Example
        @code{.py}
        # Default: H3 dispatches on HEADERS, handlers can stream body
        HttpServer server();

        # Opt out (legacy buffer-then-dispatch):
        HttpServer server(<HttpServerOptionInfo>{
            "quic_headers_only": False,
        });
        @endcode

        @see @ref AbstractHttpRequestHandler::wantsBodyStreaming()

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    bool quic_headers_only = True;
}

#! The HttpServer class implements a multithreaded HTTP server
/**
    ## Lifecycle

    HttpServer instances are NOT designed to be restartable. Once stop() is called,
    the server enters a stopped state and cannot be restarted. To create a new server,
    instantiate a new HttpServer object.

    The design is intentionally non-restartable because:
    - Internal I/O controllers set shutdown flags that prevent restart
    - Thread pools and counters are not reset after stop()
    - Listeners are not designed to be restarted after being stopped

    @see HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpAsyncSocketIoController for callback-based async I/O
*/
public class HttpServer inherits HttpServer::AbstractLogger {
    public {
        #! version of the HttpServer's implementation
        const Version = HttpServer::HttpServerVersion;
        #! default read timeout in ms
        const ReadTimeout = HttpServer::ReadTimeout;  # recvs timeout after 30 seconds
        #! default poll timeout in ms
        const PollTimeout = 250ms;

        #! The default maximum time that stop() waits for the requests in progress (the \c drain_timeout option)
        /** @since HttpServer 1.7
        */
        const DefaultDrainTimeout = 5s;

        # logging options
        const LP_LOGPARAMS = HttpServer::LP_LOGPARAMS;
        const LP_LEVELMASK = HttpServer::LP_LEVELMASK;

        #! address info flags
        const AIFlags = AI_PASSIVE | AI_ADDRCONFIG;

        #! HTTP methods supported by default
        const hash<auto> HttpMethods = {
            "HEAD": True,
            "POST": True,
            "PUT": True,
            "DELETE": True,
            "GET": True,
            "OPTIONS": True,
            "PATCH": True,
            #"TRACE": True,
            "CONNECT": True,  # RFC 8441: HTTP/2 extended CONNECT for WebSocket
        };

        #! map of HTTP result codes and text messages
        const HttpCodes = HttpServer::HttpCodes;

        #! content-encodings supported; this is a hash to simulate a set with O(ln(n)) access times
        const hash<string, string> ContentEncodings = {
            "br": "br",
            "zstd": "zstd",
            "gzip": "gzip",
            "deflate": "deflate",
            "bzip2": "bzip2",
            "x-gzip": "gzip",
            "x-deflate": "deflate",
            "x-bzip2": "bzip2",
        };

        #! The content encodings in the order of preference among those that a client accepts with the same weight
        /** The order is that of the cost of compressing a response body on the fly with the default levels (see
            @ref DefaultCompressionLevels): a client that accepts several encodings with the same weight (ex: the
            \c "gzip, deflate, br, zstd" of a browser) gets the cheapest one to compress.  For a JSON body or a
            JavaScript bundle of 5 - 7 MB, Zstandard at level 3 takes about 0.02 s, Brotli at quality 5 about 0.1 s,
            and gzip at level 6 about 0.15 s.

            @since HttpServer 1.7
        */
        const ContentEncodingPreference = ("zstd", "br", "gzip", "deflate", "bzip2");

        #! The default compression level of each content encoding for response bodies compressed on the fly
        /** A response body is compressed for each request, so the levels are lower than the maximum: for a JSON body
            or a JavaScript bundle of 5 - 7 MB, Brotli at quality 5 compresses about 100 times faster than at the
            maximum quality 11 (the default of @ref Qore::brotli() "brotli()", which takes 5 - 10 seconds for such a
            body), with an output 10 - 30% larger.

            @see
            - @ref HttpServerOptionInfo::compression_levels
            - @ref setCompressionLevels()

            @since HttpServer 1.7
        */
        const DefaultCompressionLevels = {
            "br": 5,
            "zstd": 3,
            "gzip": 6,
            "deflate": 6,
            "bzip2": BZ2_DEFAULT_COMPRESSION,
        };

        #! The range of the compression level of each content encoding: the minimum and maximum levels
        /** @since HttpServer 1.7
        */
        const CompressionLevelRanges = {
            "br": (BROTLI_MIN_QUALITY, BROTLI_MAX_QUALITY),
            "zstd": (ZSTD_MIN_LEVEL, ZSTD_MAX_LEVEL),
            "gzip": (1, 9),
            "deflate": (1, 9),
            "bzip2": (1, 9),
        };

        #! default number of idle threads to have waiting for new connections (accross all listeners)
        const DefaultIdleThreads = 10;

        #! default threadhold for data compressions; transfers smaller than this size will not be compressed
        const CompressionThreshold = 1024;

        /** @defgroup ListenerLogOptions Listener Log Options
            Options to control how listener headers and body options are logged
            @see
            - HttpServer::getListenerLogOptions()
            - HttpServer::getListenerLogOptionsID()
            - HttpServer::setListenerLogOptions()
            - HttpServer::setListenerLogOptionsID()
         */
        #/@{
        #! listener log option: log recv headers
        const int LLO_RECV_HEADERS = (1 << 0);

        #! listener log option: log recv message body
        const int LLO_RECV_BODY = (1 << 1);

        #! listener log option: log send headers
        const int LLO_SEND_HEADERS = (1 << 2);

        #! listener log option: log sent message body
        const int LLO_SEND_BODY = (1 << 3);
        #/@}

        #! Default connection time-to-live
        const DefaultTtl = 75s;
    }

    #! @cond nodoc
    private {
        # for masking HTTP request log msgs
        *code<string(string)> maskfunc;

        # quit server flag
        bool exit = False;

        # the fast routes of the listeners (see addFastRoute()), created by the first route
        *HttpFastRoutes fast_routes;

        # guards fast_routes and quic_udp_sockets
        Mutex fast_routes_lock();

        # the UDP sockets of the QUIC listeners by listener ID, which the fast routes are attached to (the sessions of
        # the listener are created from them)
        hash<string, Socket> quic_udp_sockets;

        # the TCP listeners by listener ID, whose sockets get the listener's Alt-Svc header for the responses of the fast
        # routes (guarded by fast_routes_lock)
        hash<string, HttpListener> alt_svc_targets;

        # if True then verbose exception info will be logged
        bool debug;

        Sequence seqSessions();
        Sequence seqListeners();

        bool stopped = False;

        #! Stop guard
        Mutex stop_m();

        #! True while stop() is in progress
        bool stopping = False;

        #! Signaled with stop_m when a stop in progress has ended
        Condition stop_cond();

        #! The maximum time that stop() waits for the requests in progress (the \c drain_timeout option)
        timeout drain_timeout = DefaultDrainTimeout;

        # permanent handlers; these handlers are never removed
        HttpHandlerList handlers();

        # default handler
        *HandlerInfo defaultHandler;

        # hash of listeners keyed by listener ID
        hash<string, HttpListener> listeners;

        # map of bind addresses to listener IDs
        hash<string, int> smap;

        # map of listener names to listener IDs
        hash<string, int> nmap;

        # listener Gate
        Gate lm();

        # running listener WaitGroup
        WaitGroup c();

        # dynamic handlers
        DynamicHttpHandlerList dhandlers();

        # connection thread pool
        ThreadPool threadPool;

        # other misc response headers
        hash<auto> hdr;

        #! override message body encoding if none is received from the sender
        /** @note http://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc2616#section-3.7.1 states that it must be iso-8850-1
        */
        *string override_encoding;

        #! the name the HTTP server will report itself as
        string http_server_string;

        #! valid HTTP methods
        hash<string, bool> http_methods = HttpMethods;

        #! logger
        *LoggerInterface logger;
        #! callback logger appender for the deprecated constructor (if used)
        *LoggerAppender callback_appender;

        #! Do not allow last listener to be stopped
        bool protect_last_listener = False;

        #! Idle disconnect (TTL) for connections
        date ttl = DefaultTtl;

        #! Maximum HTTP request body size in bytes (0 = unlimited)
        int max_request_body_size = 0;

        #! Maximum size in bytes of a decompressed request body (0 = default, negative = unlimited)
        int max_decoded_body_size = 0;

        #! The compression level of each content encoding for response bodies compressed on the fly
        /** Replaced as a whole, so a request reads a consistent set of levels; see setCompressionLevels()
        */
        hash<string, int> compression_levels = DefaultCompressionLevels;

        #! Enable automatic HTTP/3 (QUIC) listener creation
        bool enable_h3 = True;

        #! Default max-age for Alt-Svc header (seconds)
        int alt_svc_max_age = 3600;

        #! Map of TCP listener ID -> auto-created QUIC listener ID
        /** Keys are string listener IDs (Qore auto-converts int keys to strings
            in hashes).  Values are the corresponding QUIC listener ID.
        */
        hash<string, int> quic_listener_map;

        #! Map of TCP listener ID -> QUIC port
        /** Keys are string listener IDs (same auto-conversion as quic_listener_map).
            Values are the UDP port of the QUIC listener.
        */
        hash<string, int> quic_port_map;

        #! Map of TCP listener ID -> per-listener Alt-Svc max-age
        /** Keys are string listener IDs.  Values are the max-age value for Alt-Svc headers.
        */
        hash<string, int> quic_alt_svc_max_age;

        #! Map of TCP listener ID -> per-listener Alt-Svc header value
        /** Each TCP listener advertises only its own QUIC counterpart, not all QUIC ports.
        */
        hash<string, string> listener_alt_svc;

        #! Async I/O controller
        *HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpAsyncSocketIoController asyncIo;

        #! Async target for callback-based async I/O
        /** Defaults to a @ref Priv::HttpServerNativeAsyncTarget instance constructed
            in the @ref HttpServer::HttpServer() constructor.  Subclasses may
            replace this in their own constructor (after calling the base
            constructor) with any other @ref HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpAsyncTarget —
            for example a wrapper that adds per-dispatch save/restore of
            framework-specific thread-local data via
            @ref HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpAsyncTarget::saveDispatchContext() /
            @ref HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpAsyncTarget::restoreDispatchContext().
            Listener registration must happen after any such replacement so the
            controller captures the replacement target.
        */
        *HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpAsyncTarget asyncTarget;

        #! Keeps the Program of this server alive while a connection that its handler manages externally is open
        /** See @ref Priv::ExternalConnectionKeeper (qore#5752)
        */
        Priv::ExternalConnectionKeeper external_connections();

        #! Cached HTTP date header value
        static string cached_date_header;

        #! Epoch second when cache was last updated
        static int cached_date_epoch = 0;

        #! The running servers that set their loggers on the global async I/O controller, in the order they set them
        /** See setControllerLogger() and restoreControllerLogger()
        */
        static list<hash<Priv::ControllerLoggerInfo>> controller_loggers = ();

        #! Protects @ref controller_loggers and the global async I/O controller's logger as servers set and restore it
        static Mutex controller_logger_lck();

        #! The results of getContentEncodingPreferences() by \c Accept-Encoding header value
        /** A client sends the same header on every request, so the result is computed once per distinct value; see
            @ref MaxCachedEncodingPreferences
        */
        static hash<string, hash<string, float>> encoding_preferences_cache();

        #! Protects @ref encoding_preferences_cache
        static Mutex encoding_preferences_lck();

        #! The maximum number of header values in @ref encoding_preferences_cache and the longest value cached
        /** The header is chosen by the client, so the cache is emptied when it is full and long values are not cached
        */
        const MaxCachedEncodingPreferences = 64;
        const MaxCachedEncodingHeaderLength = 256;
    }

    private:internal {
        #! The information about the names of the fields of the configured response headers (\c hdr)
        /** A subclass can change \c hdr after the constructor and while requests are served; each response reads
            \c hdr and this once and compares the headers that this was made for with its own, so that the names are
            examined again only when the headers change (see getConfiguredHeaderInfo()).  It is replaced as a whole,
            so that the information read is that of the headers that it was made for
        */
        *hash<Priv::ConfiguredHeaderInfo> hdr_info;
    }
    #! @endcond

    #! @cond nodoc
    #! Returns the global async socket I/O controller (internal use only)
    AsyncSocketIo::AsyncSocketIoController getGlobalAsyncCtrl() {
        return AsyncSocketIo::getGlobalAsyncIoController();
    }

    #! Returns the HTTP async socket I/O controller (useful for testing cache cleanup)
    *HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpAsyncSocketIoController getHttpAsyncIo() {
        return asyncIo;
    }

    #! Returns the owner ID for this server (used to tag async I/O operations)
    string getOwnerId() {
        return self.uniqueHash();
    }

    #! Signals all active streaming handlers (SSE, WebSocket) to stop their polling loops
    /** Called by the async I/O controller during shutdown before waiting for the handler pool
        to complete.  This allows handler tasks to exit deterministically so that
        \c ThreadPool::stopWait() "stopWait()" does not block indefinitely.

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    signalStreamingHandlersToStop() {
        # Copy listeners under lock, then iterate outside to avoid holding lm
        # while calling handler.stop(), which could block or re-enter server state
        *hash<string, HttpListener> llist;
        {
            lm.enter();
            on_exit lm.exit();
            llist = listeners;
        }
        map $1.signalHandlersToStop(), llist.iterator();
    }

    #! Returns True if the server passes requests with the given HTTP method to a handler
    /** The server answers the HTTP/2 connection preface (method \c PRI) and methods that it does not support (see
        addHttpMethod()) with a 501 response without calling a handler.

        @par Example:
        @code{.py}
# a WebDAV gateway needs PROPFIND; check whether the server passes it to handlers before registering them
if (!server.acceptsHttpMethod("PROPFIND")) {
    server.addHttpMethod("PROPFIND");
}
        @endcode

        @param method the HTTP method of the request

        @return True if the server passes requests with the given HTTP method to a handler

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    bool acceptsHttpMethod(string method) {
        return method != "PRI" && http_methods{method};
    }

    # Returns True if a response to the HTTP/1.x request with the given headers can use chunked transfer encoding,
    # which requires HTTP/1.1 or later (RFC 9112 section 6.1)
    private:internal static bool requestAllowsChunked(hash<auto> hdr) {
        return hdr.http_version !~ /^(?:0\.[0-9]|1\.0)$/;
    }

    #! Returns the handler for inline check from global handler lists (no side effects)
    /** Looks up the handler from the permanent global handler list only.
        Dynamic handlers are not returned since they require lifecycle tracking
        that is incompatible with the inline path.

        @param hdr parsed HTTP headers
        @return the handler info if found, NOTHING otherwise
    */
    *HandlerInfo findGlobalHandler(hash<auto> hdr) {
        int score = 0;
        string root_path;
        *HandlerInfo hi = handlers.findHandler(hdr, \score, False, \root_path);
        if (!hi && defaultHandler) {
            hi = defaultHandler;
        }
        return hi;
    }
    #! @endcond

    #! creates the HttpServer
    /** call @ref addListener() to add and start listeners

        @param logfunc an optional closure or call reference that will be called with logging information; must take
        an initial string with optional arguments; ex:
        @code{.py} code log = sub (string fmt, ...) { vprintf(fmt, argv); }@endcode
        @param errlogfunc an optional closure or call reference that will be called with error information; must
        take an initial string with optional arguments; ex:
        @code{.py} code errloglog = sub (string fmt, ...) { vprintf(fmt, argv); }@endcode
        @param dbg if this parameter is set to @ref True "True", then additional information will be logged when
        errors occur; note that this parameter is only used if \a log is not provided
        @param name the name of the HTTP server as returned in the Server header (should be formatted according to
        <a href="http://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc2616#section-3.8">RFC 2616 section 3.8</a>)
        @param hdr a hash of headers to return in every response by default; to clear the default send an empty hash
        as the argument here

        @note A new Logger object is created for the HTTP server using either \a logfunc or \a errfunc; if both are
        given then only \a logfunc is used and all log messages (including errors) are directed to \a logfunc.  The
        Logger object's logging level is set according to which of these parameters is included.  If neither are
        included, then no logging is performed

        @deprecated use constructor(hash<HttpServerOptionInfo>) instead
    */
    deprecated constructor(*code<nothing(string)> logfunc, *code<nothing(string)> errlogfunc,
            bool dbg = False,
            string name = HttpServer::HttpServerString,
            hash<auto> hdr = {"X-Powered-By": "Qore/" + Qore::VersionString}) {
        logger = new Logger(name);
        if (logfunc || errlogfunc) {
            callback_appender = new HttpServerCallbackAppender(logfunc ?? errlogfunc);
            logger.addAppender(callback_appender);
            if (logfunc) {
                logger.setLevel(LoggerLevel::INFO);
            } else {
                logger.setLevel(LoggerLevel::ERROR);
            }
        } else {
            logger.setLevel(LoggerLevel::INFO);
        }
        init(<HttpServerOptionInfo>{
            "name": name,
            "debug": dbg,
            "hdr": hdr,
            "logger": logger,
        });
    }

    #! Creates the server with the given options
    /** @param opts see @ref HttpServerOptionInfo for more info
    */
    constructor(hash<HttpServerOptionInfo> opts) {
        init(opts);
    }

    #! Creates the server with default options and an empty logger
    constructor() {
        init(<HttpServerOptionInfo>{});
    }

    #! calls stop() and destroys the object
    /** If a stop is in progress (for example one started with stopNoWait()), waits for it to end first
    */
    destructor() {
        stopIntern(True, True);
    }

    #! Returns default response headers
    *hash<auto> getDefaultResponseHeaders() {
        return hdr;
    }

    #! Sets the compression level of each content encoding for response bodies compressed on the fly
    /** @param levels the level of each content encoding (see @ref HttpServerOptionInfo::compression_levels); an
        encoding that is not given has its default level (see @ref DefaultCompressionLevels), so an empty hash
        restores the defaults

        The levels apply to responses compressed after the call.

        @throw HTTP-SERVER-COMPRESSION-LEVEL-ERROR an unknown content encoding or a level out of the range of the
        encoding (see @ref CompressionLevelRanges); the levels are not changed in this case

        @par Example:
        @code{.py}
server.setCompressionLevels({"br": 4, "zstd": 3});
        @endcode

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    setCompressionLevels(hash<string, int> levels) {
        compression_levels = checkCompressionLevels(levels);
    }

    #! Returns the compression level of each content encoding for response bodies compressed on the fly
    /** @return the compression level of each supported content encoding

        @see setCompressionLevels()

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    hash<string, int> getCompressionLevels() {
        return compression_levels;
    }

    #! Returns the default compression levels with the given levels, after checking them
    /** @throw HTTP-SERVER-COMPRESSION-LEVEL-ERROR an unknown content encoding or a level out of range
    */
    private static hash<string, int> checkCompressionLevels(hash<string, int> levels) {
        foreach hash<auto> i in (levels.pairIterator()) {
            *list<int> range = CompressionLevelRanges{i.key};
            if (!range) {
                throw "HTTP-SERVER-COMPRESSION-LEVEL-ERROR", sprintf("cannot set a compression level for unknown "
                    "content encoding %y; supported content encodings: %y", i.key, keys CompressionLevelRanges);
            }
            if (i.value < range[0] || i.value > range[1]) {
                throw "HTTP-SERVER-COMPRESSION-LEVEL-ERROR", sprintf("compression level %d for content encoding %y "
                    "is out of range; it must be from %d to %d", i.value, i.key, range[0], range[1]);
            }
        }
        return DefaultCompressionLevels + levels;
    }

    #! Returns the TTL value
    date getTtl() {
        return ttl;
    }

    #! Sets the connection TTL value
    setTtl(date ttl) {
        if (ttl.absolute()) {
            throw "TTL-ARG-ERROR", sprintf("Idle disconnect (ttl) value is an absolute date: %y; it must be "
                "relative", ttl);
        }
        if (ttl <= 0us) {
            throw "TTL-ARG-ERROR", sprintf("Idle disconnect (ttl) value is not greater then zero: %y", ttl);
        }
        self.ttl = ttl;
    }

    #! Returns the maximum request body size in bytes (0 = unlimited)
    /** @return the maximum request body size in bytes; 0 means unlimited

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    int getMaxRequestBodySize() {
        return max_request_body_size;
    }

    #! Sets the maximum request body size in bytes (0 = unlimited)
    /** @param size the maximum request body size in bytes; 0 means unlimited

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    setMaxRequestBodySize(int size) {
        max_request_body_size = size;
    }

    #! Returns the maximum size in bytes of a decompressed request body
    /** @return the maximum size in bytes of a decompressed request body; \c 0 means that the default applies (see
        @ref HttpServer::HttpServerOptionInfo::max_decoded_body_size "HttpServerOptionInfo::max_decoded_body_size");
        a negative value means no limit

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    int getMaxDecodedBodySize() {
        return max_decoded_body_size;
    }

    #! Sets the maximum size in bytes of a decompressed request body
    /** @param size the maximum size in bytes of a decompressed request body; \c 0 means that the default applies
        (see
        @ref HttpServer::HttpServerOptionInfo::max_decoded_body_size "HttpServerOptionInfo::max_decoded_body_size"); a negative value means no limit

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    setMaxDecodedBodySize(int size) {
        max_decoded_body_size = size;
    }

    #! add a new supported HTTP method
    /** @param m the HTTP method to add; the string is converted to upper case if necessary
    */
    addHttpMethod(string m) {
        http_methods{m.upr()} = True;
    }

    #! returns the HTTP server version string
    static string getHttpServerVersionString() {
        return HttpServer::HttpServerVersion;
    }

    #! Sets the default text encoding: the encoding assumed for request text without an encoding declaration
    /** Request bodies are decoded by the rules shared by all HTTP clients and servers (see
        @ref Qore::decode_http_body() "decode_http_body()"); the default text encoding does not apply to a body whose
        encoding is declared in its \c Content-Type or in the body, or defined by its type, such as JSON.

        @param enc the default text encoding
    */
    setDefaultTextEncoding(string enc) {
        override_encoding = enc;
    }

    #! Returns the default text encoding: the encoding assumed for request text without an encoding declaration
    /** @return the encoding set with @ref setDefaultTextEncoding(), or \c "iso-8859-1" by default
    */
    string getDefaultTextEncoding() {
        return override_encoding ? override_encoding : "iso-8859-1";
    }

    #! Returns a request body received as binary data as a string
    /** Text is decoded by the rules shared by all HTTP clients and servers (see
        @ref Qore::decode_http_body() "decode_http_body()"), with the default text encoding (@ref getDefaultTextEncoding()) as the assumed
        encoding; a body of another type that is not delivered as binary data is returned in the default text
        encoding.

        @param hdr the request headers
        @param body the request body
    */
    private string decodeRequestText(hash<auto> hdr, binary body) {
        string assumed = getDefaultTextEncoding();
        auto ct = hdr."_qore_orig_content_type" ?? hdr."content-type";
        data rv = decode_http_body(ct.typeCode() == NT_STRING ? ct : NOTHING, body, assumed);
        return rv.typeCode() == NT_STRING ? rv : body.toString(assumed);
    }

    #! To call a listener's stop callback
    callListenerStopCallback(code<nothing(string)> stopc, string name, hash<auto> socket_info) {
        try {
            call_function(stopc, name);
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            logError("%y (%s): error calling stop notification closure: %s: %s: %s", name, socket_info.desc,
                get_ex_pos(ex), ex.err, ex.desc);
        }
    }

    #! adds one or more dedicated listeners to the server with the given bind address
    /** @param bind the bind address for the dedicated listener; this can be a port number or an address (or
        hostname) and a port number separated by a colon (ex: \c "192.168.20.4:8021"); the bind address will overwrite
        any \a node and \a service addresses in the \a info hash; all possible addresses will be bound to new
        listeners
        @param info a listener information hash
        @param errmap an optional reference to a hash of error information keyed by bind address

        @return a list of listener info hashes; see the return value of HttpServer::getListenerInfo() for the
        description of each hash element in the list returned by this method

        @throw HTTPSERVER-ADDLISTENER-ERROR missing bind info, duplicate bind address, or duplicate listener name
        @throw SOCKET-BIND-ERROR the socket could not be bound to the given address
        @throw HTTPLISTENER-BIND-ERROR the listener could not bind on the given address

        @note if \c bind begins with a "/" character then it is assumed to be a UNIX socket path and the \a family
        key of the \a info hash is ignored

        @note By default (\c sync: \c True), this method blocks until async I/O is ready to accept connections.
        Set \c sync: \c False in \a info to add multiple listeners without blocking, then call
        @ref waitForAsyncIo() manually.

        @since HttpServer 0.3.13.1
    */
    list<hash<auto>> addListeners(string bind, hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> info,
            *reference<hash<string, string>> errmap) {
        list<hash<auto>> rv = addListenersAsync(bind, info, \errmap).get();
        if (info.sync) {
            waitForAsyncIo();
        }
        return rv;
    }

    #! asynchronously adds one or more dedicated listeners to the server with the given bind address
    /** @param bind the bind address for the dedicated listener; this can be a port number or an address (or
        hostname) and a port number separated by a colon (ex: \c "192.168.20.4:8021"); the bind address will overwrite
        any \a node and \a service addresses in the \a info hash; all possible addresses will be bound to new
        listeners
        @param info a listener information hash
        @param errmap an optional reference to a hash of error information keyed by bind address

        @return a @ref Qore::Thread::Future "Future" resolving to a list of listener info hashes; see the return value
        of HttpServer::getListenerInfo() for the description of each hash element in the list returned by this method

        @throw HTTPSERVER-ADDLISTENER-ERROR missing bind info, duplicate bind address, or duplicate listener name
        @throw SOCKET-BIND-ERROR the socket could not be bound to the given address
        @throw HTTPLISTENER-BIND-ERROR the listener could not bind on the given address

        @note This method returns immediately after submitting setup work. Call
        @ref Qore::Thread::Future::get() "Future::get()" to wait for listener registration, and @ref waitForAsyncIo() to wait until async I/O is ready
        to accept connections.

        @par Example
        @code{.py}
        hash<string, string> errmap;
        Future setup = server.addListenersAsync("127.0.0.1:8080",
            <HttpListenerOptionInfo>{"sync": False}, \errmap);
        list<hash<auto>> listeners = setup.get();
        server.waitForAsyncIo();
        @endcode

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    Future addListenersAsync(string bind, hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> info,
            *reference<hash<string, string>> errmap) {
        return addListenersInternAsync(bind, info, \errmap);
    }

    #! adds one or more dedicated listeners to the server with the given bind address
    /** @param info a listener information hash
        @param errmap an optional reference to a hash of error information keyed by bind address

        @return a list of listener info hashes; see the return value of HttpServer::getListenerInfo() for the
        description of each hash element in the list returned by this method

        @throw HTTPSERVER-ADDLISTENER-ERROR missing bind info, duplicate bind address, or duplicate listener name
        @throw SOCKET-BIND-ERROR the socket could not be bound to the given address
        @throw HTTPLISTENER-BIND-ERROR the listener could not bind on the given address

        @note if \c bind begins with a "/" character then it is assumed to be a UNIX socket path and the \a family
        key of the \a info hash is ignored

        @note By default (\c sync: \c True), this method blocks until async I/O is ready to accept connections.
        Set \c sync: \c False in \a info to add multiple listeners without blocking, then call
        @ref waitForAsyncIo() manually.

        @since HttpServer 0.3.13.1
    */
    list<hash<auto>> addListeners(hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> info, *reference<hash<string, string>> errmap) {
        list<hash<auto>> rv = addListenersAsync(info, \errmap).get();
        if (info.sync) {
            waitForAsyncIo();
        }
        return rv;
    }

    #! asynchronously adds one or more dedicated listeners to the server with the given bind address
    /** @param info a listener information hash
        @param errmap an optional reference to a hash of error information keyed by bind address

        @return a @ref Qore::Thread::Future "Future" resolving to a list of listener info hashes; see the return value
        of HttpServer::getListenerInfo() for the description of each hash element in the list returned by this method

        @throw HTTPSERVER-ADDLISTENER-ERROR missing bind info, duplicate bind address, or duplicate listener name
        @throw SOCKET-BIND-ERROR the socket could not be bound to the given address
        @throw HTTPLISTENER-BIND-ERROR the listener could not bind on the given address

        @note This method returns immediately after submitting setup work. Call
        @ref Qore::Thread::Future::get() "Future::get()" to wait for listener registration, and @ref waitForAsyncIo() to wait until async I/O is ready
        to accept connections.

        @par Example
        @code{.py}
        hash<string, string> errmap;
        Future setup = server.addListenersAsync(<HttpListenerOptionInfo>{
            "service": 8080,
            "sync": False,
        }, \errmap);
        list<hash<auto>> listeners = setup.get();
        server.waitForAsyncIo();
        @endcode

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    Future addListenersAsync(hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> info, *reference<hash<string, string>> errmap) {
        return addListenersInternAsync(info, \errmap);
    }

    #! adds a single global listener to the server
    /** @param port the port number

        @return a listener info hash; see the return value of HttpServer::HttpServer::getListenerInfo() for the
        description of the hash returned by this method

        @throw HTTPSERVER-ADDLISTENER-ERROR missing bind info, duplicate bind address, or duplicate listener name
        @throw SOCKET-BIND-ERROR the socket could not be bound to the given address
        @throw HTTPLISTENER-BIND-ERROR the listener could not bind on the given address

        @note This method blocks until async I/O is ready to accept connections.
        Use @ref HttpServer::HttpServer::addListener "addListener(hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo>)" with \c sync: \c False to add listeners without blocking.

        @note The listener listens on all interfaces with one dual-stack socket that serves both IPv4 and IPv6
        clients; see @ref Qore::Socket::bindINET() "Socket::bindINET()"

        @note On macOS, a listener on all interfaces is refused a port that another program already uses for IPv4 on
        \c 0.0.0.0 or \c 127.0.0.1 (\c SOCKET-BIND-ERROR), as Linux refuses it, and a port assigned by the system
        (port \c 0) is one that no IPv4 socket uses.  A program that later binds a specific address of the port, such
        as \c 127.0.0.1, is refused it, also with \c SO_REUSEADDR, on macOS as on Linux.  See
        @ref Qore::Socket::bindINET() "Socket::bindINET()"
     */
    hash<auto> addListener(int port) {
        hash<auto> rv = addListenerAsync(port).get();
        # sync defaults to True, so wait for async I/O to be ready
        waitForAsyncIo();
        return rv;
    }

    #! asynchronously adds a single global listener to the server
    /** @param port the port number

        @return a @ref Qore::Thread::Future "Future" resolving to a listener info hash; see the return value of
        HttpServer::HttpServer::getListenerInfo() for the description of the hash returned by this method

        @throw HTTPSERVER-ADDLISTENER-ERROR missing bind info, duplicate bind address, or duplicate listener name
        @throw SOCKET-BIND-ERROR the socket could not be bound to the given address
        @throw HTTPLISTENER-BIND-ERROR the listener could not bind on the given address

        @note This method returns immediately after submitting setup work. Call
        @ref Qore::Thread::Future::get() "Future::get()" to wait for listener registration, and @ref waitForAsyncIo() to wait until async I/O is ready
        to accept connections.

        @par Example
        @code{.py}
        Future setup = server.addListenerAsync(8080);
        hash<auto> listener = setup.get();
        server.waitForAsyncIo();
        @endcode

        @since HttpServer 1.6
     */
    Future addListenerAsync(int port) {
        return addListenerInternAsync(<HttpListenerOptionInfo>{
            "service": port,
        });
    }

    #! adds a single global listener to the server
    /** @param opts the listener options

        @return a listener info hash; see the return value of HttpServer::HttpServer::getListenerInfo() for the
        description of the hash returned by this method

        @throw HTTPSERVER-ADDLISTENER-ERROR missing bind info, duplicate bind address, or duplicate listener name
        @throw SOCKET-BIND-ERROR the socket could not be bound to the given address
        @throw HTTPLISTENER-BIND-ERROR the listener could not bind on the given address

        @note if \a sock begins with a "/" character then it is assumed to be a UNIX socket path and the \a family
        key is ignored

        @note By default (\c sync: \c True), this method blocks until async I/O is ready to accept connections.
        Set \c sync: \c False in \a opts to add multiple listeners without blocking, then call
        @ref waitForAsyncIo() manually.

        @note A listener with a \c service and no \c node or \c family listens on all interfaces with one
        dual-stack socket that serves both IPv4 and IPv6 clients; see
        @ref Qore::Socket::bindINET() "Socket::bindINET()"

        @note On macOS, a listener on all interfaces is refused a port that another program already uses for IPv4 on
        \c 0.0.0.0 or \c 127.0.0.1 (\c SOCKET-BIND-ERROR), as Linux refuses it, and a port assigned by the system
        (port \c 0) is one that no IPv4 socket uses.  A program that later binds a specific address of the port, such
        as \c 127.0.0.1, is refused it, also with \c SO_REUSEADDR, on macOS as on Linux.  See
        @ref Qore::Socket::bindINET() "Socket::bindINET()"
     */
    hash<auto> addListener(hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> opts) {
        hash<auto> rv = addListenerAsync(opts).get();
        if (opts.sync) {
            waitForAsyncIo();
        }
        return rv;
    }

    #! asynchronously adds a single global listener to the server
    /** @param opts the listener options

        @return a @ref Qore::Thread::Future "Future" resolving to a listener info hash; see the return value of
        HttpServer::HttpServer::getListenerInfo() for the description of the hash returned by this method

        @throw HTTPSERVER-ADDLISTENER-ERROR missing bind info, duplicate bind address, or duplicate listener name
        @throw SOCKET-BIND-ERROR the socket could not be bound to the given address
        @throw HTTPLISTENER-BIND-ERROR the listener could not bind on the given address

        @note This method returns immediately after submitting setup work. Call
        @ref Qore::Thread::Future::get() "Future::get()" to wait for listener registration, and @ref waitForAsyncIo() to wait until async I/O is ready
        to accept connections.

        @par Example
        @code{.py}
        Future setup = server.addListenerAsync(<HttpListenerOptionInfo>{
            "service": 0,
            "sync": False,
        });
        hash<auto> listener = setup.get();
        server.waitForAsyncIo();
        @endcode

        @since HttpServer 1.6
     */
    Future addListenerAsync(hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> opts) {
        return addListenerInternAsync(opts);
    }

    #! Adds an HTTP/3 QUIC listener on the given UDP port
    /** The QUIC listener inherits \c additional_info from the options (if provided), ensuring
        that custom listener configuration (cookie names, OIDC flags, public URLs, etc.) is
        propagated from the parent TCP listener.

        @param opts listener options; \c cert and \c key are required for QUIC TLS 1.3;
        \c additional_info is passed through to the underlying HttpListener
        @param port the UDP port to bind for QUIC (0 for random port)

        @return a hash with \c port and \c name keys

        @throw HTTP3-ERROR if certificate/key missing

        @since Qore 3.0
    */
    hash<auto> addQuicListener(hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> opts, int port = 0) {
        if (!opts.cert || !opts.key) {
            throw "HTTP3-ERROR", "QUIC requires SSL certificate and private key for TLS 1.3";
        }
        checkSseMaxBufferSize(opts);

        # Create a UDP socket and bind it
        Socket udp_sock();
        on_error try { udp_sock.close(); } catch () {}
        string bind_addr = opts.node ?? "0.0.0.0";
        string bind_port = port > 0 ? string(port) : (opts.service ?? "0");
        # Resolve the address family: use the explicitly requested family, or detect from
        # the bind address ("::" is IPv6, anything else defaults to IPv4)
        int family;
        if (opts.family != AF_UNSPEC) {
            family = opts.family;
        } else if (bind_addr == "::" || bind_addr =~ /^[0-9a-fA-F]*:/) {
            family = AF_INET6;
        } else {
            family = AF_INET;
        }
        # SO_REUSEADDR must be False for UDP/QUIC: on Linux it lets IPv6 [::] and
        # IPv4 0.0.0.0 silently share a UDP port, after which the kernel delivers
        # IPv4 datagrams only to the IPv4 socket — the H3 listener bound on [::]
        # would then never receive any traffic from IPv4 clients.  With reuseaddr
        # disabled the bind fails on conflict (on macOS, Socket::bindINET() checks
        # the port for IPv4 on 0.0.0.0 and 127.0.0.1 itself), and the random-port
        # retry in addListenerIntern() picks a fresh port that actually works.
        udp_sock.bindINET(bind_addr, bind_port, False, family, SOCK_DGRAM);

        # Get the actual port that was bound
        hash<auto> sock_info = udp_sock.getSocketInfo(False);
        int actual_port = sock_info.port;

        # Set up SSL certificate and key for QUIC
        udp_sock.setCertificate(opts.cert);
        udp_sock.setPrivateKey(opts.key);

        string name = opts.name ?? sprintf("quic-%d", actual_port);

        # Create an HttpListener for handler dispatch (no TCP bind);
        # pass UDP socket info via ext_info so getInfo() includes address/family details
        int lid = seqListeners.next();
        string lname = name + sprintf("-%d", lid);
        hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> listener_opts = <HttpListenerOptionInfo>{
            "name": lname,
            "cert": opts.cert,
            "key": opts.key,
            "ssl_verify_flags": opts.ssl_verify_flags,
            "get_remote_certs": opts.get_remote_certs,
            "max_request_body_size": opts.max_request_body_size,
            "max_decoded_body_size": opts.max_decoded_body_size,
            "sse_max_buffer_size": opts.sse_max_buffer_size,
            "no_bind": True,
            "ext_info": sock_info{"address", "address_desc", "family", "familystr", "port",
                "hostname", "hostname_desc"},
        };
        if (opts.additional_info) {
            listener_opts.additional_info = opts.additional_info;
        }
        HttpListener http_listener = getListener(self, lid, listener_opts);
        # Increment listener counter; decremented by HttpListener::listenerStopped()
        # when the listener is stopped/deleted (on_error handles setup failures)
        c.add();
        on_error c.done();

        # the fast routes of the server, if any, are answered for the HTTP/3 requests of the listener's sessions; the
        # socket is kept so that routes created later are attached to it as well
        {
            fast_routes_lock.lock();
            on_exit fast_routes_lock.unlock();
            quic_udp_sockets{string(lid)} = udp_sock;
            if (fast_routes) {
                udp_sock.setHttpFastRoutes(fast_routes);
            }
        }
        on_error {
            fast_routes_lock.lock();
            on_exit fast_routes_lock.unlock();
            remove quic_udp_sockets{string(lid)};
        }

        # Register with the async I/O controller
        if (!asyncIo) {
            throw "HTTP3-ERROR", "async I/O controller not initialized";
        }
        asyncIo.addQuicListener(udp_sock, asyncTarget, opts.cert, opts.key, http_listener,
            opts.ssl_verify_flags, opts.ssl_accept_all_certs, opts.get_remote_certs,
            http_listener.getMaxRequestBodySize());

        listeners{lid} = http_listener;
        nmap{lname} = lid;
        # NOTE: QUIC listeners are not added to smap (bind-address map) because
        # they use UDP sockets with a different address format than TCP listeners.
        # Use getListeners() or nmap (by name) to find QUIC listeners.

        log("QUIC/HTTP/3 listener started on UDP port %d (name: %s)",
            actual_port, name);

        return {
            "port": actual_port,
            "name": name,
            "protocol": "h3",
            "id": lid,
            "alt_svc_max_age": opts.alt_svc_max_age ?? alt_svc_max_age,
        };
    }

    #! throws an exception; these objects do not support copying
    copy() {
        throw "COPY-ERROR", "this object cannot be copied";
    }

    #! returns a hash of listener information
    /** @return a hash of listener info hashes keyed by listener ID; see the return value of HttpServer::getListenerInfo() for the description of each hash value
     */
    hash<string, hash<auto>> getListeners() {
        return (map {$1.key: $1.value.getInfo()}, listeners.pairIterator()) ?? {};
    }

    #! returns a hash of information about the listener given the listener ID
    /** @param id the listener ID

        @return a hash of listener information with the following keys:
        - \c name: the listener name
        - \c hostname: the listening interface name if available (ex: "localhost"; note that this key is not present when retrieving information about UNIX sockets)
        - \c hostname_desc: a descriptive string giving the hostname and the address family if the hostname is available (ex: "ipv6[localhost]"; note that this key is not present when retrieving information about UNIX sockets)
        - \c address: a string giving the address (ex: "::ffff:0.0.0.0")
        - \c address_desc: a descriptive string giving the address and the address family (ex: "ipv6[::ffff:0.0.0.0]")
        - \c port: an integer port number if available (note that this key is not present when retrieving information about UNIX sockets)
        - \c family: the network address family (see @ref network_address_family_constants "Network Address Family Constants")
        - \c familystr: a string describing the network address family (ex: "ipv4")
        - \c proto: the protocol used; either \c "http" or \c "https" for secure listeners
        - \c id: the listener ID
        - \c bind: the bind specification used

        @throw INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR invalid listener ID
     */
    hash<auto> getListenerInfo(softint id) {
        lm.enter();
        on_exit lm.exit();

        if (!listeners.hasKey(id)) {
            throw "INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR", sprintf("there is no listener with ID %d", id);
        }

        return listeners{id}.getInfo();
    }

    #! returns a hash of information about the listener given the listener name or bind ID
    /** @param name the listener name or bind ID

        @return a hash of listener information with the following keys:
        - \c name: the listener name
        - \c hostname: the listening interface name if available (ex: "localhost"; note that this key is not present
          when retrieving information about UNIX sockets)
        - \c hostname_desc: a descriptive string giving the hostname and the address family if the hostname is
          available (ex: "ipv6[localhost]"; note that this key is not present when retrieving information about UNIX
          sockets)
        - \c address: a string giving the address (ex: "::ffff:0.0.0.0")
        - \c address_desc: a descriptive string giving the address and the address family (ex: "ipv6[::ffff:0.0.0.0]")
        - \c port: an integer port number if available (note that this key is not present when retrieving information
          about UNIX sockets)
        - \c family: the network address family (see
          @ref network_address_family_constants "Network Address Family Constants")
        - \c familystr: a string describing the network address family (ex: "ipv4")
        - \c proto: the protocol used; either \c "http" or \c "https" for secure listeners
        - \c id: the listener ID
        - \c bind: the bind specification used

        @throw INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR invalid listener name or bind ID
     */
    hash<auto> getListenerInfoName(string name) {
        lm.enter();
        on_exit lm.exit();

        softstring id = getListenerIdFromBindUnlocked(name);
        return listeners{id}.getInfo();
    }

    #! Reloads an HTTPS certificate from the original location for the given listener from the listener ID
    /** @param id the listener ID

        Subsequent connections will use the new certificate definition; the listener must have been started with the
        location information for the X.509 certificate and private key, or a REFRESH-CERTIFICATE-ERROR exception is
        raised

        @throw REFRESH-CERTIFICATE-ERROR not an HTTPS listener or certificate location information not present

        @since HttpServer 1.1
    */
    reloadListenerCertificate(int id) {
        lm.enter();
        on_exit lm.exit();

        if (!listeners.hasKey(id)) {
            throw "INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR", sprintf("there is no listener with ID %d", id);
        }

        # Reload the TCP listener: re-reads cert/key from disk, updates the
        # listener Socket and the async controller's listeners-map entry.
        listeners{id}.reloadCertificate();

        # Paired listeners must share certificate state — a cert rotated only on
        # the TCP side leaves the QUIC udp_sock holding the original cert/key,
        # which then stalls HTTP/3 handshakes for clients that follow the
        # advertised Alt-Svc and triggers HTTPCLIENT-NEGOTIATE-TIMEOUT on the
        # client side (and vice versa for QUIC-only reloads).  Push the cert
        # objects we just loaded onto the paired listener too.
        if (asyncIo && quic_listener_map.hasKey(id)) {
            int quic_id = quic_listener_map{id};
            *HttpListener quic_listener = listeners{quic_id};
            if (quic_listener) {
                *Qore::SSLCertificate cert = listeners{id}.getSslCertificateObject();
                *Qore::SSLPrivateKey key = listeners{id}.getSslPrivateKey();
                if (cert && key) {
                    asyncIo.reloadListenerCertificateByHttpListener(quic_listener, cert, key);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    #! Reloads an HTTPS certificate from the original location for the given listener from the listener name
    /** @param name the listener name or bind address

        Subsequent connections will use the new certificate definition; the listener must have been started with the
        location information for the X.509 certificate and private key, or a REFRESH-CERTIFICATE-ERROR exception is
        raised

        @throw REFRESH-CERTIFICATE-ERROR not an HTTPS listener or certificate location information not present

        @since HttpServer 1.1
    */
    reloadListenerCertificateName(string name) {
        lm.enter();
        on_exit lm.exit();

        softstring id = getListenerIdFromBindUnlocked(name);
        listeners{id}.reloadCertificate();

        # Paired listeners share the cert; see comment in reloadListenerCertificate(int).
        if (asyncIo && quic_listener_map.hasKey(id)) {
            int quic_id = quic_listener_map{id};
            *HttpListener quic_listener = listeners{quic_id};
            if (quic_listener) {
                *Qore::SSLCertificate cert = listeners{id}.getSslCertificateObject();
                *Qore::SSLPrivateKey key = listeners{id}.getSslPrivateKey();
                if (cert && key) {
                    asyncIo.reloadListenerCertificateByHttpListener(quic_listener, cert, key);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    #! returns the number of running HTTP listeners
    int getListenerCount() {
        return listeners.size();
    }

    #! Starts stopping the server in the background, as stop() does, and returns at once
    /** The server stops accepting connections and drains the connections that it has, then closes whatever is
        left, exactly as stop() does, in a thread of its own; use waitStop() to wait for the stop to end, with a
        timeout to bound the wait.

        @note before HttpServer 1.7, this method stopped the listeners without closing the connections that the server
        had: they were served until the server was destroyed

        @see
        - stop()
        - stopImmediate()
        - waitStop()
    */
    stopNoWait() {
        # the stop is claimed here, so that a stop() or the destructor called while it runs does not start another
        if (beginStop(False)) {
            try {
                background runStop(True);
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                # no thread could be started: the stop is not claimed any more
                AutoLock al(stop_m);
                stopping = False;
                stop_cond.broadcast();
                rethrow;
            }
        }
    }

    #! waits for all listeners to be stopped; call after calling HttpServer::stopNoWait()
    /** A listener is only counted as stopped once every connection thread on it has returned, so
        this method waits for all in-flight requests to complete.  A request handler that blocks
        indefinitely therefore blocks this call indefinitely as well; pass a timeout to bound the
        wait and regain control in that case.

        @param timeout_ms the maximum time to wait; 0 (the default) waits indefinitely

        @return 0 if all listeners stopped, -1 if the timeout expired while listeners were still
        running; use getRunningListenerCount() to report what is still in flight

        @note the return value is new in HttpServer 1.7; the no-argument call is unchanged

        @see
        - getRunningListenerCount()
        - stopNoWait()
    */
    int waitStop(timeout timeout_ms = 0) {
        return c.wait(timeout_ms);
    }

    #! returns the number of listeners that have not stopped yet
    /** A listener stops only after all of its connection threads have returned, so a non-zero
        value after waitStop() times out means requests are still in flight.

        @return the number of listeners still running

        @since HttpServer 1.7

        @see waitStop()
    */
    int getRunningListenerCount() {
        return c.getCount();
    }

    #! called when a listener has been registered with the async I/O controller and is accepting connections
    /** Issued synchronously from addListenerIntern() right after the listener is added to the async I/O
        controller; with the event-driven async I/O model there are no per-listener accept threads.

        @param id the listener ID
        @param sinfo a socket information hash as returned by @ref Qore::Socket::getSocketInfo()

        @since HttpServer 0.3.11
     */
    listenerStarted(int id, hash<auto> sinfo) {
        log("HTTP listener %d started on %s", id, sinfo.desc);
    }

    #! Removes a listener from the async I/O controller before the socket is closed
    /** Called from HttpListener::stop() to cancel pending accept operations on
        the listener's fd BEFORE the fd is closed.  This ensures the I/O thread
        sees clean cancellation (canceled=True callbacks) rather than error
        callbacks on a closed fd that trigger failed replenishment attempts.

        Safe to call multiple times — listenerStopped() handles the redundant
        removeListener() gracefully.
    */
    removeAsyncListener(HttpListener l) {
        if (asyncIo) {
            try {
                asyncIo.removeListener(l);
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                if (ex.err != "CONTROLLER-REMOVE-LISTENER-ERROR"
                        && ex.err != "ASYNCSOCKETIO-CANCEL-ERROR") {
                    rethrow;
                }
            }
        }
    }

    # only called from the listeners - do not call externally
    listenerStopped(HttpListener l) {
        # decrement listener count (guard against double-call from async cleanup + stop)
        on_exit {
            try {
                c.done();
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                if (ex.err != "WAITGROUP-ERROR") {
                    rethrow;
                }
            }
        }

        lm.enter();
        on_exit {
            lm.exit();
        }

        # Remove listener from async I/O controller if running
        if (asyncIo) {
            try {
                asyncIo.removeListener(l);
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                # Ignore error if listener was not registered (can happen during shutdown)
                if (ex.err != "CONTROLLER-REMOVE-LISTENER-ERROR"
                        && ex.err != "ASYNCSOCKETIO-CANCEL-ERROR") {
                    rethrow;
                }
            }
        }

        string lid = string(l.getID());
        {
            fast_routes_lock.lock();
            on_exit fast_routes_lock.unlock();
            remove alt_svc_targets{lid};
        }

        # If this TCP listener had an auto-created QUIC listener, stop it too
        if (quic_listener_map.hasKey(lid)) {
            int quic_lid = remove quic_listener_map{lid};
            remove quic_port_map{lid};
            remove quic_alt_svc_max_age{lid};
            {
                fast_routes_lock.lock();
                on_exit fast_routes_lock.unlock();
                remove quic_udp_sockets{string(quic_lid)};
            }
            updateAltSvcHeader();
            # Stop the QUIC listener if it still exists.
            # NOTE: stop() triggers the QUIC listener's own listenerStopped()
            # flow which removes it from listeners{}, nmap{}, and smap{}.
            if (listeners.hasKey(string(quic_lid))) {
                try {
                    listeners{quic_lid}.stop();
                } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                    # Best-effort cleanup
                }
            }
        }

        delete smap.(l.getAddress());
        remove listeners.(l.getID());
        remove nmap.(l.getName());
        #printf("stopped %s c=%y\n", l.getAddress(), c.getCount());
    }

    #! Stops the HTTP server and releases all resources, letting the requests in progress finish first
    /** Performs a synchronous, ordered shutdown:
        -# Starts draining the connections: closes the listening sockets, and tells the clients that their connections
           are closing: HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 connections are sent a \c GOAWAY frame with the last request that the server
           processed, an idle HTTP/1.1 connection is closed, and a request in progress on an HTTP/1.1 connection is
           answered with \c Connection: \c close; each connection is closed when it has answered its requests.  A
           protocol upgrade or an event stream that a handler starts now is refused with a 503 response, and a
           CONNECT tunnel is ended
        -# Closes the dedicated connections with their protocol's closing handshake, all at the same time: WebSocket
           connections are sent a close frame (code 1001, going away), and the server waits for the clients' close
           frames at most the close timeout of their handlers in total (see
           @ref WebSocketHandler::WebSocketHandler::setCloseTimeout() "WebSocketHandler::setCloseTimeout()")
        -# Signals streaming handlers (SSE, WebSocket) to stop
        -# Waits for the drain to end, at most the \c drain_timeout option from the start of the stop (5 seconds by
           default; see @ref HttpServer::HttpServerOptionInfo "HttpServerOptionInfo"), woken by the last connection
           that closes
        -# Stops the async I/O controller (cancels all accept, connection, and streaming
           operations that are left and waits for in-flight handler tasks to complete)
        -# Cancels remaining ops by owner on the global async I/O controller
        -# Stops listeners and waits for all listener-stopped callbacks
        -# Flushes any remaining async callbacks and stops the internal thread pool

        This method blocks until all connections are closed and handler tasks complete.

        @note
        - HttpServer instances are not restartable after \c stop(). Create a new HttpServer if a restart is needed.
        - if a stop is in progress (for example one started with stopNoWait()), this method returns at once; use
          waitStop() to wait for it
        - before HttpServer 1.7, this method did not wait for the requests in progress: it closed the connections at
          once, as stopImmediate() does

        @see
        - stopImmediate()
        - stopNoWait()
    */
    stop() {
        stopIntern(True, False);
    }

    #! Stops the HTTP server and releases all resources without waiting for the requests in progress
    /** Performs the same shutdown as stop() without draining the connections: HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 clients are sent a
        \c GOAWAY frame, and all connections are closed at once.

        This method blocks until all connections are closed and handler tasks complete.

        @since HttpServer 1.7

        @see
        - stop()
    */
    stopImmediate() {
        stopIntern(False, False);
    }

    #! Stops the server, draining the connections first if \a drain is @ref True
    /** @param drain if @ref True, the connections are drained (see stop()) before the controller is stopped
        @param wait_in_progress if @ref True and a stop is in progress, waits for it to end (used by the destructor,
        which must not destroy the object under a stop that runs in another thread)
    */
    private stopIntern(bool drain, bool wait_in_progress) {
        if (beginStop(wait_in_progress)) {
            runStop(drain);
        }
    }

    #! Claims the stop of the server; returns @ref True if the caller is to run it with runStop()
    /** A stop that is in progress or done is not run again: the call returns @ref False at once, or, with
        \a wait_in_progress, when the stop in progress has ended
    */
    private bool beginStop(bool wait_in_progress) {
        AutoLock al(stop_m);
        if (stopping && wait_in_progress) {
            while (stopping) {
                stop_cond.wait(stop_m);
            }
        }
        if (stopped || stopping) {
            return False;
        }
        stopping = True;
        return True;
    }

    #! Runs a stop claimed with beginStop()
    private runStop(bool drain) {
        # the stop is ended (and waiting destructors woken) whatever happens below
        on_exit {
            AutoLock al(stop_m);
            stopping = False;
            stop_cond.broadcast();
        }
        # the global controller logs through this server's logger until the server has stopped
        on_exit restoreControllerLogger();
        if (callback_appender) {
            try {
                logger.removeAppender(callback_appender);
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            }
            callback_appender = NOTHING;
        }
        # A drain starts first: the server accepts no new connection and takes no new long-lived stream, so that
        # nothing new starts while the long-lived connections are closed below; a drain timeout of zero or less
        # means no drain
        bool do_drain = drain && asyncIo && drain_timeout > 0;
        int drain_start_us = clock_getmicros();
        if (do_drain) {
            asyncIo.startDrain();
        }

        # Close the dedicated connections (for example WebSocket connections) with their protocol's closing handshake
        # while the I/O that the handshakes need still runs; all of them close at the same time, so this
        # waits at most one handshake timeout in total
        closeDedicatedConnections();

        # Wait for the requests in progress to be answered, at most what is left of the drain timeout; the other
        # long-lived streams (SSE, persistent handler loops) are ended first, as they have no end that a drain could
        # wait for
        if (do_drain) {
            signalStreamingHandlersToStop();
            asyncIo.signalPersistentQueuesToClose();
            int remaining_ms = drain_timeout - (clock_getmicros() - drain_start_us) / 1000;
            if (remaining_ms <= 0 || !asyncIo.waitDrain(remaining_ms)) {
                log("stop: the requests in progress did not finish in the drain timeout (%y); closing the connections "
                    "that are left", drain_timeout);
            }
        }

        # Cancel operations owned by this server on the global controller FIRST
        # (e.g. HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 poll operations from execAsyncPollOperation).
        # This must happen before asyncIo.stop() because that method calls
        # handler_pool.stopWait() which blocks until all handler tasks complete.
        # Handler tasks waiting in execAsyncPollOperation() (done.waitForZero())
        # will only unblock once their operations are cancelled and the onComplete
        # callback decrements the counter.
        AsyncSocketIo::AsyncSocketIoController ctrl = AsyncSocketIo::getGlobalAsyncIoController();
        string ownerId = self.uniqueHash();
        @debug(log("stop: calling cancelByOwner(%s)", ownerId));
        int canceled = ctrl.cancelByOwner(ownerId);
        @debug(log("stop: cancelByOwner() canceled %d operations", canceled));

        # Flush callbacks from the cancelByOwner above so that handler tasks
        # blocked in execAsyncPollOperation() are unblocked before we stop
        # the handler pool
        ctrl.flushCallbacks();

        # Stop the callback-based async I/O controller
        # The new HttpAsyncSocketIoController.stop() cancels all HTTP operations it manages
        # (accept, header reads, idle connections) on the global async I/O controller
        if (asyncIo) {
            # Signal streaming handlers before stopping the controller so handler tasks
            # exit promptly (done here instead of in HttpServerNativeAsyncTarget.stopStreaming()
            # to avoid a reference cycle between the target and HttpServer)
            signalStreamingHandlersToStop();
            asyncIo.stop();
        }

        # Shutdown all listeners - stop accepting new connections
        {
            lm.enter();
            on_exit {
                lm.exit();
            }

            map $1.stop(), listeners.iterator();
        }

        # Wait for all listener stopped callbacks outside of lm scope to avoid deadlock
        # with background listener stop threads from stopNoWait() that need lm to call
        # listenerStopped() -> c.done()
        c.wait();

        # Flush any remaining async callbacks (from listener stops)
        ctrl.flushCallbacks();

        threadPool.stop();

        {
            AutoLock al(stop_m);
            stopped = True;
            stopping = False;
        }
    }

    #! Closes the dedicated connections of all listeners with their protocol's closing handshake, all at the same time
    /** Each dedicated socket handler starts the closing handshake on its connections
        (@ref HttpServer::AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface::startClosingConnections() "AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface::startClosingConnections()"), and then the server waits until the latest
        of the deadlines that the handlers return; the connections still open then are stopped at once with the
        listeners.
    */
    private closeDedicatedConnections() {
        hash<string, AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface> handlers = {};
        {
            lm.enter();
            on_exit lm.exit();
            foreach HttpListener l in (listeners.iterator()) {
                handlers += l.getDedicatedSocketHandlers();
            }
        }
        HttpListener::closeHandlerConnections(handlers, NOTHING, sub (string fmt, ...) {
            logError("%s", vsprintf(fmt, argv));
        });
    }

    #! stops a single listener based on its name or bind address; does not return until all connections on the listener have closed
    /** @param bind listener bind address

        @throw INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR invalid listener bind address
        @throw HTTP-SERVER-ERROR cannot stop last listener
    */
    stopListener(softstring bind) {
        lm.enter();
        on_exit lm.exit();

        *int id = smap{bind};
        if (!id) {
            id = nmap{bind};
        }

        if (!id) {
            throw "INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR", sprintf("there is no listener with name or bind address %y", bind);
        }

        if (protect_last_listener && (elements smap == 1)) {
            throw "HTTP-SERVER-ERROR", "cannot stop last listener";
        }

        listeners{id}.stop();
    }

    #! stops a single listener based on its listener ID; does not return until all connections on the listener have closed
    /** @param id listener id

        @throw INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR invalid listener ID
        @throw HTTP-SERVER-ERROR cannot stop last listener
    */
    stopListenerID(softint id) {
        lm.enter();
        on_exit lm.exit();

        if (!listeners.hasKey(id)) {
            throw "INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR", sprintf("there is no listener with ID %d", id);
        }

        if (protect_last_listener && (smap.size() == 1)) {
            throw "HTTP-SERVER-ERROR", "cannot stop last listener";
        }

        listeners{id}.stop();
    }

    #! starts the shutdown process for a listener based on its listener ID; returns immediately
    /** @param id listener id

        @throw INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR invalid listener ID
        @throw HTTP-SERVER-ERROR cannot stop last listener
    */
    stopListenerIDNoWait(softint id) {
        lm.enter();
        on_exit lm.exit();

        if (!listeners.hasKey(id)) {
            throw "INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR", sprintf("there is no listener with ID %d", id);
        }

        if (protect_last_listener && (smap.size() == 1)) {
            throw "HTTP-SERVER-ERROR", "cannot stop last listener";
        }

        background listeners{id}.stop();
    }

    #! sets the default request handler when no other handler can be matched
    setDefaultHandler(string name, HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler obj) {
        defaultHandler = new HandlerInfo(name, <HttpHandlerConfigInfo>{
            "handler": obj,
        });
    }

    #! sets a request handler according to the arguments given
    setHandler(string name, hash<HttpHandlerConfigInfo> info) {
        handlers.setHandler(name, info);
    }

    #! sets a request handler according to the arguments given
    setHandler(string name, string path, *softlist<softstring> content_types, HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler obj, *softlist<softstring> special_headers, bool isregex = True) {
        handlers.setHandler(name, <HttpHandlerConfigInfo>{
            "path": path,
            "isregex": isregex,
            "content_types": content_types,
            "handler": obj,
            "headers": special_headers,
        });
    }

    #! sets a dynamic request handler according to the arguments given
    setDynamicHandler(string name, hash<HttpHandlerConfigInfo> info) {
        dhandlers.setHandler(name, info);
    }

    #! sets a dynamic request handler according to the arguments given
    setDynamicHandler(string name, string path, *softlist<softstring> content_types,
            HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler obj, *softlist<softstring> special_headers, bool isregex = True) {
        dhandlers.setHandler(name, <HttpHandlerConfigInfo>{
            "path": path,
            "isregex": isregex,
            "content_types": content_types,
            "handler": obj,
            "headers": special_headers,
        });
    }

    #! sets a request handler according to the arguments given
    setHandler(string name, HttpServer::AbstractUrlHandler obj) {
        handlers.setHandler(name, <HttpHandlerConfigInfo>{
            "path": obj.url_root,
            "isregex": False,
            "handler": obj,
        });
    }

    #! removes a request handler registered via @ref setHandler() by name
    /** @param name the name the handler was registered under

        @throw INVALID-HANDLER-ERROR no handler with the given name exists

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    removeHandler(string name) {
        handlers.removeHandler(name);
    }

    #! removes a request handler registered via @ref setHandler() by reference
    /** @param handler the handler instance to remove

        @throw INVALID-HANDLER-ERROR the handler is not registered

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    removeHandler(HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler handler) {
        handlers.removeHandler(handler);
    }

    #! sets a dynamic request handler according to the arguments given
    setDynamicHandler(string name, HttpServer::AbstractUrlHandler obj) {
        dhandlers.setHandler(name, <HttpHandlerConfigInfo>{
            "path": obj.url_root,
            "isregex": False,
            "handler": obj,
        });
    }

    #! adds a request handler to a listener given the listener's name or bind address
    addHandlerToListener(softstring bind, string name, hash<HttpHandlerConfigInfo> info) {
        lm.enter();
        on_exit lm.exit();

        softstring id = getListenerIdFromBindUnlocked(bind);
        listeners{id}.addHandlers({name: info});
    }

    #! adds a request handler to a listener given the listener's id
    /** @param id listener id
        @param name handler name
        @param info the handler configuration info to add to the listener

        @note If the listener has a paired QUIC/HTTP3 listener, the handler is automatically
        propagated to it with a \c "-h3" suffix on the name.

        @throw INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR invalid listener id passed
    */
    addHandlerToListenerID(softint id, string name, hash<HttpHandlerConfigInfo> info) {
        lm.enter();
        on_exit lm.exit();

        if (!listeners.hasKey(id)) {
            throw "INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR", sprintf("there is no listener with ID %d", id);
        }

        listeners{id}.addHandlers({name: info});

        # propagate handler to the paired QUIC/HTTP3 listener if one exists
        if (*int quic_id = quic_listener_map{id}) {
            if (listeners.hasKey(quic_id)) {
                listeners{quic_id}.addHandlers({name + "-h3": info});
            }
        }
    }

    #! adds a request handler to a listener given the listener's id
    /** @param id listener id
        @param name handler name
        @param path handler path
        @param content_type handler's content type
        @param obj handler object
        @param special_headers headers to match
        @param isregex is the path a regular expression

        @throw INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR invalid listener id passed
    */
    addHandlerToListenerID(softint id, string name, string path, *softlist content_type,
            HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler obj, *softlist special_headers, bool isregex = True) {
        addHandlerToListenerID(id, name, <HttpHandlerConfigInfo>{
            "path": path,
            "isregex": isregex,
            "content_types": content_type,
            "handler": obj,
            "headers": special_headers,
        });
    }

    #! adds a request handler to a listener given the listener's name or bind address
    addHandlerToListener(softstring bind, string name, HttpServer::AbstractUrlHandler obj) {
        lm.enter();
        on_exit lm.exit();

        softstring id = getListenerIdFromBindUnlocked(bind);

        hash<string, hash<HttpHandlerConfigInfo>> h = {
            name: <HttpHandlerConfigInfo>{
                "path": obj.url_root,
                "isregex": False,
                "handler": obj,
            },
        };
        listeners{id}.addHandlers(h);
    }

    #! adds a request handler to a listener given the listener's id
    /** @param id listener id
        @param name handler name
        @param obj handler object

        @throw INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR invalid listener id passed
    */
    addHandlerToListenerID(softint id, string name, HttpServer::AbstractUrlHandler obj) {
        lm.enter();
        on_exit lm.exit();

        if (!listeners.hasKey(id)) {
            throw "INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR", sprintf("there is no listener with ID %d", id);
        }

        hash<string, hash<HttpHandlerConfigInfo>> h = {
            name: <HttpHandlerConfigInfo>{
                "path": obj.url_root,
                "isregex": False,
                "handler": obj,
            },
        };
        listeners{id}.addHandlers(h);

        # propagate handler to the paired QUIC/HTTP3 listener if one exists
        if (*int quic_id = quic_listener_map{id}) {
            if (listeners.hasKey(quic_id)) {
                listeners{quic_id}.addHandlers({name + "-h3": h{name}});
            }
        }
    }

    #! remove request handler from a listener given the listener's id
    /** @param id listener id
        @param handler_name name of handler to remove
        @return whether listener was stopped

        @note If the listener has a paired QUIC/HTTP3 listener, the propagated handler
        (with \c "-h3" suffix) is also removed from it.

        @throw INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR invalid listener id passed
    */
    bool removeHandlerFromListenerID(softstring id, string handler_name) {
        lm.enter();
        on_exit lm.exit();

        if (!listeners.hasKey(id)) {
            throw "INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR", sprintf("there is no listener with ID %d", id);
        }
        listeners{id}.removeHandler(handler_name);

        # also remove the propagated handler from the paired QUIC listener
        if (*int quic_id = quic_listener_map{id}) {
            if (listeners.hasKey(quic_id)) {
                try {
                    listeners{quic_id}.removeHandler(handler_name + "-h3");
                } catch () {
                    # handler may not exist on the QUIC listener (e.g., added before QUIC pairing)
                }
                HttpServer::HttpListener::stopIfNoHandlers(listeners{quic_id});
            }
        }

        return HttpServer::HttpListener::stopIfNoHandlers(listeners{id});
    }

    #! remove request handler from a listener given the listener's id
    /** @param id listener id
        @param handler handler to remove
        @return whether listener was stopped

        @note If the listener has a paired QUIC/HTTP3 listener, the handler is also removed
        from it.

        @throw INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR invalid listener id passed
    */
    bool removeHandlerFromListenerID(softstring id, HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler handler) {
        lm.enter();
        on_exit lm.exit();

        if (!listeners.hasKey(id)) {
            throw "INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR", sprintf("there is no listener with ID %d", id);
        }
        listeners{id}.removeHandler(handler);

        # also remove the same handler from the paired QUIC listener
        if (*int quic_id = quic_listener_map{id}) {
            if (listeners.hasKey(quic_id)) {
                try {
                    listeners{quic_id}.removeHandler(handler);
                } catch () {
                    # handler may not exist on the QUIC listener
                }
                HttpServer::HttpListener::stopIfNoHandlers(listeners{quic_id});
            }
        }

        return HttpServer::HttpListener::stopIfNoHandlers(listeners{id});
    }

    #! turns on or off header and body logging options for receive operations for the given listener
    /** @par Example:
        @code{.py}
http.setListenerLogOptions("app", LLO_RECV_HEADERS|LLO_SEND_HEADERS);
        @endcode

        @param bind the name of the listener or bind string
        @param code a binary-or'ed product of @ref ListenerLogOptions "listener log options"; bits set enable logging; bits not set disable logging

        @since HttpServer 0.3.11
     */
    setListenerLogOptions(softstring bind, softint code) {
        lm.enter();
        on_exit lm.exit();

        setListenerLogOptionsUnlocked(getListenerIdFromBindUnlocked(bind), code);
    }

    #! turns on or off header and body logging options for receive operations for the given listener
    /** @par Example:
        @code{.py}
http.setListenerLogOptionsID(0, LLO_RECV_HEADERS|LLO_SEND_HEADERS);
        @endcode

        @param id the unique listener ID
        @param code a binary-or'ed product of @ref ListenerLogOptions "listener log options"; bits set enable logging; bits not set disable logging

        @throw INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR invalid listener id passed

        @since HttpServer 0.3.11
     */
    setListenerLogOptionsID(softint id, softint code) {
        lm.enter();
        on_exit lm.exit();

        if (!listeners.hasKey(id))
            throw "INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR", sprintf("there is no listener with ID %d", id);

        setListenerLogOptionsUnlocked(id, code);
    }

    #! returns a binary-or'ed product of @ref ListenerLogOptions "listener log options" corresponding to enabled log options for the given listener
    /**
        @throw INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR invalid listener id passed

        @since HttpServer 0.3.11
    */
    int getListenerLogOptions(softstring bind) {
        lm.enter();
        on_exit lm.exit();

        return getListenerLogOptionsUnlocked(getListenerIdFromBindUnlocked(bind));
    }

    #! returns a binary-or'ed product of @ref ListenerLogOptions "listener log options" corresponding to enabled log options for the given listener
    /**
        @throw INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR invalid listener id passed

        @since HttpServer 0.3.11
    */
    int getListenerLogOptionsID(softint id) {
        lm.enter();
        on_exit lm.exit();

        if (!listeners.hasKey(id))
            throw "INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR", sprintf("there is no listener with ID %d", id);

        return getListenerLogOptionsUnlocked(id);
    }

    #! Disable dynamic handler
    /** @throw DISABLEHANDLER-ERROR if the given dynamic handler does not exist
    */
    disableDynamicHandler(string name) {
        dhandlers.disableHandler(name);
    }

    #! remove dynamic handler
    /** @throw REMOVEHANDLER-ERROR if the given dynamic handler does not exist
    */
    removeDynamicHandler(string name, *bool force_immediate) {
        dhandlers.removeHandler(name, force_immediate);
    }

    #! Returns @ref True if the arguments of messages for @ref log() should be prepared
    /** Reimplement in a subclass that reimplements @ref log() and can tell that the message will not be logged.

        This implementation returns @ref True if a logger allowing informational messages is set, or if no logger is
        set, as a subclass can reimplement @ref log() without one.

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    bool isLogInfoEnabled() {
        return !logger || logger.isEnabledFor(LoggerLevel::INFO);
    }

    #! called to log information to the registered log code
    log(string fmt, ...) {
        logger.logArgs(LoggerLevel::INFO, "HttpServer: " + fmt, argv);
    }

    #! called to log error information to the registered error log code
    logError(string fmt, ...) {
        logger.logArgs(LoggerLevel::ERROR, "HttpServer: " + fmt, argv);
    }

    #! Returns the character encoding of the text of a response: the charset parameter of its Content-Type, else the given encoding
    /** @param content_type the \c Content-Type of the response, if any
        @param default_encoding the response encoding, used when the \c Content-Type has no charset parameter
    */
    private static string getResponseTextEncoding(*string content_type, string default_encoding) {
        *string charset = (content_type =~ x/;\s*charset\s*=\s*"?([^";\s]+)/i)[0];
        return charset ?? default_encoding;
    }

    #! Returns True if the given names denote the same character encoding
    private static bool sameEncoding(string enc1, string enc2) {
        # the canonical names of the encodings are compared
        return http_get_charset_label(enc1) == http_get_charset_label(enc2);
    }

    #! Returns the Content-Type for a response body without one, or @ref nothing if none is to be sent
    /** A string body is HTML in the response encoding, as documented for handlers; binary data is
        \c application/octet-stream, as it has no character encoding; a body produced by a \c send_callback has no
        known type, so no \c Content-Type is sent (RFC 9110 section 8.3), and the client examines the data.

        @param rv the response
        @param encoding the character encoding of a string body
    */
    private static *string getDefaultContentType(hash<auto> rv, string encoding) {
        auto body = rv.body ?? rv.chunked_body;
        if (body instanceof StringInputStream || body.typeCode() == NT_STRING) {
            return body.val() || body instanceof InputStream ? MimeTypeHtml + ";charset=" + http_get_charset_label(encoding) : NOTHING;
        }
        if (body instanceof InputStream || body.typeCode() == NT_BINARY) {
            return body.val() || body instanceof InputStream ? MimeTypeOctetStream : NOTHING;
        }
        return NOTHING;
    }

    #! Sets the headers of the response to a \c HEAD request that depend on the message body, which the response does not send
    /** RFC 9110 section 9.3.2: the server should send the headers that a response to a \c GET request would have, and
        must not send a \c Content-Length unless it is the number of octets that the response to a \c GET request would
        have.  The \c Content-Type is that of the \c GET response (a body without one has a default, and a string body
        has its character encoding).  The \c Content-Length is set only when it is known to be the length of the body
        that a \c GET response sends: an in-memory body of binary data, or of text in ASCII sent in an encoding that
        has ASCII as its subset, which is not compressed; otherwise none is sent, which is allowed.

        The status code is the handler's: a handler that answers a \c HEAD request with a \c 200 response and no message
        body (the usual way to answer one) keeps its \c 200 status; only a \c GET response without a body is changed to
        \c 204.

        @param rv the response, with the message body that a \c GET request would get; its headers are set
        @param cx the connection context
        @param s the client socket
    */
    private static prepareHeadResponse(reference<hash<auto>> rv, hash<auto> cx, Socket s) {
        string encoding = cx."response-encoding" ?? s.getEncoding().lwr();
        if (!rv.hdr."Content-Type") {
            if (*string default_content_type = getDefaultContentType(rv, encoding)) {
                rv.hdr."Content-Type" = default_content_type;
            }
        }
        # the character encoding of a string body is given in the Content-Type, as for a GET response
        hash<auto> probe = rv;
        HttpServer::http_set_reply_headers(s, cx, \probe);
        if (probe.hdr."Content-Type") {
            rv.hdr."Content-Type" = probe.hdr."Content-Type";
        }

        # a body above the compression threshold is compressed for a client that accepts it: the GET response then has
        # a Content-Encoding header, and the length of the compressed body, which is not known without compressing it
        if (rv.body && !(rv.body instanceof InputStream) && !rv.send_callback && !rv.no_content_encoding
                && !rv.hdr."Content-Encoding" && rv.body.size() > CompressionThreshold
                && inlist(cx.encoding, ("br", "zstd", "deflate", "gzip", "bzip2"))) {
            rv.hdr."Content-Encoding" = cx.encoding;
            remove rv.hdr."Content-Length";
            return;
        }

        if (rv.hdr."Content-Length" || rv.hdr."Transfer-Encoding" || rv.hdr."Content-Encoding"
                || rv.hasKey("chunked_body") || rv.send_callback || rv.body instanceof InputStream) {
            # a Content-Length of the handler is sent as it is; a length that is not known is not sent
            return;
        }
        int tc = rv.body.typeCode();
        int len;
        if (tc == NT_BINARY) {
            len = rv.body.size();
        } else if (tc == NT_STRING) {
            string label = http_get_charset_label(encoding);
            if ((label != "utf-8" && label != "us-ascii" && label != "iso-8859-1") || rv.body !~ /^[\x00-\x7f]*$/) {
                return;
            }
            len = strlen(rv.body);
        } else {
            return;
        }
        rv.hdr."Content-Length" = string(len);
    }

    #! sends an HTTP error message on the socket
    sendHttpError(HttpListener listener, hash<auto> cx, Socket s, int status_code, *data msg, *InputStream chunked_msg,
            *hash<auto> extra_hdrs, *string encoding, bool head) {
        if (!s.isOpen()) {
            listener.log("cid %d: connection closed by peer", cx.id);
            return;
        }

        # the names of the fields managed by the server are canonical from here on; the headers are set in an
        # "auto!" local, as a caller's literal may be narrowed (e.g. hash<string, int> for {"X-Count": 1}), and
        # adding the Transfer-Encoding header would throw
        *hash<auto!> err_hdrs = http_canonicalize_reply_header_names(extra_hdrs);

        bool is_http2 = cx."header-info".http2 ?? False;
        bool is_multiplexed = is_http2 || (cx."header-info".http3 ?? False);
        data response_msg;
        # issue #3116: do not send a reply body if the response code does not allow it
        /** http://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc2616#section-4.4
            1.Any response message which "MUST NOT" include a message-body (such
            as the 1xx, 204, and 304 responses and any response to a HEAD
            request) is always terminated by the first empty line after the
            header fields, regardless of the entity-header fields present in
            the message.
        */
        bool body_allowed = !head && (status_code >= 200) && (status_code != 204) && (status_code != 304);
        if (msg || !chunked_msg) {
            if (body_allowed) {
                if (msg.typeCode() == NT_STRING) {
                    response_msg = !err_hdrs."Content-Type" && acceptsHtml(cx)
                        ? getHtmlErrorMessage(status_code, msg)
                        : msg;
                } else {
                    response_msg = msg;
                }
            }
        } else if (!body_allowed) {
            chunked_msg = NOTHING;
        } else {
            if (chunked_msg instanceof StringInputStream) {
                # ignore any encoding passed in case of a string input stream response body, and use its encoding
                encoding = cast<StringInputStream>(chunked_msg).getEncoding();
            }
            if (is_multiplexed) {
                # Keep the InputStream for HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 streaming - don't buffer
            } else if (HttpServer::requestAllowsChunked(cx.hdr ?? {})) {
                err_hdrs."Transfer-Encoding" = "chunked";
            } else {
                # a response to an HTTP/1.0 request must not have Transfer-Encoding (RFC 9112 section 6.1); an error
                # message is small, so it is sent with a Content-Length instead of closing the connection after it
                response_msg = readAllStream(chunked_msg);
                chunked_msg = NOTHING;
            }
        }
        if (!chunked_msg) {
            # a message body in memory is sent with a Content-Length
            remove err_hdrs."Transfer-Encoding";
        }

        # restore encoding on exit
        string old_encoding = s.getEncoding();
        on_exit if (old_encoding != s.getEncoding())
            s.setEncoding(old_encoding);

        if (!encoding) {
            encoding = s.getEncoding().lwr();
        } else if (encoding != s.getEncoding().lwr()) {
            s.setEncoding(encoding);
        }

        hash<auto> hdr += getResponseHeaders(cx."listener-id");

        # an error message is text unless it is binary data; binary data has no character encoding
        bool binary_msg = response_msg.typeCode() == NT_BINARY
            || (chunked_msg && !(chunked_msg instanceof StringInputStream));
        if (!err_hdrs."Content-Type") {
            hdr."Content-Type" = binary_msg ? MimeTypeOctetStream : MimeTypeHtml + ";charset=" + http_get_charset_label(encoding);
        } else if (!binary_msg && err_hdrs."Content-Type" !~ /charset=/i) {
            err_hdrs."Content-Type" += ";charset=" + http_get_charset_label(encoding);
        }

        if (!is_multiplexed) {
            hdr.Connection = cx.close ? "close" : "Keep-Alive";
        }

        hdr = mergeResponseHeaders(hdr, err_hdrs);

        # Merge any response headers stashed by a handler's preprocessRequest() —
        # e.g. CORS headers — so that framework-level errors still return headers
        # the browser needs to read the body
        if (cx."error-response-headers".typeCode() == NT_HASH) {
            hdr = mergeResponseHeaders(hdr, cx."error-response-headers");
        }
        hdr = http_canonicalize_reply_header_names(hdr);

        hdr.Date = getDateHeader();
        if (is_multiplexed) {
            # HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 prohibit connection-specific headers (RFC 9113 Section 8.2.2,
            # RFC 9114 Section 4.2)
            remove hdr{"Connection", "Keep-Alive", "Proxy-Connection", "Transfer-Encoding", "Upgrade", "TE"};
        }

        # log exceptions if not "SOCKET-SEND-ERROR" (probably broken pipe)
        try {
            if (is_http2) {
                # HTTP/2: send response via HTTP/2 DATA frames
                int stream_id = cx."header-info".stream_id;
                auto send_body = response_msg ?? chunked_msg;
                AbstractPollOperation op;
                if (send_body instanceof InputStream) {
                    # Use streaming response for InputStream bodies
                    op = s.startPollSendHttp2StreamingResponse(stream_id, status_code, hdr,
                        send_body);
                } else {
                    if (send_body && !hdr."Content-Length") {
                        hdr."Content-Length" = string(send_body.size());
                    }
                    op = s.startPollSendHttp2Response(stream_id, status_code, hdr, send_body);
                }
                execAsyncPollOperation(s, op);
            } else if (cx."header-info".http3) {
                # HTTP/3: send response via QUIC stream (NOT the HTTP/1.x sendHTTPResponse path)
                int session_id = cx."header-info".session_id;
                int stream_id = cx."header-info".stream_id;
                auto send_body = response_msg ?? chunked_msg;
                if (send_body instanceof InputStream) {
                    # HTTP/3: stream InputStream response incrementally to avoid buffering
                    AbstractPollOperation op = s.startPollQuicSendStreamingResponse(
                        session_id, stream_id, status_code, hdr, send_body);
                    execAsyncPollOperation(s, op);
                } else {
                    s.submitQuicResponse(session_id, stream_id, status_code, hdr, send_body);
                }
            } else if (head && status_code >= 200) {
                # the response to a HEAD request has no Content-Length added: the length of the GET response is not
                # that of the (empty) message body sent here
                execAsyncPollOperation(s, s.startPollSendHttpResponse(status_code,
                    HttpServer::HttpCodes{status_code}, "1.1", hdr, NOTHING, True));
            } else {
                s.sendHTTPResponse(status_code, HttpServer::HttpCodes{status_code}, "1.1", hdr,
                    response_msg ?? chunked_msg);
            }
            listener.logResponse(cx, status_code, response_msg ?? chunked_msg, hdr);
            if (status_code >= 200) {
                cx."response-sent" = True;
            }
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            if (ex.err != "SOCKET-SEND-ERROR") {
                string estr = sprintf("%s: %s: %s", get_ex_pos(ex), ex.err, ex.desc);
                logError("%s", estr);
            }
        }
    }

    string getHtmlErrorMessage(int status_code, string msg) {
        return sprintf("<html><head><title>%s %s</title></head><body><h1>%s</h1><pre>%s</pre><p><hr><address>%s on "
            "%s</address></body></html>",
                status_code, HttpServer::HttpCodes{status_code}, HttpServer::HttpCodes{status_code},
                html_encode(msg), http_server_string, gethostname());
    }

    static bool acceptsHtml(hash<auto> cx) {
        if (!cx.hdr.accept.val() || cx.hdr.accept =~ /\btext\/html\b/i || cx.hdr.accept =~ /\b\*\/\*\b/
            || cx.hdr.accept =~ /\btext\/\*\b/i) {
            return True;
        }
        return False;
    }

    #! masks log messages by removing sensitive data
    /** @see setMaskCode()

        @since %HttpServer 0.3.12
    */
    string maskData(string msg) {
        return maskfunc ? maskfunc(msg) : http_mask_data(msg);
    }

    #! sets the @ref closure "closure" or @ref call_reference "call reference" that will be used to mask sensitive data in log messages
    /** @par Example
        @code{.py}
        http_server.setMaskCode(string sub (string msg) { return do_mask(msg); });
        @endcode

        @param maskfunc a @ref closure "closure" or @ref call_reference "call reference" that takes a single string argument and returns the masked string

        @see maskData()

        @since %HttpServer 0.3.12
    */
    setMaskCode(code<string(string)> maskfunc) {
        self.maskfunc = maskfunc;
    }

    #! Returns supported encodings in decreasing quality order, by ContentEncodingPreference on ties
    /** The result depends only on the header value and every request of a client carries the same one, so it is
        computed once per distinct value (see @ref encoding_preferences_cache)
    */
    private static hash<string, float> getContentEncodingPreferences(*string header) {
        string key = header ?? "";
        if (key.size() > MaxCachedEncodingHeaderLength) {
            return computeContentEncodingPreferences(header);
        }
        {
            encoding_preferences_lck.lock();
            on_exit encoding_preferences_lck.unlock();
            if (encoding_preferences_cache.hasKey(key)) {
                return encoding_preferences_cache{key};
            }
        }
        hash<string, float> rv = computeContentEncodingPreferences(header);
        {
            encoding_preferences_lck.lock();
            on_exit encoding_preferences_lck.unlock();
            if (encoding_preferences_cache.size() >= MaxCachedEncodingPreferences) {
                encoding_preferences_cache = {};
            }
            encoding_preferences_cache{key} = rv;
        }
        return rv;
    }

    #! Computes the result of getContentEncodingPreferences() for a header value
    private static hash<string, float> computeContentEncodingPreferences(*string header) {
        hash<string, float> weights;
        foreach string item in ((header ?? "").split(",")) {
            list<string> parts = item.lwr().split(";");
            string name = trim(parts[0]);
            if (!name) {
                continue;
            }
            float quality = 1.0;
            if (parts.size() > 1) {
                *list<*string> match = (trim(parts[1]) =~ x/^q\s*=\s*(0(?:\.[0-9]{0,3})?|1(?:\.0{0,3})?)$/);
                if (!match || parts.size() != 2) {
                    quality = 0.0;
                } else {
                    quality = match[0].toFloat();
                }
            }
            name = ContentEncodings{name} ?? name;
            weights{name} = weights.hasKey(name) ? min(weights{name}, quality) : quality;
        }
        # the candidates in the order of preference for the same weight
        hash<string, float> candidates;
        foreach string encoding in (ContentEncodingPreference) {
            candidates{encoding} = weights{encoding} ?? weights."*" ?? 0.0;
        }
        # Unspecified identity is a fallback, not a preference above explicitly accepted encodings.
        candidates.identity = weights.identity ?? (weights.hasKey("*") && weights."*" == 0.0 ? 0.0 : 0.001);
        hash<string, int> preference_order;
        int position = 0;
        foreach string encoding in (keys candidates) {
            preference_order{encoding} = position++;
        }
        list<string> encodings = sort(keys candidates, int sub(string left, string right) {
            return (candidates{right} <=> candidates{left})
                ?: (preference_order{left} <=> preference_order{right});
        });
        hash<string, float> result;
        foreach string encoding in (encodings) {
            result{encoding} = candidates{encoding};
        }
        return result;
    }

    #! returns a complete URL from a bind address
    /** @param bind the bind address; if for any reason there is a path in the bind address, it will be ignored
        @param host the hostname to use in case the bind string is only a port number; if none is passed or the value passed is equal to the return value of @ref Qore::gethostname(), then \c "localhost" is used

        @note reexported from @ref HttpServer::http_get_url_from_bind()
     */
    static string getURLFromBind(softstring bind, *string host) {
        return HttpServer::http_get_url_from_bind(bind, host);
    }

    #! turns on or off debugging; when debugging is enabled more verbose error messages are reported
    setDebug(bool dbg = True) {
        debug = dbg;
    }

    #! returns the current status of the debug flag
    bool getDebug() {
        return debug;
    }

    #! Adds or replaces a fast route: a path that the async I/O thread answers without calling a handler
    /** A request for the path that qualifies is answered on the async I/O thread, with no hand-off to a worker
        thread and no Qore code; every other request takes the normal path.  A route answers a \c GET request for its
        exact path (the query string is not part of the path) that has no body, no \c Expect or \c Upgrade header, is
        HTTP/1.1 (with a keep-alive connection, or with \c Connection: \c close, in which case the response has
        \c Connection: \c close and the connection is closed after it), and has no further request already received.
        Every request of a connection is covered, the first one included.  The route is answered without the handler
        chain, and so without authentication, logging or request preprocessing: \c public must be \c True.

        A \c HEAD request for the path is answered the same way, with the headers of the response to a \c GET request
        (with its \c Content-Length, except for a \c 204 response, which has none) and no message body
        (RFC 9110 section 9.3.2).

        An HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 request is answered the same way, when its headers have been received (a request with a body
        takes the normal path).

        The server's configured response headers (and the \c Server header) are added to each response, so that it has
        the headers that the normal path sends, including the per-listener \c Alt-Svc header that advertises HTTP/3
        on a listener that has it (a header that the route's response has itself replaces it).

        @param path the exact path of the route, starting with \c "/"
        @param info the route; see @ref Qore::HttpFastRoutes::addRoute() "HttpFastRoutes::addRoute()" for the keys.  A response
        in it can set \c hdr to add or replace response headers

        @par Example:
        @code{.py}
FastCell ready(0, 30s);
hs.addFastRoute("/ready", {
    "public": True,
    "rules": (
        {"cell": ready, "op": "==", "value": 1, "response": {"code": 200, "body": "ok", "content_type": "text/plain"}},
    ),
    "otherwise": {"code": 503, "body": "not ready", "content_type": "text/plain"},
});

# later, from any thread
ready.set(1);
        @endcode

        @throw HTTP-FAST-ROUTE-ERROR the route is not valid

        @see
        - @ref Qore::FastCell "FastCell"
        - @ref Qore::FastResponseSlot "FastResponseSlot"
        - examples/fast-routes.qr: a program that serves a readiness check, a status document and a health check, as
          fast routes, as an inline handler and with an ordinary handler

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    addFastRoute(string path, hash<auto> info) {
        getOrCreateFastRoutes().addRoute(path, addServerHeadersToFastRoute(info));
    }

    #! Removes a fast route
    /** Requests for the path take the normal path (the handler) from then on.

        @param path the path of the route

        @return @ref True if there was a route for the path

        @par Example:
        @code{.py}
# stop answering /ready from the fast path while the application restarts its checks
if (hs.removeFastRoute("/ready")) {
    printf("the route was removed\n");
}
        @endcode

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    bool removeFastRoute(string path) {
        *HttpFastRoutes routes = getFastRoutes();
        return routes ? routes.removeRoute(path) : False;
    }

    #! Returns the number of requests that each fast route answered, and why the other requests took the normal path
    /** The counters show how many requests bypass the handler chain (and so its logging and authentication), and why a
        request that was expected to be answered by a route was not: for example \c stale when the cell or the slot of
        a route had not been updated for longer than its maximum age.

        @return a hash with the keys \c routes and \c fallbacks; see @ref Qore::HttpFastRoutes::getInfo()

        @par Example:
        @code{.py}
hash<auto> info = hs.getFastRouteInfo();
printf("/ready answered %d requests; %d took the normal path because a cell was stale\n",
    info.routes."/ready" ?? 0, info.fallbacks.stale);
        @endcode

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    hash<auto> getFastRouteInfo() {
        *HttpFastRoutes routes = getFastRoutes();
        return routes ? routes.getInfo() : {"routes": {}, "fallbacks": {}};
    }

    #! Returns the fast routes, if any route was added
    /** The listeners of the server (the existing ones and those added later) have them attached to their sockets.
    */
    private *HttpFastRoutes getFastRoutes() {
        fast_routes_lock.lock();
        on_exit fast_routes_lock.unlock();
        return fast_routes;
    }

    #! Returns the fast routes, creating them and attaching them to the listeners if there are none yet
    private HttpFastRoutes getOrCreateFastRoutes() {
        bool created = False;
        HttpFastRoutes routes;
        {
            fast_routes_lock.lock();
            on_exit fast_routes_lock.unlock();
            if (!fast_routes) {
                fast_routes = new HttpFastRoutes();
                created = True;
            }
            routes = fast_routes;
        }
        if (created) {
            # a listener added from now on attaches the routes itself (see addListenerIntern()); the existing ones
            # are attached here
            *hash<string, HttpListener> llist;
            {
                lm.enter();
                on_exit lm.exit();
                llist = listeners;
            }
            map $1.setHttpFastRoutes(routes), llist.iterator();
            # the sockets of the QUIC listeners (their sessions are created from them)
            list<Socket> usocks;
            {
                fast_routes_lock.lock();
                on_exit fast_routes_lock.unlock();
                usocks = quic_udp_sockets.values();
            }
            foreach Socket usock in (usocks) {
                try {
                    usock.setHttpFastRoutes(routes);
                } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                    # a listener that is being stopped
                }
            }
        }
        return routes;
    }

    #! Adds the server's response headers to the responses of a fast route description
    private hash<auto> addServerHeadersToFastRoute(hash<auto> info0) {
        hash<auto> base = getResponseHeaders(0);
        if (!(map $1, keys base, $1.lwr() == "server")) {
            base.Server = HttpServerString;
        }
        # the hashes are copied to "auto!" locals: a literal such as {"code": 204} has the type hash<string, int>
        code add = auto sub (auto response) {
            if (response instanceof FastResponseSlot) {
                # the slot builds its responses with the server's headers and the 204 rule, as for a hash below
                cast<FastResponseSlot>(response).setDefaults(base);
                return response;
            }
            if (response.typeCode() != NT_HASH) {
                # not a response: the route parser reports it
                return response;
            }
            hash<auto!> r = response;
            # as for the response of a handler, a 200 response without a body is a 204 response (see
            # sendResponse()), and has no body
            if ((r.code ?? 200) == 200) {
                *auto body = r.body;
                int tc = body.typeCode();
                bool empty = tc == NT_NOTHING || tc == NT_NULL || ((tc == NT_STRING || tc == NT_BINARY)
                    && !body.size());
                if (empty) {
                    r.code = 204;
                    remove r.body;
                }
            }
            r.hdr = HttpServer::mergeResponseHeaders(base, r.hdr);
            return r;
        };
        hash<auto!> info = info0;
        if (info.response) {
            info.response = add(info.response);
        }
        if (info.otherwise) {
            info.otherwise = add(info.otherwise);
        }
        if (info.rules.typeCode() == NT_LIST) {
            info.rules = map (($1.typeCode() == NT_HASH && $1.response) ? $1 + {"response": add($1.response)} : $1),
                info.rules;
        }
        return info;
    }

    #! Returns a connection to the async I/O controller for keep-alive handling
    /** @param sock the socket to return
        @param cx the connection context
        @param listener the listener for this connection

        @since HttpServer 1.5
    */
    returnConnectionToAsyncIo(Socket sock, hash<auto> cx, HttpListener listener) {
        if (asyncIo && asyncTarget) {
            asyncIo.addIdleConnection(sock, listener, getAsyncConnectionContext(cx), asyncTarget, ttl);
        }
    }

    #! Closes an HTTP/1.x connection after its last response, which the current handler thread sent
    /** The connection is closed with a lingering close, so the client receives the response even if it is still
        sending data that the server did not read; see the \c lingering_close_timeout option in
        @ref HttpServerOptionInfo.

        @param sock the connection's socket; the response must have been sent completely
        @param cx the connection context
        @param listener the listener that accepted the connection

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    closeConnectionAfterResponse(Socket sock, hash<auto> cx, HttpListener listener) {
        if (asyncIo && asyncTarget) {
            asyncIo.closeConnection(sock, listener, getAsyncConnectionContext(cx), asyncTarget);
        } else {
            sock.close();
        }
    }

    #! Returns the connection context for the async I/O controller from a request context
    private hash<auto> getAsyncConnectionContext(hash<auto> cx) {
        hash<auto> async_cx = exists cx.peer ? cx : cast<hash>({
            "peer": cx."peer-info",
            "socket": cx."socket-info",
        });
        if (exists cx{"peer-info-future"}) {
            async_cx{"peer-info-future"} = cx{"peer-info-future"};
        }
        return async_cx;
    }

    #! Waits for async I/O to be ready to accept connections
    /** This method blocks until the async I/O controller and its coordinator thread are ready
        to process connections. Call this after @ref addListener() (with \c sync: \c False) to
        ensure the server is fully ready before clients connect.

        When \c sync is \c True (the default), @ref addListener() calls this method automatically,
        so an explicit call is only needed when adding multiple listeners with \c sync: \c False
        for batch setup efficiency.

        Common use cases:
        - Test setup: call after \c addListener() to guarantee the server is accepting before
          clients connect
        - Batch listener setup: add several listeners with \c sync: \c False, then call this
          once to wait for all of them
        - Orchestration: ensure the server is fully operational before advertising its address
          to service registries, load balancers, or health checks

        @param timeout maximum time to wait; use @ref NOTHING for no timeout (default: 5 seconds)

        @return @ref True if async I/O is ready, @ref False if the timeout expired

        @since HttpServer 1.1.4
    */
    bool waitForAsyncIo(*timeout timeout) {
        if (!asyncIo) {
            return False;
        }
        timeout = timeout ?? 5s;
        # Wait for the global async I/O controller's I/O thread to be ready
        return AsyncSocketIo::getGlobalAsyncIoController().waitForProcessing(timeout);
    }

    startConnection(code<nothing()> c) {
        threadPool.submit(c);
    }

    #! This method is called after each request to clear context info so that it does not leak into other requests
    /** Reimplement in subclasses if necessarily

        @since HttpServer 1.1.2
    */
    clearContextInfo() {
        # this method left intentionally blank
    }

    #! Calculate the Date header only once per second
    static string getDateHeader() {
        int now = clock_getseconds();
        if (now != cached_date_epoch) {
            # Update header BEFORE epoch to avoid a race: if epoch is set first,
            # a concurrent thread sees epoch == now and returns the stale (or
            # uninitialized NOTHING) cached_date_header before we assign it.
            cached_date_header = gmtime().format("Dy, DD Mon YYYY HH:mm:SS") + " GMT";
            cached_date_epoch = now;
        }
        return cached_date_header;
    }

    #! Common constructor code
    private init(hash<HttpServerOptionInfo> opts) {
        drain_timeout = opts.drain_timeout ?? opts.quic_drain_timeout ?? DefaultDrainTimeout;
        # checked before anything is created
        compression_levels = checkCompressionLevels(opts.compression_levels ?? {});
        int idle_threads = opts.idle_threads;
        if (idle_threads <= 0) {
            # auto-detect: 2 × CPU count (or the fixed fallback when sysconf is
            # unavailable).  See HttpServerOptionInfo::idle_threads for why this
            # lives here rather than in the hashdecl member initializer.
%ifndef NO_SYSCONF
            idle_threads = sysconf(SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN) * 2;
%else
            idle_threads = HttpServer::DefaultIdleThreads;
%endif
        }
        if (idle_threads < 1) {
            throw "HTTP-SERVER-INIT-ERROR", sprintf("idle_threads (%d) must be greater than zero", idle_threads);
        }
        if (opts.io_operation_timeout <= 0) {
            throw "HTTP-SERVER-INIT-ERROR", sprintf("io_operation_timeout (%d ms) must be greater than zero",
                opts.io_operation_timeout);
        }
        threadPool = new ThreadPool(-1, idle_threads);
        # Apply sentinel-based defaults for members whose "real" default is a
        # RuntimeConstantRefNode or expression too complex for the AOT writer
        # to round-trip faithfully; see the hashdecl declaration for context.
        http_server_string = opts.name.val() ? opts.name : HttpServer::HttpServerString;
        debug = opts.debug;
        hdr = opts.hdr.val()
            ? http_canonicalize_reply_header_names(opts.hdr)
            : {"X-Powered-By": "Qore/" + Qore::VersionString};
        protect_last_listener = opts.protect_last_listener ?? False;
        logger = opts.logger ?? new Logger(http_server_string, LoggerLevel::getLevelInfo());
        date effective_ttl = opts.ttl ? opts.ttl : HttpServer::DefaultTtl;
        if (effective_ttl) {
            setTtl(effective_ttl);
        }
        max_request_body_size = opts.max_request_body_size ?? 0;
        max_decoded_body_size = opts.max_decoded_body_size ?? 0;
        enable_h3 = opts.enable_h3 ?? True;
        alt_svc_max_age = opts.alt_svc_max_age ?? 3600;
        # Initialize async I/O
        AsyncSocketIo::AsyncSocketIoController ctrl = AsyncSocketIo::getGlobalAsyncIoController();
        # the global controller logs through the server's logger while the server runs
        setControllerLogger();
        on_error restoreControllerLogger();
        asyncTarget = new Priv::HttpServerNativeAsyncTarget(logger);
        asyncIo = new HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpAsyncSocketIoController(ctrl, logger, {
            "max_handler_threads": opts.max_handler_threads,
            "min_idle_handler_threads": opts.min_idle_handler_threads,
            "max_idle_handler_threads": opts.max_idle_handler_threads,
            "quic_drain_timeout": drain_timeout,
            "quic_headers_only": opts.quic_headers_only,
            "lingering_close_timeout": opts.lingering_close_timeout,
            "peer_hostname_lookup": opts.peer_hostname_lookup,
            "io_operation_timeout": opts.io_operation_timeout,
        });
        asyncIo.start();
    }

    #! Sets the server's logger on the global async I/O controller
    /** The controller is shared by the whole process and has a single logger, so the logger of the server that set
        it last is used; restoreControllerLogger() gives the controller back the logger this server replaced when the
        server stops, so a stopped server's logger is not kept by the controller and the controller's logger is that
        of a server still running, or the one it had before the servers set theirs.
    */
    private:internal setControllerLogger() {
        AutoLock al(controller_logger_lck);
        controller_loggers += <Priv::ControllerLoggerInfo>{
            "owner": self.uniqueHash(),
            "logger": logger,
            "prev": Qore::AsyncIoController::getLogger(),
        };
        Qore::AsyncIoController::setLogger(logger);
    }

    #! Gives the global async I/O controller back the logger that this server replaced; see setControllerLogger()
    /** Does nothing if the server did not set its logger on the controller or has already restored it.
    */
    private:internal restoreControllerLogger() {
        string owner = self.uniqueHash();
        # holds the entry removed until after the lock has been released, so that a logger released here is
        # destroyed without the lock held: its destructor can run code that creates or stops a server
        *hash<Priv::ControllerLoggerInfo> info;
        {
            AutoLock al(controller_logger_lck);
            *int i;
            foreach hash<Priv::ControllerLoggerInfo> entry in (controller_loggers) {
                if (entry.owner == owner) {
                    i = $#;
                    break;
                }
            }
            if (!exists i) {
                return;
            }
            info = controller_loggers[i];
            if (i < (controller_loggers.size() - 1)) {
                # a later server replaced this server's logger: that server restores the logger this server
                # replaced
                if (controller_loggers[i + 1].prev == info.logger) {
                    controller_loggers[i + 1].prev = info.prev;
                }
            } else if (Qore::AsyncIoController::getLogger() == info.logger) {
                # this server's logger is replaced only if nothing else has replaced it on the controller since
                Qore::AsyncIoController::setLogger(info.prev);
            }
            splice controller_loggers, i, 1;
        }
    }

    #! Updates the per-listener Alt-Svc response headers based on active QUIC listeners
    /** Rebuilds the \c Alt-Svc header for each TCP listener that has a QUIC counterpart.
        Each TCP listener only advertises its own QUIC port (not all QUIC ports) to prevent
        browsers from connecting to the wrong QUIC listener when multiple listeners are active.
        Called whenever QUIC listeners are added or removed.

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    private updateAltSvcHeader() {
        # Build per-listener Alt-Svc headers.  Each TCP listener advertises
        # only its own QUIC counterpart so browsers don't confuse origins.
        # NOTE: thread-safe because Qore hash/string value assignments are
        # atomic (reference-counted); readers get the old or new value,
        # never a partial/corrupt value.
        foreach string lid in (keys quic_port_map) {
            int quic_port = quic_port_map{lid};
            int ma = quic_alt_svc_max_age{lid} ?? alt_svc_max_age;
            listener_alt_svc{lid} = sprintf("h3=\":%d\"; ma=%d", quic_port, ma);
        }
        # Remove entries for TCP listeners that no longer have QUIC counterparts
        foreach string lid in (keys listener_alt_svc) {
            if (!quic_port_map.hasKey(lid)) {
                remove listener_alt_svc{lid};
            }
        }
        # Keep global hdr clean (no Alt-Svc) — it's injected per-listener
        remove hdr."Alt-Svc";

        # the responses of the fast routes carry the listener's Alt-Svc header too
        list<HttpListener> targets;
        {
            fast_routes_lock.lock();
            on_exit fast_routes_lock.unlock();
            targets = alt_svc_targets.values();
        }
        foreach HttpListener l in (targets) {
            applyAltSvcToFastRoutes(l);
        }
    }

    #! Gives the socket of a TCP listener its Alt-Svc header (or removes it) for the responses of the fast routes
    /** The sockets accepted from the listener, also those that are open, use it.  A listener whose socket is being
        stopped is ignored.
    */
    private applyAltSvcToFastRoutes(HttpListener l) {
        *string value = listener_alt_svc{string(l.getID())};
        try {
            l.setHttpFastExtraHeaders(value ? {"Alt-Svc": value} : NOTHING);
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            # a listener that is being stopped
        }
    }

    #! Returns response headers with per-listener Alt-Svc injected
    /** @param listener_id the TCP listener ID (from cx."listener-id")
        @return the base response headers with Alt-Svc for this listener's QUIC counterpart
    */
    private:internal hash<auto> getResponseHeaders(int listener_id) {
        # the headers are copied to an "auto!" local: the hdr option may be narrowed (e.g. hash<string, int> for
        # {"X-Instance": 1}), and every response adds string headers to them
        hash<auto!> rv = self.hdr;
        if (*string alt_svc = listener_alt_svc{listener_id}) {
            rv."Alt-Svc" = alt_svc;
        }
        return rv;
    }

    #! Returns @ref True if an HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 extended CONNECT that a handler answered with an ordinary 2xx response is refused
    /** Any 2xx response to an extended CONNECT establishes a tunnel (RFC 8441 section 4, RFC 9220); a handler that
        answers one with an ordinary (non-streaming) 2xx response, such as a catch-all handler that serves a page for
        every path, cannot accept the protocol, so the server answers @c 501 instead of sending the response as it
        is.  A subclass can override this method to send such responses unchanged, as a server without this check
        did, for example to test how clients handle them; a production server should not override it, as clients
        take such a response for an accepted tunnel.

        @param cx the call context hash
        @param hdr the request headers
        @param rv the handler's response

        @return @ref True (the default) to answer @c 501, @ref False to send the handler's response as it is

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    private bool refuseNonTunnelExtendedConnectResponse(hash<auto> cx, hash<auto> hdr, hash<auto> rv) {
        return True;
    }

    #! Returns the configured response headers of the listener merged with a handler's response headers
    /** @param conf_hdr the configured response headers (\c hdr), read once for the response
        @param info the information about the names of the fields of \a conf_hdr; see getConfiguredHeaderInfo()
        @param listener_id the listener ID
        @param hdr the response headers of the handler; the names of the fields managed by the server must be
        canonical (see @ref HttpServer::http_canonicalize_reply_header_names())

        @return the merged headers; a field in \a hdr replaces the configured fields with the same name in any case
    */
    private:internal hash<auto> getMergedResponseHeaders(hash<auto> conf_hdr, hash<Priv::ConfiguredHeaderInfo> info,
            int listener_id, *hash<auto> hdr) {
        # the headers of getResponseHeaders(), built here, as this is called for every response and a call of that
        # method costs more than building them
        hash<auto!> base = conf_hdr;
        *string alt_svc = listener_alt_svc{listener_id};
        if (alt_svc) {
            base."Alt-Svc" = alt_svc;
        }
        if (!hdr) {
            return base;
        }
        # this is called for every response: the plain hash addition gives the merged headers when the handler's
        # headers have no field of the configured headers in any case; when they have one, the merge removes the
        # configured field, so that the handler's field is sent in its own place, as any other field of the handler
        if (info.merge_names || alt_svc) {
            string names = "\n" + (keys hdr).join("\n").lwr() + "\n";
            if (alt_svc && names.find("\nalt-svc\n") >= 0) {
                return mergeResponseHeaders(base, hdr);
            }
            foreach string name in (info.merge_names) {
                if (names.find(name) >= 0) {
                    return mergeResponseHeaders(base, hdr);
                }
            }
        }
        return base + hdr;
    }

    #! Returns the information about the names of the fields of the configured response headers given
    /** Called by sendReply() when the configured headers (\c hdr) of a response are not equal to those that
        \c hdr_info was made for (a subclass can change \c hdr after the constructor and while requests are served);
        the information is kept in \c hdr_info for the next responses.  It depends only on the names of the fields,
        which equal headers share, so a response uses it with its own configured headers, whose fields can be in
        another order
    */
    private:internal hash<Priv::ConfiguredHeaderInfo> getConfiguredHeaderInfo(hash<auto> conf_hdr) {
        hash<Priv::ConfiguredHeaderInfo> info = <Priv::ConfiguredHeaderInfo>{
            "hdr": conf_hdr,
            # the canonical names replace the others, so the keys differ unless they are all canonical
            "canonical": http_canonicalize_reply_header_names(conf_hdr) == conf_hdr,
            "merge_names": map "\n" + $1.lwr() + "\n", keys conf_hdr,
        };
        hdr_info = info;
        return info;
    }

    #! Returns \a hdr merged into \a base; a field in \a hdr replaces the fields of \a base with the same name in any case
    private static hash<auto> mergeResponseHeaders(hash<auto> base, *hash<auto> hdr) {
        if (!hdr) {
            return base;
        }
        if (!base) {
            return hdr;
        }
        hash<string, bool> names = map {$1.lwr(): True}, keys hdr;
        return (map {$1.key: $1.value}, base.pairIterator(), !names{$1.key.lwr()}) + hdr;
    }

    #! returns the listener ID from the bind name or throws an exception if not valid
    /** @throw INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR invalid listener bind address
    */
    private int getListenerIdFromBindUnlocked(string bind) {
        *int id;
        if (!(id = (smap{bind} ?? nmap{bind}))){
            throw "INVALID-LISTENER-ERROR", sprintf("there is no listener with name or bind address %y", bind);
        }
        return id;
    }

    #! turns on or off header and body logging options for the given listener according to the option code
    private setListenerLogOptionsUnlocked(softstring id, int code) {
        HttpListener l = listeners{id};
        l.logRecvHeaders(code & LLO_RECV_HEADERS);
        l.logRecvBody(code & LLO_RECV_BODY);
        l.logSendHeaders(code & LLO_SEND_HEADERS);
        l.logSendBody(code & LLO_SEND_BODY);
    }

    #! returns header and body logging options for the given listener as a binary-or'ed value of @ref ListenerLogOptions "listener log option codes"
    private int getListenerLogOptionsUnlocked(softstring id) {
        HttpListener l = listeners{id};
        hash<auto> h = l.getLogOptions();
        return
            (h.recv_headers ? LLO_RECV_HEADERS : 0)
            | (h.recv_body ? LLO_RECV_BODY : 0)
            | (h.send_headers ? LLO_SEND_HEADERS : 0)
            | (h.send_body ? LLO_SEND_BODY : 0);
    }

    #! helper method to set HTTP response headers
    static nothing setReplyHeaders(Socket s, hash<auto> cx, reference<hash<auto>> rv) {
        HttpServer::http_set_reply_headers(s, cx, \rv);
    }

    #! Creates a new HttpListener object; reimplement in subclasses to override the listener class
    private HttpListener getListener(HttpServer server, int id, hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> info) {
        return new HttpListener(server, \handleRequest(), id, seqSessions, info);
    }

    #! @cond nodoc
    private HttpListener addListenerIntern(*string node, *softstring service, *Qore::SSLCertificate cert,
            *Qore::SSLPrivateKey key, *hash<string, hash<HttpHandlerConfigInfo>> handler_info, *LoggerInterface logger,
            *code stopc, *string name, int family = AF_UNSPEC) {
        return addListenerIntern(<HttpListenerOptionInfo>{
            "node": node,
            "service": service,
            "cert": cert,
            "key": key,
            "handler_info": handler_info,
            "logger": logger,
            "stopc": stopc,
            "family": family,
        });
    }

    private HttpListener addListenerIntern(hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> info) {
        if (!exists info.node && !exists info.service) {
            throw "HTTPSERVER-ADDLISTENER-ERROR", sprintf("missing both \"node\" and \"service\" from listener option hash: %y", info);
        }
        checkSseMaxBufferSize(info);

        string sock = (exists info.service ? sprintf("%s:%s", info.node ?? "*", info.service) : info.node);

        lm.enter();
        on_exit lm.exit();

        if (smap{sock}) {
            throw "HTTPSERVER-ADDLISTENER-ERROR", sprintf("cannot add listener on %y; there is already an existing listener with this bind address", sock);
        }

        # A "random port" request (service == "0") lets us transparently retry
        # if the kernel-assigned TCP port turns out to be taken on UDP by
        # something else in the process — most commonly a still-lingering
        # ephemeral UDP socket from a previous H3 client in the same test
        # process.  For an explicit port the caller committed to that port
        # and we fail loudly instead of silently degrading to TCP-only (the
        # old behaviour would leave clients hitting QUIC-HANDSHAKE-TIMEOUT
        # with no indication why).
        bool random_port = (info.service == "0");
        bool needs_h3 = enable_h3 && info.cert && info.key && (info.enable_h3 ?? True);
        int max_attempts = (random_port && needs_h3) ? 10 : 1;

        string base_name = info.name ?? "listener";

        # Allocate the listener ID once, OUTSIDE the retry loop.  Each H3
        # random-port retry tears down the just-bound TCP listener and rebinds
        # on a fresh kernel-assigned port, but the logical listener identity
        # (id and name) does not change across attempts.  Burning a fresh
        # sequence number per attempt would let the final listener land at
        # id=N>0 when retries fire, breaking callers that look up the first
        # listener by its conventional name "listener-0".
        int id = seqListeners.next();
        info.name = base_name + sprintf("-%d", id);

        if (nmap{info.name}) {
            throw "HTTPSERVER-ADDLISTENER-ERROR",
                sprintf("there is already an active listener with name %y", info.name);
        }

        for (int attempt = 1; attempt <= max_attempts; ++attempt) {

            c.add();
            #printf("HttpServer::addListenerIntern() %y c=%y\n", sock, c.getCount());

            HttpListener l;
            try {
                l = getListener(self, id, info);
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                c.done();
                rethrow;
            }

            # Register listener in maps BEFORE adding to async I/O controller
            # This prevents a race where an accept could arrive before registration is complete
            listeners{id} = l;
            smap{sock} = id;
            nmap{info.name} = id;

            # rename listener if a wildcard service was provided
            string effective_sock = sock;
            if (info.service == "0") {
                int port = l.getInfo().port;
                effective_sock = sprintf("%s:%s", info.node ?? "*", port);
                smap{effective_sock} = remove smap{sock};
            }

            # Auto-create the paired QUIC listener and populate the per-listener
            # Alt-Svc entry BEFORE the TCP listener starts accepting connections.
            # If we did this after asyncIo.addListener(), there would be a race
            # window where a client could connect, send a request, and receive a
            # response that omits the Alt-Svc header (because listener_alt_svc
            # hasn't been populated yet).  This was observed intermittently in
            # HttpServerHttp3Integration.qtest::testHttpClientHttp3Disabled: a
            # fast client completed the handshake + request before the QUIC
            # setup + updateAltSvcHeader() sequence finished on the server
            # thread.
            bool h3_ok = True;
            *string h3_err;
            *string h3_desc;
            if (needs_h3) {
                try {
                    # Use the actual listener socket info to get the resolved address
                    # and family, since the original info may have AF_UNSPEC / no node
                    hash<auto> linfo = l.getInfo();
                    # When TCP bound to the IPv4 wildcard (0.0.0.0), bind the paired
                    # QUIC listener to "::" dual-stack instead.  The Alt-Svc header
                    # advertises "h3=\":<port>\"" which clients resolve against the
                    # original hostname; a host like "localhost" commonly resolves
                    # to ::1 first, so an IPv4-only QUIC listener is unreachable
                    # from the family the client picked and the handshake stalls
                    # ~30s until ngtcp2's idle timeout.  Linux IPv6 sockets accept
                    # IPv4 clients via v4-mapped addresses when bindv6only=0
                    # (the default), so "::" covers both families on one socket.
                    string q_node;
                    int q_family;
                    if (linfo.address == "0.0.0.0") {
                        q_node = "::";
                        q_family = AF_INET6;
                    } else {
                        q_node = linfo.address ?? info.node;
                        q_family = linfo.family;
                    }
                    hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> quic_opts = <HttpListenerOptionInfo>{
                        "cert": info.cert,
                        "key": info.key,
                        "node": q_node,
                        "family": q_family,
                        "alt_svc_max_age": info.alt_svc_max_age,
                        # the QUIC listener enforces the same body size limits as its TCP listener
                        "max_request_body_size": info.max_request_body_size,
                        "max_decoded_body_size": info.max_decoded_body_size,
                        # and the same bound of the data held for the clients of SSE streams
                        "sse_max_buffer_size": info.sse_max_buffer_size,
                    };
                    if (info.additional_info) {
                        quic_opts.additional_info = info.additional_info;
                    }
                    hash<auto> quic_info = addQuicListener(quic_opts, linfo.port);
                    quic_port_map{id} = quic_info.port;
                    quic_alt_svc_max_age{id} = quic_info.alt_svc_max_age;
                    quic_listener_map{id} = quic_info.id;
                    updateAltSvcHeader();
                    log("Auto-created QUIC/HTTP/3 listener on UDP port %d for TCP listener %d",
                        quic_info.port, id);
                } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                    h3_ok = False;
                    h3_err = ex.err;
                    h3_desc = ex.desc;
                }
            }

            if (!h3_ok) {
                if (random_port && attempt < max_attempts) {
                    # Retry with a fresh random port.  Tear down everything
                    # bound so far so the next iteration starts clean.  This
                    # is strictly better than silently degrading to TCP-only
                    # on a random-port listener, because the caller asked for
                    # enable_h3 and has no way to observe that it didn't take.
                    log("WARNING: attempt %d/%d: paired QUIC listener bind "
                        "failed on port %d (%s: %s); retrying with a fresh "
                        "random port", attempt, max_attempts,
                        l.getInfo().port, h3_err, h3_desc);
                    remove listeners{id};
                    remove smap{effective_sock};
                    remove nmap{info.name};
                    try { l.stop(); } catch () {}
                    # l.stop() drives listenerStopped() which already calls
                    # c.done(); only decrement here if l.stop() didn't get
                    # there (i.e. threw before reaching it).  Mirrors the
                    # WAITGROUP-ERROR defense in listenerStopped() at the
                    # top of this class.
                    try {
                        c.done();
                    } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                        if (ex.err != "WAITGROUP-ERROR") {
                            rethrow;
                        }
                    }
                    continue;
                }
                if (!random_port) {
                    # Explicit port — caller committed to this port; we can't
                    # pick a different one behind their back.  Roll back the
                    # TCP listener and surface a hard error so misconfiguration
                    # (port in use, no IPv6 support, etc.) is visible.
                    log("ERROR: failed to bind paired QUIC listener on "
                        "explicit port %s: %s: %s",
                        info.service, h3_err, h3_desc);
                    remove listeners{id};
                    remove smap{effective_sock};
                    remove nmap{info.name};
                    try { l.stop(); } catch () {}
                    # See the random_port retry branch above for rationale.
                    try {
                        c.done();
                    } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                        if (ex.err != "WAITGROUP-ERROR") {
                            rethrow;
                        }
                    }
                    throw "HTTPSERVER-H3-BIND-ERROR",
                        sprintf("failed to bind paired HTTP/3 UDP listener on "
                            "requested port %s: %s: %s",
                            info.service, h3_err, h3_desc);
                }
                # random_port && attempt == max_attempts: retries exhausted.
                # Keep the TCP listener running (we already told the caller we
                # have a bind) but log a clear warning that H3 is disabled.
                log("WARNING: exhausted %d QUIC-bind attempts for random-port "
                    "listener %d; continuing with TCP-only (no H3) on port %d: "
                    "last error: %s: %s",
                    max_attempts, id, l.getInfo().port, h3_err, h3_desc);
            }

            # the fast routes of the server, if any, are answered for the connections accepted from the listener, with
            # the listener's Alt-Svc header
            {
                fast_routes_lock.lock();
                on_exit fast_routes_lock.unlock();
                alt_svc_targets{string(l.getID())} = l;
            }
            applyAltSvcToFastRoutes(l);
            if (*HttpFastRoutes fast = getFastRoutes()) {
                l.setHttpFastRoutes(fast);
            }

            # Add the TCP listener to the async I/O controller — start accepting.
            # Done AFTER QUIC + Alt-Svc setup so the first TCP response already
            # carries the Alt-Svc header (see comment above).
            asyncIo.addListener(l, asyncTarget, l.isSsl(), l.getSslCertificateObject(),
                l.getSslPrivateKeyObject(), l.getHttpVersion(), l.getSslVerifyFlags(),
                l.getSslAcceptAllCerts(), l.getGetRemoteCerts(), l.getMaxRequestBodySize(), l);

            # Notify that the listener is now accepting connections.  With the
            # event-driven async I/O model there are no per-listener threads, so
            # this notification (formerly issued as the first action of the
            # listener's dedicated accept thread) is issued here instead, right
            # after the listener has been registered with the async I/O
            # controller.  addListenerIntern() runs in the async I/O controller
            # task whose result the caller awaits via Future::get(), so this call
            # happens-before the caller resumes, preserving the original ordering
            # relative to server startup (i.e. it is delivered before
            # HttpServer::startup() completes for system listeners).  Wrapped so a
            # notification handler error cannot tear down a successfully-started
            # listener.
            try {
                listenerStarted(id, l.getListenerSocketInfo());
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                logError("error in listenerStarted() notification for listener %d (%s): %s: %s", id,
                    l.getListenerSocketInfo().desc, ex.err, ex.desc);
            }

            return l;
        }

        throw "HTTPSERVER-ADDLISTENER-ERROR",
            sprintf("internal error: retry loop exhausted without a decision");
    }

    private Future addListenerInternAsync(hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> info) {
        Promise p();
        Future f = p.getFuture();
        try {
            getGlobalAsyncCtrl().submitTask(sub () {
                try {
                    p.set(addListenerIntern(info).getInfo());
                } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                    p.setError(ex.err, ex.desc, ex.arg);
                }
            });
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            p.setError(ex.err, ex.desc, ex.arg);
        }
        return f;
    }

    private Future resolveINETListenerAddressesAsync(hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> info) {
        if (info.node || info.family != AF_UNSPEC) {
            return getaddrinfo_async(info.node, info.service, info.family, AIFlags);
        }

        Future inet6_f = getaddrinfo_async(NOTHING, info.service, AF_INET6, AIFlags);
        Future inet_f = getaddrinfo_async(NOTHING, info.service, AF_INET, AIFlags);
        Promise p();
        Future f = p.getFuture();

        try {
            getGlobalAsyncCtrl().submitTask(sub () {
                try {
                    list<auto> al;
                    *hash<ExceptionInfo> first_ex;
                    try {
                        al += inet6_f.get();
                    } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                        first_ex = ex;
                    }
                    try {
                        al += inet_f.get();
                    } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                        if (!first_ex) {
                            first_ex = ex;
                        }
                    }
                    if (!al.size()) {
                        if (!first_ex) {
                            throw "QOREADDRINFO-GETINFO-ERROR",
                                sprintf("async address resolution for wildcard service %y returned no addresses",
                                    info.service);
                        }
                        throw first_ex.err, first_ex.desc, first_ex.arg;
                    }

                    p.set(al);
                } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                    p.setError(ex.err, ex.desc, ex.arg);
                }
            });
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            p.setError(ex.err, ex.desc, ex.arg);
        }

        return f;
    }

    private list<hash<auto>> addINETListenersInternResolved(hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> info, list<auto> al,
            *reference<hash<string, string>> errmap) {
        # sort ipv6 addresses first in list
        al = sort(al, int sub (hash<auto> l, hash<auto> r) {
            return l.family === AF_INET6 ? -1 : 0;
        });

        list<hash<auto>> rv = ();
        foreach hash<auto> h in (al) {
            try {
                hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> new_info = info;
                new_info += {
                    "node": h.address,
                    "service": h.port,
                };
                rv += addListenerIntern(new_info).getInfo();
%ifdef Windows
                if (!info.node && (info.family != AF_UNSPEC)) {
                    break;
                }
%else
                # exit after the first listener is bound if the node address is missing (= bind on all addresses)
                if (!info.node) {
                    break;
                }
%endif
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                string errstr = sprintf("%s:%s: %s: %s: %s", h.address_desc, h.port, get_ex_pos(ex), ex.err, ex.desc);
                string bind;
                if (exists info.node) {
                    if (exists info.service) {
                        bind = sprintf("%s:%s", info.node, info.service);
                    } else {
                        bind = info.node;
                    }
                } else {
                    bind = info.service;
                }
                errmap{bind ?* "unknown"} = errstr;
                if (ex.err != "HTTPLISTENER-BIND-ERROR" && ex.err != "SOCKET-BIND-ERROR") {
                    logError("add listener %s", errstr);
                    rethrow;
                }

                log("cannot add listener on %s", errstr);
            }
        }

        return rv;
    }

    private Future addINETListenersInternAsync(hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> info,
            *reference<hash<string, string>> errmap) {
        Promise p();
        Future f = p.getFuture();
        try {
            Future addr_f = resolveINETListenerAddressesAsync(info);
            getGlobalAsyncCtrl().submitTask(sub () {
                try {
                    p.set(addINETListenersInternResolved(info, addr_f.get(), \errmap));
                } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                    p.setError(ex.err, ex.desc, ex.arg);
                }
            });
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            p.setError(ex.err, ex.desc, ex.arg);
        }
        return f;
    }

    private Future addListenerInfoListAsync(hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> info) {
        Promise p();
        Future f = p.getFuture();
        try {
            getGlobalAsyncCtrl().submitTask(sub () {
                try {
                    p.set(list(addListenerIntern(info).getInfo()));
                } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                    p.setError(ex.err, ex.desc, ex.arg);
                }
            });
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            p.setError(ex.err, ex.desc, ex.arg);
        }
        return f;
    }

    private Future addListenersInternAsync(string bind, hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> info,
            *reference<hash<string, string>> errmap) {
        if (bind !~ /:\w+/) {
            if (bind =~ /^\//) {
                info.node = bind;
                remove info.service;
                return addListenerInfoListAsync(info);
            }

            # assume service only
            remove info.node;
            info.service = bind;
            return addINETListenersInternAsync(info, \errmap);
        }

        # add node and service
        (info.node, info.service) = (bind =~ x/(.+):(\w+)/);
        return addINETListenersInternAsync(info, \errmap);
    }

    private Future addListenersInternAsync(hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> info,
            *reference<hash<string, string>> errmap) {
        if (exists info.node && !exists info.service) {
            # try to bind without a service, can be a UNIX domain socket
            return addListenerInfoListAsync(info);
        }

        return addINETListenersInternAsync(info, \errmap);
    }

    private hash<HttpResponseInfo> noHandlerError(hash<auto> cx, hash<auto> hdr, auto body) {
        string str = "";
        if (hdr.path)
            str = sprintf("url=%y", hdr.path);
        else
            str = "<no URL>";

        if (hdr."content-type")
            str += sprintf(", content-type=%y", hdr."content-type");
        else
            str += ", <no content-type>";

        if (isLogInfoEnabled()) {
            log("no handler for %s (from %s) hdr: %y", str, cx."peer-info".address_desc, http_mask_value(hdr));
        }
        return new hash<HttpResponseInfo>({
            "code" : 501,
            "body" : sprintf("no handler has been registered for %s", str),
        });
    }

    #! Sends an error response for a request of handleRequest() and records that a response was sent
    /** sendHttpError() takes the request context by value, so the context of handleRequest() is marked here; otherwise
        the request ends as one whose handler sent no response, and a second response (500) followed the error on the
        same connection
    */
    private:internal sendRequestError(HttpListener listener, reference<hash<auto>> cx, Socket s, int status_code,
            *data msg, *InputStream chunked_msg, *hash<auto> extra_hdrs, *string encoding, bool head) {
        cx."response-sent" = True;
        sendHttpError(listener, cx, s, status_code, msg, chunked_msg, extra_hdrs, encoding, head);
    }

    #! @cond nodoc
    #! Reads the body of a request for handleRequest()
    /** @return a hash with the body in its \c body key, or @ref nothing if the request has been answered (an error
        response was sent or captured)
    */
    private *hash<auto> getRequestBody(HttpListener listener, Socket s, reference<hash<auto>> cx, hash<auto> hdr,
            *HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler handler, int max_body, bool is_multiplexed, bool want_streaming,
            hash<auto> info, *reference<hash<HttpAsyncResponseInfo>> async_response_out) {
        *data body;
        if (cx."header-info".body_streaming && !want_streaming) {
            # Handler doesn't support streaming — buffer body on handler thread
            # for backward compatibility (body was previously read by I/O thread)
            #
            # Resume the h2/h3 poll operation on the I/O thread when this block exits
            # (normal flow, early return on error, or exception).  This ensures the
            # connection stays alive (PING, SETTINGS, WINDOW_UPDATE) while the handler
            # processes the request — critical for long-running handlers like bulk
            # object creation.  The controller also has a safety-net resume after
            # handler return to cover wantsBodyStreaming() handlers that skip this block.
            on_exit {
                if (cx."header-info".resume_body_streaming) {
                    cx."header-info".resume_body_streaming();
                }
            }
            int sid = cx."header-info".stream_id;
            binary body_data = binary();
            # set when the body read stops because the body exceeds the maximum size
            bool hit_max_body = False;
            if (cx."header-info".http3) {
                # HTTP/3: use QUIC stream data methods (CV-based; I/O thread stays active)
                int session_id = cx."header-info".session_id;
                try {
                    while (True) {
                        *binary chunk = s.readQuicStreamDataBlock(session_id, sid, 30s);
                        if (!chunk) {
                            break;
                        }
                        body_data += chunk;
                        # Incremental size check: reject early instead of buffering the full body
                        if (max_body > 0 && body_data.size() > max_body) {
                            break;
                        }
                    }
                } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                    if (ex.err == "QUIC-STREAM-TIMEOUT") {
                        listener.logError("HTTP/3 request body read timeout from %s",
                            cx."peer-info".address_desc);
                        cx.close = True;
                        if (async_response_out) {
                            async_response_out = buildAsyncErrorResponse(408,
                                "Request Timeout", cx, listener, s, !is_multiplexed);
                        } else {
                            sendRequestError(listener, \cx, s, 408, "Request Timeout",
                                NOTHING, NOTHING, cx."response-encoding",
                                hdr.method == "HEAD");
                        }
                        return NOTHING;
                    }
                    if (ex.err != "HTTP-BODY-TOO-LARGE") {
                        rethrow;
                    }
                    # the I/O layer stopped reading a body that exceeds the limit; the bytes it buffered
                    # until then were delivered first
                    hit_max_body = True;
                }
                # trailers sent after the body
                if (*hash<auto> trailers = s.getQuicStreamTrailers(session_id, sid)) {
                    cx.trailers = trailers;
                }
                # NOTE: cleanupQuicStream is called by the controller's on_exit handler
            } else {
                # HTTP/2: drive inbound DATA frames through register_body_queue.
                # The I/O thread drains frames into the Queue (NOTHING sentinel
                # = END_STREAM) so the handler thread never touches the H2
                # session lock.
                *code<nothing(Queue)> register_body_queue
                    = cx."header-info".register_body_queue;
                if (!register_body_queue) {
                    throw "HTTP2-BODY-STREAMING-ERROR",
                        sprintf("body_streaming dispatch missing register_body_queue "
                            "closure for stream %d", sid);
                }
                Queue body_queue(-1);
                register_body_queue(body_queue);

                bool h2_complete = False;
                while (True) {
                    auto chunk;
                    try {
                        chunk = body_queue.get(30s);
                    } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                        if (ex.err == "QUEUE-TIMEOUT") {
                            break;
                        }
                        rethrow;
                    }
                    if (chunk.typeCode() == NT_HASH) {
                        if (chunk.err != "HTTP-BODY-TOO-LARGE") {
                            # the body ended before END_STREAM (HTTP-BODY-INCOMPLETE): the handler must not
                            # get the part received for the complete body
                            throw chunk.err, chunk.desc;
                        }
                        # the I/O layer stopped buffering a body that exceeds the limit; the bytes it
                        # buffered until then were delivered first, and the rest of the body is discarded
                        hit_max_body = True;
                        break;
                    }
                    if (!(chunk instanceof binary)) {
                        # NOTHING sentinel → END_STREAM observed by I/O thread
                        h2_complete = True;
                        break;
                    }
                    body_data += chunk;
                    # Incremental size check: reject early instead of buffering the full body
                    if (max_body > 0 && body_data.size() > max_body) {
                        hit_max_body = True;
                        break;
                    }
                }
                # trailers sent after the body
                if (h2_complete) {
                    if (*hash<auto> trailers = s.getHttp2StreamTrailers(sid)) {
                        cx.trailers = trailers;
                    }
                }
                if (!h2_complete && !hit_max_body) {
                    listener.logError("HTTP/2 request body read timeout from %s",
                        cx."peer-info".address_desc);
                    cx.close = True;
                    if (async_response_out) {
                        async_response_out = buildAsyncErrorResponse(408,
                            "Request Timeout", cx, listener, s, !is_multiplexed);
                    } else {
                        sendRequestError(listener, \cx, s, 408, "Request Timeout",
                            NOTHING, NOTHING, cx."response-encoding",
                            hdr.method == "HEAD");
                    }
                    return NOTHING;
                }
                # Defensive consistency check: if Content-Length advertised a
                # non-zero body but END_STREAM arrived with no DATA chunks
                # delivered, the request body was lost in transit
                # (intermittent multiplexed-dispatch race observed on Alpine
                # ARM64 CI).  Fail the request explicitly with 502 instead
                # of silently dispatching the handler with an empty body —
                # which would otherwise be auto-converted to 204 No Content
                # and corrupt application semantics for echo/upload paths.
                if (h2_complete && !body_data.size() && hdr."content-length"
                        && int(hdr."content-length") > 0) {
                    string etxt = sprintf(
                        "HTTP/2 request body delivery race: Content-Length "
                        "%y advertised but no DATA chunks received before "
                        "END_STREAM (stream=%d)",
                        hdr."content-length", sid);
                    listener.logError("%s: from %s", etxt,
                        cx."peer-info".address_desc);
                    cx.close = True;
                    if (async_response_out) {
                        async_response_out = buildAsyncErrorResponse(502, etxt,
                            cx, listener, s, !is_multiplexed);
                    } else {
                        sendRequestError(listener, \cx, s, 502, etxt, NOTHING,
                            NOTHING, cx."response-encoding",
                            hdr.method == "HEAD");
                    }
                    return NOTHING;
                }
            }
            # Apply max body size check
            if (hit_max_body || (max_body > 0 && body_data.size() > max_body)) {
                string etxt = sprintf("%s request body size %d exceeds maximum allowed body size %d",
                    cx."header-info".http3 ? "HTTP/3" : "HTTP/2",
                    body_data.size(), max_body);
                listener.logError("%s: from %s", etxt, cx."peer-info".address_desc);
                cx.close = True;
                if (async_response_out) {
                    async_response_out = buildAsyncErrorResponse(413, etxt, cx,
                        listener, s, !is_multiplexed);
                } else {
                    sendRequestError(listener, \cx, s, 413, etxt, NOTHING, NOTHING,
                        cx."response-encoding", hdr.method == "HEAD");
                }
                return NOTHING;
            }
            if (body_data.size()) {
                # a multipart container's octets are not text in the request charset: each part
                # carries its own encoding, so the container is handed over as bytes
                if (hdr."content-encoding" || hdr."content-type" == MimeTypeOctetStream
                        || hdr."content-type" =~ /^application\/connect\+/
                        || hdr."content-type" =~ /^multipart\//i) {
                    body = body_data;
                } else {
                    body = decodeRequestText(hdr, body_data);
                }
            }
        } else if (cx."header-info".body) {
            # Body pre-read by I/O controller (H1 async, H2, or H3)
            # Check body size against limit (body was already read by the
            # async I/O layer; this is a secondary check)
            if (max_body > 0 && cx."header-info".body.size() > max_body) {
                string proto = cx."header-info".http3 ? "HTTP/3"
                    : (cx."header-info".http2 ? "HTTP/2" : "HTTP/1.x");
                string etxt = sprintf("%s request body size %d exceeds maximum allowed body size %d",
                    proto, cx."header-info".body.size(), max_body);
                listener.logError("%s: from %s", etxt, cx."peer-info".address_desc);
                cx.close = True;
                sendRequestError(listener, \cx, s, 413, etxt, NOTHING, NOTHING,
                    cx."response-encoding", hdr.method == "HEAD");
                return NOTHING;
            }
            # Body is always binary from the I/O layer — convert to string if text content type;
            # a multipart container stays binary, as its parts carry their own encodings
            if (hdr."content-encoding" || hdr."content-type" == MimeTypeOctetStream
                    || hdr."content-type" =~ /^application\/connect\+/
                    || hdr."content-type" =~ /^multipart\//i) {
                body = cx."header-info".body;
            } else {
                # the buffered bytes are decoded by the rules shared by all HTTP clients and servers
                body = decodeRequestText(hdr, cx."header-info".body);
            }
        } else if (exists hdr."content-length" && !is_multiplexed) {
            # HTTP/1.x only: the request body is read from the raw socket here.
            #
            # HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 bodies never arrive as raw socket bytes; they are
            # delivered as DATA frames by the multiplexed session and surface either
            # in cx."header-info".body (pre-read by the I/O controller) or through
            # the body_streaming branch above, where the handler reads the stream
            # itself.  Reaching a synchronous socket read on a multiplexed connection
            # would raise SOCKET-NON-BLOCK-ERROR against the async I/O controller
            # that still owns the socket, turning a valid request into a 400.
            # the length is the digits of the header and nothing else: a value that another parser would read
            # differently (a sign, hexadecimal, an exponent, a list) is refused, as is one that a general
            # conversion would make a length of 0 or less, for which the read below would wait for the connection
            # to close (RFC 9110 section 8.6)
            *int content_length = http_parse_content_length(hdr."content-length");
            if (!exists content_length) {
                string etxt = sprintf("invalid Content-Length %y", hdr."content-length");
                listener.logError("%s: from %s", etxt, cx."peer-info".address_desc);
                cx.close = True;
                sendRequestError(listener, \cx, s, 400, etxt, NOTHING, NOTHING, cx."response-encoding",
                    hdr.method == "HEAD");
                return NOTHING;
            }
            # Pre-read Content-Length check against max body size limit
            if (max_body > 0 && content_length > max_body) {
                string etxt = sprintf("Content-Length %d exceeds maximum allowed body size %d",
                    content_length, max_body);
                listener.logError("%s: from %s", etxt, cx."peer-info".address_desc);
                cx.close = True;
                sendRequestError(listener, \cx, s, 413, etxt, NOTHING, NOTHING,
                    cx."response-encoding", hdr.method == "HEAD");
                return NOTHING;
            }
            # HTTP/1.x: read body from socket; a length of 0 is no body
            try {
                if (!content_length) {
                    # nothing to read
                # a multipart container stays binary here too, so that a direct read and a
                # buffered one hand the handler the same bytes
                } else if (hdr."content-encoding" || hdr."content-type" == MimeTypeOctetStream
                        || hdr."content-type" =~ /^multipart\//i) {
                    body = s.recvBinary(content_length, HttpServer::ReadTimeout);
                } else {
                    # text is decoded by the rules shared by all HTTP clients and servers, as it is when the
                    # body was buffered
                    body = decodeRequestText(hdr, s.recvBinary(content_length, HttpServer::ReadTimeout));
                }
                #printf("HTTP DEBUG: %s\n", body);
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                string etxt = sprintf("error reading body in %s (Content-Length: %d): %s: %s", hdr.method,
                    content_length, ex.err, ex.desc);
                string str = sprintf("%s: received from %s via %s (header: %y)", etxt,
                    cx."peer-info".address_desc, cx."socket-info".address_desc, hdr);
                listener.logError("%s", str);
                cx.close = True;
                sendRequestError(listener, \cx, s, 400, etxt, NOTHING, NOTHING, cx."response-encoding",
                    hdr.method == "HEAD");
                return NOTHING;
            }
        }
        return {"body": body};
    }

    #! Handles incoming requests
    /** This method is called by listeners when a request is received.

        If async_response_out is provided, captures response info instead of sending
    */
    private handleRequest(RequestHelper request_helper, HttpListener listener, Socket s,
            reference<hash<auto>> cx, hash<auto> hdr, bool head = False, HttpPersistentHandlerInfo phi,
            hash<auto> info, *reference<hash<HttpAsyncResponseInfo>> async_response_out) {
        remove cx."response-sent";
        bool is_http2 = cx."header-info".http2 ?? False;
        bool is_multiplexed = is_http2 || (cx."header-info".http3 ?? False);
        # Check for HTTP/2 connection preface (RFC 7540 Section 3.5)
        # The preface is "PRI * HTTP/2.0\r\n\r\nSM\r\n\r\n"
        # HTTP parser separates this into method="PRI", path="*", protocol="HTTP/2.0" or "2.0"
        if (hdr.method == "PRI") {
            string err;
            # Check if this is an HTTP/2 preface: path="*" and version starts with "2" or "HTTP/2"
            if (hdr.path == "*" && (hdr.http_version =~ /^2\./ || hdr.http_version =~ /^HTTP\/2\./)) {
                err = "HTTP/2 cleartext (h2c) direct connection preface is not supported; "
                    "use HTTP/2 over TLS with ALPN negotiation or HTTP/1.1 Upgrade";
                listener.logError("HTTP/2 cleartext connection preface received from %s - not supported; "
                    "client should use HTTPS with ALPN or HTTP/1.1 Upgrade", info.address_desc);
            } else {
                err = sprintf("unknown / unsupported HTTP method %n", hdr.method);
                listener.logError("%s: received from %s via %s", err, info.address_desc,
                    listener.getListenerSocketInfo().address_desc);
            }
            cx.close = True;
            # Mark response as sent before calling sendHttpError to prevent double response
            cx."response-sent" = True;
            sendRequestError(listener, \cx, s, 501, err, NOTHING, NOTHING, cx."response-encoding", head);
            return;
        }

        if (!http_methods{hdr.method}) {
            string err = sprintf("unknown / unsupported HTTP method %n", hdr.method);
            string str = sprintf("%s: received from %s via %s", err, info.address_desc,
                listener.getListenerSocketInfo().address_desc);
            listener.logError("%s", str);
            cx.close = True;
            sendRequestError(listener, \cx, s, 501, err, NOTHING, NOTHING, cx."response-encoding", head);
            return;
        }

        # the decoded resource path selects the handler by key; an escaped NUL character (%00) decodes to a path that
        # names no resource and cannot be a key, so the request is malformed; it is answered before any handler is
        # selected, and an HTTP/1.x connection is closed, as the request body has not been read
        if (exists hdr.path && hdr.path.contains(chr(0))) {
            string err = "the request path contains an escaped NUL character";
            listener.logError("%s: received from %s via %s", err, info.address_desc,
                listener.getListenerSocketInfo().address_desc);
            if (async_response_out) {
                async_response_out = buildAsyncErrorResponse(400, err, cx, listener, s, !is_multiplexed);
            } else {
                cx.close = True;
                sendRequestError(listener, \cx, s, 400, err, NOTHING, NOTHING, cx."response-encoding", head);
            }
            return;
        }

        # Protocol header parsers expose encoding names but discard their q-values. Select
        # once from the original header so HTTP/1, HTTP/2, and HTTP/3 all honor exclusions.
        hash<string, float> preferences = getContentEncodingPreferences(hdr."accept-encoding");
        cx."identity-acceptable" = (preferences.identity ?? 1.0) > 0.0;
        hash<string, bool> acceptable;
        foreach string encoding in (keys preferences) {
            if (preferences{encoding} <= 0.0) {
                continue;
            }
            if (encoding == "identity") {
                break;
            }
            if (!cx.encoding) {
                cx.encoding = encoding;
            }
            acceptable{encoding} = True;
        }
        cx."header-info"."accept-encoding" = acceptable;
        # the weight of each acceptable encoding, and the levels for a handler that compresses its own stream
        cx."header-info"."accept-encoding-weights" = map {$1: preferences{$1}}, keys acceptable;
        cx."compression-levels" = compression_levels;

        # the effective maximum request body size of the listener, read once per request so a change made with
        # setMaxRequestBodySize() applies to the next request; handlers that read the body as a stream enforce the
        # total themselves with this value
        int max_body = listener.getMaxRequestBodySize();
        cx."header-info".max_request_body_size = max_body;

        # erase the encoding string on exit
        on_exit remove cx.encoding;

        hash<auto> url += {
            "path": hdr.path,
        };

        # add logging functions and url
        cx += {
            "logger": logger,
            "url": url,
            "debug": debug,
        };

        # clear any context info set during handling the request so it does not leak into other requests
        on_exit try {
            clearContextInfo();
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            # log the error
            string desc = "error clearing request context data: " + !debug
                ? sprintf("%s: %s: %s", get_ex_pos(ex), ex.err, ex.desc)
                : get_exception_string(ex);
            desc += sprintf(": received from %s", cx."peer-info".address_desc);
            listener.logError("%s", desc);
        }

        HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler handler;
        # the dynamic handler helper maintains the request count for dynamic handlers so any call to remove the
        # handler will only return after all in-progress requests are completed; the object is only allocated if
        # needed
        DynamicHandlerHelper dhh;

        if (phi.handler) {
            handler = phi.handler;
        } else {
            # find a handler for the request
            *HandlerInfo hi;
            int score = 0;
            string root_path;
            if (listener.hasHandlers()) {
                hi = listener.findHandler(hdr, \score, True, \root_path);
                if (!hi) {
                    if (listener.defaultHandler) {
                        hi = listener.defaultHandler;
                    } else if (async_response_out) {
                        # Capture the 404 response for the async I/O controller to send
                        async_response_out = buildAsyncErrorResponse(404, "Not found", cx,
                            listener, s, !is_multiplexed);
                        return;
                    } else {
                        sendRequestError(listener, \cx, s, 404, "Not found", NOTHING, NOTHING,
                            cx."response-encoding", head);
                        return;
                    }
                }
            } else {
                hi = handlers.findHandler(hdr, \score, False, \root_path);
                if (score < 3) {
                    *HandlerInfo dhi = dhandlers.findHandler(hdr, \score, \dhh, \root_path);
                    if (dhi)
                        hi = dhi;
                }
            }

            if (hi) {
                handler = hi.obj;
                cx.handler_name = hi.name;
                if (root_path)
                    cx.root_path = root_path;
            }
        }

        try {
            if (!handler && defaultHandler) {
                handler = defaultHandler.obj;
                cx.handler_name = defaultHandler.name;
            }

            if (handler && hdr.expect) {
                if (hdr.expect == "100-continue") {
                    hash<HttpResponseInfo> resp = handler.handleExpectHeader(cx, hdr);
                    # 100-continue is an intermediate response that must be sent directly on the
                    # socket before the client sends the body.  Do NOT route through sendReply()
                    # or doResponse() because those capture to async_response_out, which would
                    # replace the eventual main response and never reach the client.
                    if (resp."code" == 100) {
                        if (cx."header-info".http2) {
                            # HTTP/2: no 100-continue mechanism; the body is already being sent
                            # by the client on the stream — nothing to do
                        } else {
                            # HTTP/1.x: handler thread owns socket exclusively (removed
                            # from I/O controller at header dispatch). sendIntern handles
                            # EAGAIN on non-blocking sockets. Fully async 100-Continue
                            # requires the I/O controller to handle body reading for H1.
                            s.send("HTTP/1.1 100 Continue\r\n\r\n");
                        }
                    } else {
                        # Handler rejected the request (non-100 response)
                        sendReply(request_helper, listener, s, handler, resp, \cx, hdr, head,
                            \async_response_out);
                        return;
                    }
                } else {
                    string etxt = sprintf("unrecognized 'Expect' header: %y", hdr.expect);
                    string str = sprintf("%s: received from %s via %s (header: %y)", etxt,
                        cx."peer-info".address_desc, cx."socket-info".address_desc, hdr);
                    listener.logError("%s", str);
                    cx.close = True;
                    sendRequestError(listener, \cx, s, 400, etxt, NOTHING, NOTHING, cx."response-encoding",
                        hdr.method == "HEAD");
                    return;
                }
            }

            # Set body size limits on socket before any body reads
            s.setMaxChunkedBodySize(max_body);
            s.setHttp2MaxRequestBodySize(max_body);

            # Check if handler wants body streaming (bidi) for HTTP/2 or HTTP/3
            bool want_streaming = cx."header-info".body_streaming
                && handler
                && handler.wantsBodyStreaming(cx, hdr);

            # the request body, read in a method of its own so that its locals and early returns are not part of the
            # frame of this method, which every request runs (qore#5759); NOTHING if the request was answered there
            *hash<auto> body_info = getRequestBody(listener, s, \cx, hdr, handler, max_body, is_multiplexed,
                want_streaming, info, \async_response_out);
            if (!body_info) {
                return;
            }
            *data body = body_info.body;

            if (request_helper.log_info) {
                listener.doLogRecvBody(body, cx, hdr, info);
            }

            hash<HttpResponseInfo> rv;

            #printf("HTTP DEBUG: B: handler: %y strict-bool-eval: %y\n", cx.handler_name, get_parse_options() & PO_STRICT_BOOLEAN_EVAL);
            #printf("HTTP DEBUG: B: handler: %y: context: %y, hdr: %y, body: %y\n", cx.handler_name, cx, hdr, body);
            #printf("HTTP DEBUG: BEFORE handler=%s", dbg_node_info(handlers.(cx.handler_name)));

            #printf("HTTP DEBUG: A: cid: %d handler: %y auth: %y\n", cx.id, cx.handler_name, boolean(handler.auth));

            if (!handler) {
                cx.handler_name = "error";
                rv = noHandlerError(cx, hdr, body);
            } else {
                # check for authentication info
                #printf("HTTP DEBUG: handler: %y (auth: %y) hdr: %y\n", cx.handler_name,
                #    exists handler.auth && handler.auth.requiresAuthentication(), hdr);

                # allow the handler to remove any authentication information in thread-local data, for example
                on_exit if (handler.auth) {
                    handler.auth.endRequest();
                }

                *hash<auto> tld_save = handler.saveThreadLocalData();
                on_exit handler.restoreThreadLocalData(tld_save);

                # preform any pre-processing on the request that can aid in authentication and has to be performed to
                # handle the request anyway
                *hash<HttpResponseInfo> ah;
                try {
                    ah = handler.preprocessRequest(listener, \hdr, \cx);
                } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                    # log the error
                    string desc = sprintf("Handler %y preprocessing error from %s: ", cx.handler_name,
                        cx."peer-info".address_desc) + (
                            !debug
                                ? sprintf("%s: %s: %s", cx.handler_name, get_ex_pos(ex), ex.err, ex.desc)
                                : get_exception_string(ex)
                        );
                    listener.logError("%s", desc);
                    ah = <HttpResponseInfo>{
                        "code": 501,
                        "body": "Server Preprocessing Error",
                    };
                }

                if (!ah) {
                    try {
                        ah = handler.authenticateRequest(listener, hdr, \cx);
                    } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                        # log the error
                        string desc = sprintf("Handler %y authentication error from %s: ", cx.handler_name,
                            cx."peer-info".address_desc) + (
                                !debug
                                    ? sprintf("%s: %s: %s", cx.handler_name, get_ex_pos(ex), ex.err, ex.desc)
                                    : get_exception_string(ex)
                            );
                        listener.logError("%s", desc);
                        ah = <HttpResponseInfo>{
                            "code": 501,
                            "body": "Server Authentication Error",
                        };
                    }
                }

                # return an error if authentication failed
                if (ah) {
                    if (!ah."code") {
                        ah."code" = 401;
                        cx.close = True;
                    } else if ((ah."code" / 100) >= 4) {
                        cx.close = True;
                    }
                    if (!ah.body.val() && !ah.hasKeyValue("chunked_body")) {
                        ah.body = "Authentication is required to access this server";
                    }
                    rv = ah;
                } else {
                    # decode body if applicable; a decoded body that is too large raises HTTP-BODY-TOO-LARGE,
                    # which is answered with 413
                    if (body && hdr."content-encoding" && handler.decompress) {
                        body = AbstractHttpRequestHandler::decodeRequestBody(hdr."content-encoding", body,
                            handler.decompress_to_string ? s.getEncoding() : NOTHING,
                            handler.getMaxDecodedBodySize(listener));
                    }

                    hash<auto!> hrv = handler.handleRequest(listener, s, cx, hdr, body);
                    if (hrv.hasKey("raw_response")) {
                        delete hrv."raw_response";
                    }
                    rv = cast<hash<HttpResponseInfo>>(hrv);

                    # manage persistent handler info, if applicable
                    if (!phi.handler) {
                        if (handler.isPersistent()) {
                            phi.assign(dhh, handler);
                            if (!cx.persistent && !rv.close) {
                                cx.persistent = True;
                            }
                        }
                    } else if (!handler.isPersistent()) {
                        phi.clear();
                    } else if (!cx.persistent && !rv.close) {
                        cx.persistent = True;
                    }
                }
            }

            # An opt-in interactive bidirectional streaming handler
            # (AbstractStreamRequest::wantsInteractiveStreaming()) drives an interleaved
            # read/reply loop on its dedicated thread and sends the full response itself
            # (early 200 headers + DATA frames + END_STREAM) via the stream-context/wakeSocket
            # model.  It signals completion with reply_sent; mark response-sent so the block
            # below tells the controller to leave the now-half-closed stream alone.
            if (want_streaming && rv.reply_sent
                    && (cx."header-info".http2 || cx."header-info".http3)) {
                cx."response-sent" = True;
            }

            # Handle HTTP/2 bidirectional streaming: the handler managed the stream
            # lifecycle directly (read body data, sent response).  Mark as dedicated
            # so the controller leaves the stream alone.
            if (want_streaming && cx."response-sent") {
                request_helper.setDedicatedExternal();
                if (async_response_out) {
                    delete async_response_out;
                }
                return;
            }

            if (rv.reply_sent) {
                # the handler sent the response itself: only an HTTP/1.x response has then been written on this
                # thread; the transmission of an HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 response cannot be observed
                if (rv.after_send) {
                    ResponseAfterSendNotifier after_send(remove rv.after_send, cx, handler, listener);
                    after_send.notify(!is_multiplexed && rv."code" >= 200);
                }
                if (is_multiplexed && async_response_out) {
                    if (rv.streaming) {
                        # Streaming handler already sent response directly via protocol-specific
                        # APIs (e.g., HTTP/3 QUIC submitQuicResponseStreaming + submitQuicStreamData
                        # for SSE).  Signal the controller to acknowledge and continue.
                        async_response_out = <HttpAsyncResponseInfo>{
                            "streaming": True,
                            "stream_type": rv.stream_type,
                        };
                    } else {
                        # Async multiplexed mode: the handler claimed reply_sent but didn't actually
                        # send a response; generate a 500 error for the controller to send
                        string err = sprintf("handler %y set reply_sent without sending a response",
                            cx.handler_name);
                        logError("%s", err);
                        async_response_out = buildAsyncErrorResponse(500, err, cx, listener, s, False);
                    }
                } else if (!is_multiplexed && rv."code" >= 200) {
                    cx."response-sent" = True;
                }
                return;
            }

            # Re-establish handler-specific thread-local data around sendReply so that
            # any deferred handler dispatch performed from inside sendReply (SSE setup
            # via setupAsyncSseConnection*(), WebSocket 101 registered()/startImpl(),
            # etc.) runs in the handler's TLD context — identical to handler.handleRequest()
            # earlier in this method.  Without this, handler methods invoked from
            # sendReply run with TLD already restored (e.g. tld.svc == NOTHING for
            # service-bound handlers), which breaks ServiceApi::startThread(),
            # service-log routing, and any other service-context API called from the
            # setup path.
            if (handler) {
                *hash<auto> tld_save = handler.saveThreadLocalData();
                on_exit handler.restoreThreadLocalData(tld_save);
                sendReply(request_helper, listener, s, handler, rv, \cx, hdr, head, \async_response_out);
            } else {
                sendReply(request_helper, listener, s, handler, rv, \cx, hdr, head, \async_response_out);
            }
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            string desc = !debug
                ? sprintf("%s: %s: %s", get_ex_pos(ex), ex.err, ex.desc)
                : get_exception_string(ex);

            string str = sprintf("handler: %s: %s", cx.handler_name, desc);

            if (ex.err == "ENCODING-CONVERSION-ERROR") {
                s.setEncoding("utf8");
                cx."response-encoding" = "utf8";
            }

            cx.close = True;
            remove cx.persistent;

            # Clean up leaked persistent state when the handler became persistent
            # during a failed request.  handler.setPersistent() is called in
            # RestStreamRequest::constructor() BEFORE stream data is read, so if
            # stream I/O fails (e.g., HTTP2-STREAM-TIMEOUT), the handler is
            # persistent but phi.handler was never set (phi.assign() at line 2806
            # is after handler.handleRequest()).  Without this cleanup, the
            # SqlUtilPersistentDataHelper and its DB transaction leak in
            # thread-local data and are never rolled back.
            if (handler && handler.isPersistent() && !phi.handler) {
                phi.assign(dhh, handler);
                phi.clear();
            }

            # if there is a pending chunked body that has not yet been read on exit, then read it and discard before sending any response
            on_exit if (s.pendingHttpChunkedBody())
                s.readHTTPChunkedBodyBinary(HttpServer::ReadTimeout);

            int err_code = ex.err == "HTTP-BODY-TOO-LARGE" ? 413 : 500;
            if (async_response_out) {
                # Capture the error response for the async I/O controller to send
                # Sending synchronously from the handler thread races with the
                # I/O controller on multiplexed (HTTP/2, HTTP/3) connections
                async_response_out = buildAsyncErrorResponse(err_code, str, cx, listener, s, !is_multiplexed);
            } else {
                sendRequestError(listener, \cx, s, err_code, str, NOTHING, NOTHING, cx."response-encoding", head);
            }
            logError("%s", str);
        }
    }
    #! @endcond

    # Builds an async error response for the controller to send
    # Builds an async error response to avoid synchronous sends that race with the I/O controller
    hash<HttpAsyncResponseInfo> buildAsyncErrorResponse(int status_code, string msg,
            hash<auto> cx, HttpListener listener, Socket s, bool close) {
        *string encoding = cx."response-encoding";
        if (!encoding) {
            encoding = s.getEncoding().lwr();
        }
        string err_body = acceptsHtml(cx) ? getHtmlErrorMessage(status_code, msg) : msg;
        hash<auto> err_hdr = getResponseHeaders(cx."listener-id") + {
            "Content-Type": MimeTypeHtml + ";charset=" + http_get_charset_label(encoding),
            "Date": getDateHeader(),
        };
        # Merge any response headers stashed by a handler's preprocessRequest() —
        # e.g. CORS headers — so that framework-level exceptions during response
        # serialization still return headers the browser needs to read the body
        if (cx."error-response-headers".typeCode() == NT_HASH) {
            err_hdr = http_canonicalize_reply_header_names(mergeResponseHeaders(err_hdr,
                cx."error-response-headers"));
        }
        # an HTTP/1.x connection closed after the response is announced in it (RFC 9112 section 9.6)
        if (close) {
            err_hdr.Connection = "close";
        }
        return <HttpAsyncResponseInfo>{
            "code": status_code,
            "hdr": err_hdr,
            "body": err_body,
            "close": close,
            "cx": cx,
            "listener": listener,
        };
    }

    # Logs a framework-level error via the handler's own logError() method if it has
    # one.  For Qorus service-bound handlers, this routes the message into the
    # owning service's log file (see OMQ::AbstractServiceHttpHandler::logError()),
    # so operators see protocol-upgrade / SSE setup failures in the service log
    # instead of only in the listener log.  Best-effort: any error (method missing,
    # etc.) is swallowed so the outer error path continues.
    private:internal logViaHandlerIfPossible(*object handler, int cid, string err) {
        if (!handler) {
            return;
        }
        try {
            call_object_method(handler, "logError", "cid %d: %s", cid, err);
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ignored) {
            # handler has no logError() method, or logError() threw — already logged
            # via listener.logError() at the call site, so this is a safe no-op
        }
    }

    # sends a reply to a request
    # If async_response_out is provided, capture response info instead of sending
    private sendReply(RequestHelper request_helper, HttpListener listener, Socket s,
            *HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler handler, hash<auto> rv, reference<hash> cx, hash<auto> hdr,
            bool head, *reference<hash<HttpAsyncResponseInfo>> async_response_out) {
        # a stopping server takes no new long-lived connection, which would hold its drain until the drain timeout:
        # a protocol upgrade (HTTP/1.1 101, or an HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 extended CONNECT), a CONNECT tunnel or an event
        # stream is refused (qore#5899)
        if (stopping && (rv."code" == 101 || hdr.method == "CONNECT"
                || (rv.sse_source ? rv.sse_source.isUsed() : rv.hdr."Content-Type" == MimeTypeSse))) {
            listener.log("cid %d: refusing %s from handler %y: the server is stopping", cx.id,
                (rv."code" == 101 || hdr.method == "CONNECT") ? "a protocol upgrade or tunnel" : "an event stream",
                cx.handler_name);
            cx.close = True;
            rv = <HttpResponseInfo>{
                "code": 503,
                "body": "the server is stopping",
                "hdr": {"Content-Type": "text/plain", "Connection": "close"},
            };
        }
        if (request_helper.log_info) {
            listener.log("sendReply: code=%d async_response_out=%y http2=%y http3=%y",
                rv."code", exists async_response_out, cx."header-info".http2, cx."header-info".http3);
        }
        # a handler can close a connection, but it cannot keep open one that the request requires to close: an
        # HTTP/1.0 request without the keep-alive option, or any request with the close option (RFC 9112 section
        # 9.3); every hash<HttpResponseInfo> has a "close" key, so a False value is not a request to keep it open
        bool handler_close = boolean(rv.close);
        if (handler_close) {
            cx.close = True;
        }

        rv.close = boolean(cx.close);

        # reports the transmission result of the response to the handler's after_send callback; the result is
        # reported by the code that sends the response, or here if it is not sent
        *ResponseAfterSendNotifier after_send;
        if (rv.after_send) {
            after_send = new ResponseAfterSendNotifier(remove rv.after_send, cx, handler, listener);
        }
        on_error if (after_send) {
            after_send.notify(False);
        }

        # if there is a pending chunked body that has not yet been read on exit, then read it and discard before sending any response
        on_exit if (s.pendingHttpChunkedBody())
            s.readHTTPChunkedBodyBinary(HttpServer::ReadTimeout);

        if (!HttpCodes{rv."code"}) { # if the handler returns an invalid hash
            string str = sprintf("%s handler returned an invalid response rv: %y", cx.handler_name, rv);
            cx.close = True;
            remove cx.persistent;
            # an event source of the response is not used; it releases what it registered
            if (rv.sse_source instanceof HttpServerAsyncIo::AbstractSseEventSource) {
                try {
                    rv.sse_source.stop();
                } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                    listener.logError("cid %d: the unused SSE event source of handler %y could not be stopped: %s",
                        cx.id, cx.handler_name, get_exception_string(ex));
                }
            }

            if (async_response_out) {
                # the async I/O controller sends the error response; sending it here as well sent a second error
                # response, as sendHttpError() cannot mark the caller's context as having sent a response
                bool is_multiplexed = (cx."header-info".http2 ?? False) || (cx."header-info".http3 ?? False);
                async_response_out = buildAsyncErrorResponse(500, str, cx, listener, s, !is_multiplexed);
            } else {
                sendHttpError(listener, cx, s, 500, str, NOTHING, NOTHING, cx."response-encoding", head);
                cx."response-sent" = True;
            }
            logError("%s", str);
            # the handler's response was replaced with an error response
            if (after_send) {
                after_send.notify(False);
            }
            return;
        }

        #printf("HTTP DEBUG: handler %s returned: %n\n", cx.handler_name, rv);

        # currently enforced by the handlers:
        # RFC 2616 section 4.3: http://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc2616#section-4.3
        # All 1xx (informational), 204 (no content), and 304 (not modified) responses
        # MUST NOT include a message-body. All other responses do include a
        # message-body, although it MAY be of zero length.

        # the configured headers of this response, read once, as a subclass can change them while requests are served,
        # and the information about their names, made again only when they change
        hash<auto> conf_hdr = self.hdr;
        *hash<Priv::ConfiguredHeaderInfo> hdr_info = self.hdr_info;
        if (!hdr_info || hdr_info.hdr !== conf_hdr) {
            hdr_info = getConfiguredHeaderInfo(conf_hdr);
        }
        # the names of the fields managed by the server are canonical from here on
        rv.hdr = getMergedResponseHeaders(conf_hdr, hdr_info, cx."listener-id",
            http_canonicalize_reply_header_names(rv.hdr));

        # a response to a HEAD request has the headers of the GET response (RFC 9110 section 9.3.2)
        if (head && rv."code" >= 200 && rv."code" != 204 && rv."code" != 304) {
            prepareHeadResponse(\rv, cx, s);
        }

        if ((exists rv.body || (bool chunked_body = rv.hasKeyValue("chunked_body")) || rv.send_callback)
            && (head
                || (rv."code" < 200
                    || (rv."code" == 204
                    || rv."code" == 304)))) {
            remove rv.body;
            if (chunked_body) {
                remove rv.chunked_body;
            }
            # a send_callback provides a message body too
            remove rv.send_callback;
        }

        bool is_sse = False;
        if (rv.hdr."Content-Type") {
            string ct = rv.hdr."Content-Type".lwr();
            # the exact media type check is made only for a value that can be an event stream, as this runs for every
            # response
            if (ct.startsWith(MimeTypeSse)) {
                is_sse = isEventStreamMediaType(ct);
            }
        }

        bool is_h1 = !(cx."header-info".http2 ?? False) && !(cx."header-info".http3 ?? False);

        # the source of an event stream that pushes its events to a stream sent by the async I/O controller, so that no
        # thread waits for them (qore#5758): it is replaced by its run (see takeSseSource()); no local variable is
        # added to this method, which every response runs
        if (rv.sse_source) {
            rv.sse_source = takeSseSource(rv, listener, cx, is_sse && !head && rv."code" >= 200 && rv."code" < 300
                && (!is_h1 || exists async_response_out));
            if (rv.sse_source.isUsed()) {
                remove rv.send_callback;
            }
        }

        # a response to an HTTP/1.0 request must not have Transfer-Encoding (RFC 9112 section 6.1); a message body of
        # unknown length is delimited by closing the connection instead (RFC 9112 section 6.3)
        bool chunked_allowed = !is_h1 || HttpServer::requestAllowsChunked(hdr);
        if (!chunked_allowed) {
            remove rv.hdr."Transfer-Encoding";
            if (!is_sse && !rv.hdr."Content-Length" && (rv.send_callback || rv.hasKeyValue("chunked_body")
                    || (rv.body instanceof InputStream))) {
                cx.close = True;
                rv.close = True;
                # a stream request handler has already set the header
                rv.hdr.Connection = "close";
            }
        }
        bool has_content_length = boolean(rv.hdr."Content-Length");
        bool has_transfer_encoding = boolean(rv.hdr."Transfer-Encoding");
        if (is_h1 && chunked_allowed && rv.send_callback && !has_content_length && !has_transfer_encoding) {
            rv.hdr."Transfer-Encoding" = "chunked";
            has_transfer_encoding = True;
        }
        if (is_h1 && is_sse && !rv.body && !rv.hasKey("chunked_body") && !rv.send_callback
                && !has_content_length && !has_transfer_encoding) {
            rv.hdr.Connection = "close";
        }

        if (!head && rv."code" == 200 && hdr.method != "OPTIONS" && !rv.streaming && !is_sse
            && !rv.hasKey("chunked_body") && !(rv.body instanceof InputStream) && !rv.send_callback) {
            bool empty_body = !rv.hasKey("body");
            if (!empty_body) {
                int body_tc = rv.body.typeCode();
                if (body_tc == NT_NOTHING || body_tc == NT_NULL) {
                    empty_body = True;
                } else if ((body_tc == NT_STRING || body_tc == NT_BINARY) && rv.body.size() == 0) {
                    empty_body = True;
                }
            }
            if (empty_body) {
                rv."code" = 204;
                remove rv.body;
            }
        }

        rv.hdr.Date = getDateHeader();

        # the names of the fields managed by the server are canonical since getMergedResponseHeaders() above if they
        # are canonical in the configured headers, as the handler's headers were made canonical and the server sets
        # only canonical names after that; whether they are canonical in the configured headers merged above is known
        # from getConfiguredHeaderInfo(), which costs less than finding the names of the merged headers canonical again
        HttpServer::http_set_reply_headers(s, cx, \rv, NOTHING, hdr_info.canonical);

        # Detect HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 extended CONNECT (RFC 8441 / RFC 9220)
        bool ext_connect = is_extended_connect(cx, hdr);

        # any 2xx response to an extended CONNECT establishes the tunnel (RFC 8441 section 4, RFC 9220): a handler
        # that is not a socket handler and answers with an ordinary (non-streaming) 2xx response, such as a catch-all
        # handler that serves a page for every path, cannot accept the protocol; sent as it is, the response would
        # promise a tunnel and end the stream at once (issue #5667).  The request is refused instead, so that a
        # client in automatic HTTP-version mode can fall back to an HTTP/1.1 Upgrade and one that requested HTTP/2 or
        # HTTP/3 gets a clear error
        if (ext_connect && !rv.streaming && rv."code" >= 200 && rv."code" < 300
                && refuseNonTunnelExtendedConnectResponse(cx, hdr, rv)) {
            string proto_label = (cx."header-info".http3 ?? False) ? "HTTP/3" : "HTTP/2";
            listener.logError("cid %d: %s extended CONNECT (protocol %y) answered by handler %y with a %d response "
                "that does not establish a tunnel; refusing the request", cx.id, proto_label, hdr.":protocol",
                cx.handler_name, rv."code");
            # the refusal replaces the handler's response and is sent as an error response below, which also works
            # with the asynchronous I/O controller (HTTP/2 multiplexing, HTTP/3)
            rv."code" = 501;
            rv.body = sprintf("the resource does not support the requested protocol (%s extended CONNECT)",
                proto_label);
            remove rv.chunked_body;
            remove rv.send_callback;
            # the headers of the handler's response describe the representation that is not sent (ETag,
            # Content-Range, Set-Cookie, ...): the refusal has only the listener's headers and the standard ones
            # (RFC 9110 sections 8.8 and 14.4)
            rv.hdr = getMergedResponseHeaders(conf_hdr, hdr_info, cx."listener-id", NOTHING)
                + {"Date": getDateHeader()};
            HttpServer::http_set_reply_headers(s, cx, \rv);
        }

        if (rv."code" >= 300 && rv."code" < 400) {
            # handle redirect msgs (RFC 2616 section 10.3 http://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc2616#section-10.3)
            doResponse(listener, s, cx, rv, chunked_allowed, after_send, \async_response_out,
                request_helper.log_info);
        } else if (rv."code" >= 400) {
            # a send_callback provides the message body
            if (!head && !rv.body && !rv.hasKeyValue("chunked_body") && !rv.send_callback) {
                rv.body = sprintf("unknown error in %s handler", cx.handler_name);
            }
            if (async_response_out) {
                # Capture the error response for the async I/O controller to send
                # through h2op (HTTP/2) or sendHTTPResponse (HTTP/1.x), rather than
                # sending directly on the socket which would bypass the poll operation
                # state machine and corrupt the connection lifecycle; the body has the same default
                # Content-Type as with sendHttpError() and a successful response
                if (!rv.hdr."Content-Type") {
                    *string default_content_type = getDefaultContentType(rv, cx."response-encoding"
                        ?? s.getEncoding().lwr());
                    if (default_content_type) {
                        rv.hdr."Content-Type" = default_content_type;
                    }
                }
                doResponse(listener, s, cx, rv, chunked_allowed, after_send, \async_response_out,
                    request_helper.log_info);
            } else {
                sendHttpError(listener, cx, s, rv."code", rv.body,
                    rv.hasKey("chunked_body") ? rv.chunked_body : NOTHING,
                    rv.hdr, cx."response-encoding", head);
                cx."response-sent" = True;
                if (after_send) {
                    after_send.notify(True);
                }
            }
            remove cx.persistent;
        } else {
            #printf("**** RESPONSE: %d ct: %s (te: %y) encoding: %y: %Y\n", rv."code", rv.hdr."Content-Type",
            #    rv.hdr."Transfer-Encoding", cx.encoding, rv.body);

            # the Content-Type of a body without one depends on the data, which compression below turns into
            # binary data; compression does not set a Content-Type, so it is determined only for a response without one
            *string default_content_type = rv.hdr."Content-Type"
                ? NOTHING
                : getDefaultContentType(rv, cx."response-encoding" ?? s.getEncoding().lwr());

            # compress body if eligible for compression; skip InputStream bodies (streaming),
            # send_callback responses, and handlers that manage their own content encoding
            if (rv.body && !(rv.body instanceof InputStream) && !rv.send_callback
                && !rv.no_content_encoding && !rv.hdr."Content-Encoding"
                && rv.body.size() > CompressionThreshold) {
                int orig_size = rv.body.size();
                # the levels are read once, as they can be changed concurrently
                hash<string, int> levels = compression_levels;
                if (cx.encoding == "br") {
                    rv.hdr."Content-Encoding" = "br";
                    rv.body = brotli(rv.body, levels.br);
                } else if (cx.encoding == "zstd") {
                    rv.hdr."Content-Encoding" = "zstd";
                    rv.body = zstd(rv.body, levels.zstd);
                } else if (cx.encoding == "deflate") {
                    rv.hdr."Content-Encoding" = "deflate";
                    rv.body = compress(rv.body, levels.deflate);
                } else if (cx.encoding == "gzip") {
                    rv.hdr."Content-Encoding" = "gzip";
                    rv.body = gzip(rv.body, levels.gzip);
                } else if (cx.encoding == "bzip2") {
                    rv.hdr."Content-Encoding" = "bzip2";
                    rv.body = bzip2(rv.body, levels.bzip2);
                }
                if (rv.hdr."Content-Encoding") {
                    listener.log("cid %d: %s compressed %s: %d -> %d bytes (type: %s)", cx.id,
                        cx."header-info".http3 ? "HTTP/3" : cx."header-info".http2 ? "HTTP/2" : "HTTP/1.x",
                        rv.hdr."Content-Encoding", orig_size, rv.body.size(), rv.body.type());
                }
            }

            if (default_content_type && !rv.hdr."Content-Type") {
                rv.hdr."Content-Type" = default_content_type;
            }

            # For HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 extended CONNECT streaming, don't send response here -
            # it's handled in the dedicated socket section
            if (!rv.streaming || !ext_connect) {
                doResponse(listener, s, cx, rv, chunked_allowed, after_send, \async_response_out,
                    request_helper.log_info);
            }
        }

        # a response that does not end the exchange (a Server-Sent Events stream, a protocol upgrade, or an extended
        # CONNECT response) is never completely sent
        if (after_send && ((rv.streaming && ext_connect) || (rv.sse_source && rv.sse_source.isUsed())
                || rv.hdr."Content-Type" == MimeTypeSse
                || rv."code" == 101)) {
            if (async_response_out) {
                remove async_response_out.after_send;
            }
            after_send.notify(False);
        }

        if (rv.log)
            listener.log("cid %d: %s (from %s): %s", cx.id, cx.handler_name, cx."peer-info".address_desc, rv.log);
        if (rv.errlog)
            listener.logError("cid %d: %s (from %s): %s", cx.id, cx.handler_name, cx."peer-info".address_desc,
                rv.errlog);

        if (!rv.close && rv.user_state) {
            cx.user_state += rv.user_state;
        }

        # Check for dedicated socket handlers: HTTP/1.1 WebSocket (101), SSE,
        # HTTP/2 extended CONNECT (200 + streaming), or HTTP/3 extended CONNECT (200 + streaming)
        bool is_http3 = ext_connect && (cx."header-info".http3 ?? False);
        if (rv.streaming && ext_connect) {
            string proto_label = is_http3 ? "HTTP/3" : "HTTP/2";
            string sock_id = sprintf("%y:%d", cx.id, cx."header-info".stream_id);
            if (handler_close) {
                listener.logError("cid %d: %s extended CONNECT returned from handler %y, but the close "
                    "flag is set", cx.id, proto_label, cx.handler_name);
            } else if (!(handler instanceof HttpServer::AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface)) {
                listener.logError("cid %d: %s extended CONNECT returned from handler %y, but the handler "
                    "does not inherit AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface (class: %y)", cx.id, proto_label,
                    cx.handler_name, handler.className());
                self.sendHttpError(listener, cx, s, 501,
                    "extended CONNECT handler must inherit AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface",
                    NOTHING, NOTHING, cx."response-encoding", False);
                return;
            } else {
                AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface sockhandler
                    = cast<HttpServer::AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface>(handler);
                if (listener.registerDedicatedSocket(request_helper, sock_id, sockhandler)) {
                    # holds the request helper for the server's end of a stream whose handler takes it over
                    # (WebSocketHandler), which then runs ws_end when the stream ends: no thread of the handler pool
                    # waits for the stream (qore#5752)
                    Priv::DedicatedConnectionHolder holder(request_helper, external_connections);
                    try {
                        int stream_id = cx."header-info".stream_id;
                        hash<auto> resp_hdr = rv.hdr ?? {};

                        if (is_http3) {
                            # HTTP/3 (RFC 9220): submit CONNECT response via QUIC session
                            int session_id = cx."header-info".session_id;
                            s.submitQuicConnectResponse(session_id, stream_id,
                                rv."code" ?? 200, resp_hdr);
                            *AsyncSocketIo::AsyncSocketIoController ctrl = cx.async_ctrl;
                            if (ctrl) {
                                ctrl.wakeSocket(s);
                            }
                        } else {
                            # HTTP/2 multiplexed mode: submit CONNECT response directly
                            # to the nghttp2 session.  h2op on the I/O thread will flush it.
                            s.submitHttp2ConnectResponse(stream_id, rv."code" ?? 200, resp_hdr);
                            # Wake the I/O controller so h2op's continuePoll() flushes
                            # the pending CONNECT response
                            *AsyncSocketIo::AsyncSocketIoController ctrl = cx.async_ctrl;
                            if (ctrl) {
                                ctrl.wakeSocket(s);
                            }
                        }

                        hash<auto> sock_cx = cx + {"listener": listener, "id": sock_id};
                        sock_cx."header-info".ws_end = getAsyncWebSocketEnd(holder, listener, NOTHING, sock_id,
                            cx.handler_name);
                        sock_cx."header-info".ws_accept_end = sub () { holder.accept(); };
                        sock_cx."header-info".ws_submit = getHandlerPoolSubmitter();
                        if (sockhandler.start(listener.getID(), sock_cx, hdr, s)) {
                            request_helper.setDedicatedExternal();
                        }
                    } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                        if (ex.err == "SOCKET-NOT-OPEN") {
                            listener.logError("cid %d: %s extended CONNECT handler %y: socket closed by "
                                "remote", cx.id, proto_label, cx.handler_name);
                        } else {
                            string desc = !debug
                                ? sprintf("%s: %s: %s", get_ex_pos(ex), ex.err, ex.desc)
                                : get_exception_string(ex);
                            listener.logError("cid %d: error in %s extended CONNECT handler %y: %s",
                                cx.id, proto_label, cx.handler_name, desc);
                        }
                    }
                    # a handler that did not take over the end of the stream does not need the holder: the request
                    # helper then counts the stream until this request ends, as before
                    if (!request_helper.isDedicatedExternal() || !holder.isAccepted()) {
                        holder.release();
                    }
                    if (request_helper.isDedicatedExternal()) {
                        listener.log("cid %d: %s extended CONNECT handler %y managed externally",
                            cx.id, proto_label, cx.handler_name);
                    } else {
                        listener.removeDedicatedSocket(sock_id);
                        listener.log("cid %d: stopped %s extended CONNECT handler: %y",
                            cx.id, proto_label, cx.handler_name);
                    }
                } else {
                    listener.logError("cid %d: cannot start %s extended CONNECT handler %y because the "
                        "listener is shutting down", cx.id, proto_label, cx.handler_name);
                }
            }
            return;
        }
        # a response with an event source (see takeSseSource()) is an event stream only if the source is used, and
        # never needs a socket handler; the stream is set up in a method of its own, so that its locals are not part
        # of the frame of this method, which every response runs (qore#5758)
        if (rv.sse_source ? rv.sse_source.isUsed() : rv.hdr."Content-Type" == MimeTypeSse) {
            setupSseResponse(request_helper, listener, s, handler, rv, cx, hdr, handler_close, \async_response_out);
        } else if (rv."code" == 101) {
            # WebSocket upgrade — use async I/O controller
            if (handler_close) {
                string err = sprintf("101 Switching Protocols returned from handler %y with connection "
                    "close flag set — protocol upgrade requires a persistent connection",
                    cx.handler_name);
                listener.logError("cid %d: %s", cx.id, err);
                logViaHandlerIfPossible(handler, cx.id, err);
                throw "WEBSOCKET-HANDLER-ERROR", err;
            } else if (!(handler instanceof HttpServer::AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface)) {
                string err = sprintf("101 Switching Protocols returned from handler %y, but the handler "
                    "does not inherit AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface (class: %y) — protocol upgrade "
                    "requires a handler that implements AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface",
                    cx.handler_name, handler.className());
                listener.logError("cid %d: %s", cx.id, err);
                logViaHandlerIfPossible(handler, cx.id, err);
                throw "WEBSOCKET-HANDLER-ERROR", err;
            } else {
                if (listener.registerDedicatedSocket(request_helper, cx.id, handler)) {
                    # True if the handler manages the connection after start() returns (an asynchronous HTTP/1.1
                    # WebSocket connection): the server's end of the connection (ws_end below) then runs when the
                    # connection ends, not here (qore#5752)
                    bool externally_managed = False;
                    # holds the request helper for the server's end of an externally managed connection
                    Priv::DedicatedConnectionHolder holder(request_helper, external_connections);
                    on_exit {
                        cx.close = True;
                        if (!externally_managed) {
                            holder.release();
                            listener.removeDedicatedSocket(cx.id);
                            listener.log("cid %d: stopped dedicated socket handler: %y", cx.id, cx.handler_name);
                        }
                    }
                    AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface sockhandler = cast<HttpServer::AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface>(handler);

                    # Create WebSocket poll operation (submitted by startImpl after
                    # setConnection/registered, ensuring echo mode is set before any
                    # frames arrive on the I/O thread)
                    *HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpWebSocketPollOperation ws_poll_op;
                    if (asyncIo) {
                        ws_poll_op = new HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpWebSocketPollOperation(s);
                    }

                    if (async_response_out) {
                        if (asyncIo) {
                            # Async mode: send 101 response via the I/O controller
                            # before entering startImpl(). This must happen BEFORE
                            # registered() because registered() may throw (e.g., bad
                            # handler), and the client expects the 101 response
                            # regardless. Uses the global async I/O controller since
                            # the socket was removed from the HTTP server's controller
                            # at header dispatch.
                            AbstractPollOperation send_op = s.startPollSendHttpResponse(
                                async_response_out."code",
                                HttpServer::HttpCodes{async_response_out."code"}, "1.1",
                                async_response_out.hdr ?? {}, async_response_out.body);
                            execAsyncPollOperation(s, send_op);
                        } else {
                            # Non-async mode: send 101 response synchronously on
                            # handler thread (sendIntern handles EAGAIN on non-blocking
                            # sockets via poll())
                            s.sendHTTPResponse(async_response_out."code",
                                HttpServer::HttpCodes{async_response_out."code"}, "1.1",
                                async_response_out.hdr ?? {}, async_response_out.body);
                        }
                        delete async_response_out;
                    }
                    on_exit {
                        if (ws_poll_op && !externally_managed) {
                            asyncIo.deregisterH1WebSocketConnection(s);
                        }
                    }

                    # Pass poll_op and async I/O context to handler via header-info
                    hash<auto> ws_cx = cx + {"listener": listener};
                    if (ws_poll_op) {
                        ws_cx."header-info".ws_poll_op = ws_poll_op;
                        # the controller keeps this context in the connection's entry for the life of the
                        # connection, so the reference back to the controller is opaque, as the controller's
                        # references to its connections' entries are (see
                        # HttpAsyncSocketIoController::connections)
                        ws_cx."header-info".async_io_controller @= asyncIo;
                        ws_cx."header-info".async_target = asyncTarget;
                        # the server's end of a connection that the handler manages externally, which the handler
                        # runs when the connection ends; it holds the request helper, so the listener's count of
                        # dedicated connections (dThreads, which a stop of the listener waits for) includes the
                        # connection until then
                        ws_cx."header-info".ws_end = getAsyncWebSocketEnd(holder, listener, s, cx.id,
                            cx.handler_name);
                        # called by a handler that takes over the end of the connection (WebSocketHandler)
                        ws_cx."header-info".ws_accept_end = sub () { holder.accept(); };
                    }

                    # test hook: deterministically widen the pre-start window between
                    # registerDedicatedSocket() (which incremented the listener's dThreads
                    # WaitGroup) and start() (which increments the handler's lh{lid}) so a
                    # regression test can force a listener stop into it; compiled out of
                    # release builds
                    @debug {
                        if (*string prestart_delay_ms = getenv("QORE_WS_PRESTART_DELAY_MS")) {
                            usleep(prestart_delay_ms.toInt() * 1000);
                        }
                    }
                    try {
                        # an asynchronous HTTP/1.1 WebSocket connection returns as soon as it is set up, and its
                        # handler runs ws_end when it ends; no thread of the handler pool waits for it
                        externally_managed = sockhandler.start(listener.getID(), ws_cx, hdr, s)
                            && holder.isAccepted();
                    } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                        if (ex.err == "SOCKET-NOT-OPEN") {
                            listener.logError("cid %d: dedicated socket handler %y: socket closed by remote", cx.id,
                                cx.handler_name);
                        } else {
                            string desc = !debug
                                ? sprintf("%s: %s: %s", get_ex_pos(ex), ex.err, ex.desc)
                                : get_exception_string(ex);
                            listener.logError("cid %d: error in dedicated socket handler %y: %s", cx.id,
                                cx.handler_name, desc);
                        }
                    }
                } else {
                    listener.logError("cid %d: cannot start dedicated socket handler %y because the listener is "
                        "shutting down", cx.id, cx.handler_name);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    #! Returns the server's end of an asynchronous HTTP/1.1 WebSocket connection that its handler manages externally
    /** The handler calls the code when the connection has ended (see \c WebSocketHandler::startImpl()); it
        deregisters the connection from the async I/O controller, which closes its socket, removes it from the
        listener's dedicated sockets, and logs its end, as the server did when the handler's start() returned while it
        waited for the connection.  The code holds the request helper, so the listener's count of dedicated
        connections (which a stop of the listener waits for) includes the connection until the code is released, and
        it runs its actions once.

        @param holder holds the connection's request helper until the connection has ended; the server releases it
        itself when the handler does not manage the connection externally, so that code kept by a handler that does
        not call it holds nothing
        @param listener the connection's listener
        @param s the connection's socket, for an HTTP/1.1 connection, which the code deregisters from the async I/O
        controller
        @param sock_id the ID of the dedicated socket
        @param handler_name the name of the handler

        @return the code that ends the connection in the server
    */
    private code<nothing()> getAsyncWebSocketEnd(Priv::DedicatedConnectionHolder holder, HttpListener listener,
            *Socket s, softstring sock_id, *string handler_name) {
        # the controller and the handler keep this code for the life of the connection, so its references to the
        # controller and to the listener (which references the server) are weak, as the context's own reference to
        # the controller is (see the 101 path); the connection's context still references the listener, so the
        # Program is kept running while the connection is open (see Priv::ExternalConnectionKeeper)
        hash<auto> refs;
        refs.ctrl @= asyncIo;
        refs.listener @= listener;
        return sub () {
            # runs once
            if (!holder.claim()) {
                return;
            }
            # the listener counts the connection until its end is complete
            on_exit holder.release();
            *HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpAsyncSocketIoController ctrl = refs.ctrl;
            # the listener, unless it has been deleted
            *HttpListener l = refs.listener;
            if (s && ctrl) {
                try {
                    ctrl.deregisterH1WebSocketConnection(s);
                } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                    if (l) {
                        l.logError("dedicated socket %y: error deregistering the WebSocket connection of handler "
                            "%y: %s: %s", sock_id, handler_name, ex.err, ex.desc);
                    }
                }
            }
            if (l) {
                l.removeDedicatedSocket(sock_id);
                l.log("dedicated socket %y: stopped dedicated socket handler: %y", sock_id, handler_name);
            }
        };
    }

    #! Returns code that runs code on a thread of the handler pool, with the dispatch context of the async target
    /** For the end of a multiplexed WebSocket stream that its handler manages externally (see
        \c WebSocketHandler::startImpl()); the end runs user code, which must not run on the async I/O callback
        worker that reports the end of the stream.  Code that the pool does not take (when it is stopping) runs on a
        thread of its own, or, if no thread can be started, in the calling thread.
    */
    private code<nothing(code<nothing()>)> getHandlerPoolSubmitter() {
        # held in the connection's context, so the references to the controller and the target are weak
        hash<auto> refs;
        refs.ctrl @= asyncIo;
        refs.target @= asyncTarget;
        return sub (code<nothing()> c) {
            *HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpAsyncSocketIoController ctrl = refs.ctrl;
            *HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpAsyncTarget target = refs.target;
            if (ctrl && target) {
                try {
                    ctrl.submitToHandlerPool(target, c);
                    return;
                } catch () {
                    # the pool is stopping or refuses the task: run it on a thread of its own
                }
            }
            try {
                background c();
            } catch () {
                # no thread can be started (a thread limit): the end must still run, so it runs here
                c();
            }
        };
    }

    #! Returns @ref True if a Content-Type value has the media type of an event stream (\c text/event-stream)
    /** The media type is compared case-insensitively and its parameters are ignored (RFC 9110 section 8.3.1), as the
        socket layer does when it decides that a response without a message body has no \c Content-Length.
    */
    private static bool isEventStreamMediaType(auto ct) {
        return ct.typeCode() == NT_STRING && ct =~ /^[ \t]*text\/event-stream[ \t]*(;|$)/i;
    }

    #! Sets up the stream of a Server-Sent Events response on the async I/O controller
    /** Called by sendReply() for an event stream: a response of a socket handler with the \c text/event-stream
        \c Content-Type, or a response with an event source that is used (see takeSseSource()).
    */
    private setupSseResponse(RequestHelper request_helper, HttpListener listener, Socket s,
            *HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler handler, hash<auto> rv, hash<auto> cx, hash<auto> hdr,
            bool handler_close, *reference<hash<HttpAsyncResponseInfo>> async_response_out) {
        # the run of the response's event source, if any
        *Priv::SseEventSourceRun sse_run = rv.sse_source;
        # SSE response — set up async SSE connection via the I/O controller.
        # The write path depends on the HTTP version:
        # - HTTP/1.x: HttpSseStreamPollOperation queues events for the I/O
        #   controller's Sse state to drain via chunked encoding
        # - HTTP/2: Http2SseStream pushes DATA frames directly via
        #   sendHttp2StreamData(); the nghttp2 session's sendPendingData()
        #   (driven by h2op on the I/O thread) flushes them to the socket
        # - HTTP/3: Http3SseStream pushes DATA on the QUIC stream via
        #   submitQuicStreamData()
        bool sse_is_http2 = cx."header-info".http2 ?? False;
        bool sse_is_http3 = cx."header-info".http3 ?? False;
        # the bound of the data held for the client, which a socket handler's setup applies before it registers the
        # connection (unless it has its own); applied below to a stream that its setup left without one
        cx.sse_max_buffer_size = listener.getSseMaxBufferSize();
        # a close that the request requires does not prevent an SSE stream: an HTTP/1.x stream is delimited
        # by the connection close anyway
        if (handler_close) {
            string err = sprintf("SSE response (Content-Type: %s) returned from handler %y with "
                "connection close flag set — SSE requires a persistent connection",
                MimeTypeSse, cx.handler_name);
            listener.logError("cid %d: %s", cx.id, err);
            logViaHandlerIfPossible(handler, cx.id, err);
            throw "SSE-HANDLER-ERROR", err;
        } else if (!sse_run && !(handler instanceof HttpServer::AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface)) {
            string err = sprintf("SSE response (Content-Type: %s) returned from handler %y, but the "
                "handler does not inherit AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface (class: %y) — SSE "
                "requires a handler that implements setupAsyncSseConnection{,Http2,Http3}()",
                MimeTypeSse, cx.handler_name, handler.className());
            listener.logError("cid %d: %s", cx.id, err);
            logViaHandlerIfPossible(handler, cx.id, err);
            throw "SSE-HANDLER-ERROR", err;
        } else if (sse_is_http2 || sse_is_http3) {
            # HTTP/2 / HTTP/3 SSE: multiplexed streams — submit response
            # headers directly on the stream and hand the stream to a
            # protocol-specific SSE writer.  The handler now owns this
            # stream lifecycle, so mark request_helper as dedicated; the
            # async I/O controller's multiplexed-result dispatcher will
            # leave the stream alone.
            try {
                int stream_id = cx."header-info".stream_id;
                hash<auto> resp_hdr = rv.hdr ?? {};
                # HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 do not use chunked Transfer-Encoding
                remove resp_hdr."Transfer-Encoding";
                *AsyncSocketIo::AsyncSocketIoController ctrl = cx.async_ctrl;
                Socket sse_sock = s;
                *code<nothing()> wake_cb;
                if (ctrl) {
                    wake_cb = sub () { ctrl.wakeSocket(sse_sock); };
                }
                string cid = sprintf("%d:%d", cx.id, stream_id);
                # the setup methods are resolved by name, so a handler that is not a
                # ServerSentEventHandler may implement them and return nothing; that costs
                # it the peer-close notification below but must not break its stream
                *HttpServerAsyncIo::AbstractSseConnection sse_conn;
                # a handler that is not a socket handler gets the stream from the server, for its event source
                bool sock_handler = handler instanceof HttpServer::AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface;
                if (sse_is_http3) {
                    int session_id = cx."header-info".session_id;
                    s.submitQuicResponseStreaming(session_id, stream_id,
                        rv."code" ?? 200, resp_hdr);
                    if (sock_handler) {
                        sse_conn = call_object_method(handler, "setupAsyncSseConnectionHttp3",
                            s, cx, hdr, cid, session_id, stream_id, wake_cb);
                    } else {
                        sse_conn = new HttpServerAsyncIo::Http3SseStream(s, session_id, stream_id, wake_cb,
                            True);
                    }
                } else {
                    s.submitHttp2StreamingResponseHeaders(stream_id,
                        rv."code" ?? 200, resp_hdr);
                    if (wake_cb) {
                        wake_cb();
                    }
                    if (sock_handler) {
                        sse_conn = call_object_method(handler, "setupAsyncSseConnectionHttp2",
                            s, cx, hdr, cid, stream_id, wake_cb);
                    } else {
                        sse_conn = new HttpServerAsyncIo::Http2SseStream(s, stream_id, wake_cb, True);
                    }
                }
                if (sse_conn) {
                    configureSseStream(listener, cid, sse_conn);
                }
                # Deregister the connection when the peer goes away.  On
                # HTTP/1.x the socket is the stream, so the SSE poll
                # operation's close callback covers this (see the branch
                # below); HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 multiplex, so the transport has
                # to report the teardown of this one stream explicitly, and
                # the controller supplies the hook that wires it up.
                *code<nothing(object)> register_sse_close_notify =
                    cx."header-info"."register_sse_close_notify";
                if (sse_conn && register_sse_close_notify) {
                    sse_conn.setPeerCloseCallback(getSseStreamEndCallback(sock_handler
                        ? cast<HttpServer::AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface>(handler)
                        : NOTHING, cid, sse_run));
                    register_sse_close_notify(sse_conn);
                } else if (sse_run) {
                    # an event source is stopped only when the end of its stream is reported: without the
                    # notification, the stream is not started and is reset
                    listener.logError("cid %d: the %s SSE stream of handler %y has no peer-close notification "
                        "(%s); resetting the stream of its event source", cx.id, sse_is_http3 ? "HTTP/3" : "HTTP/2",
                        cx.handler_name, sse_conn
                            ? "the async I/O controller supplied no hook"
                            : "no SSE connection object");
                    try {
                        if (sse_conn) {
                            sse_conn.abort();
                        } else if (sse_is_http3) {
                            s.cancelQuicStream(cx."header-info".session_id, stream_id);
                        } else {
                            s.resetHttp2Stream(stream_id);
                        }
                    } catch () {
                        # the stream has already ended
                    }
                    remove sse_conn;
                    # a socket handler's connection, which its setup registered, has ended
                    if (sock_handler) {
                        try {
                            cast<HttpServer::AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface>(handler).deregisterConnection(cid);
                        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                            listener.logError("cid %d: error deregistering SSE connection %y of handler %y: %s",
                                cx.id, cid, cx.handler_name, get_exception_string(ex));
                        }
                    }
                } else {
                    listener.logError("cid %d: the %s SSE stream of handler %y has no peer-close "
                        "notification (%s); the connection will stay registered after the peer "
                        "disconnects", cx.id, sse_is_http3 ? "HTTP/3" : "HTTP/2",
                        cx.handler_name, sse_conn
                            ? "the async I/O controller supplied no hook"
                            : "the handler returned no SSE connection object");
                }
                # Tell the controller this stream is owned by the handler
                request_helper.setDedicated();
                # the event source pushes the events of the stream from now on
                if (sse_run && sse_conn) {
                    sse_run.start(sse_conn);
                }
                # Wake the I/O thread to flush the HEADERS frame now (and
                # any DATA queued by the connection's registered() hook)
                if (ctrl) {
                    ctrl.wakeSocket(s);
                }
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                listener.logError("cid %d: error setting up %s SSE connection for handler %y: %s: %s",
                    cx.id, sse_is_http3 ? "HTTP/3" : "HTTP/2", cx.handler_name, ex.err, ex.desc);
            }
        } else if (async_response_out) {
            # HTTP/1.x SSE path — use the poll-op state machine in the
            # async I/O controller.  See setupAsyncSseConnection() for the
            # H1 poll loop; setWakeCallback() wires the async controller
            # to re-run continuePoll() when events are queued.
            try {
                string cid = string(cx.id);
                HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpSseStreamPollOperation poll_op =
                    (handler instanceof HttpServer::AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface)
                    ? call_object_method(handler, "setupAsyncSseConnection", s, cx, hdr, cid)
                    # a handler that is not a socket handler gets the stream from the server, for its event
                    # source
                    : new HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpSseStreamPollOperation(s);
                # Wire a wake callback so that events queued by a Qore-side
                # handler thread (e.g. via ServerSentEventConnection::send())
                # immediately trigger the async controller to re-run
                # continuePoll() and drain the event_channel.  Without
                # this, server-push SSE events sit in the channel until
                # the client sends something — which it never does.
                *AsyncSocketIo::AsyncSocketIoController ctrl = cx.async_ctrl;
                if (ctrl) {
                    Socket sse_sock = s;
                    poll_op.setWakeCallback(sub () { ctrl.wakeSocket(sse_sock); });
                }
                configureSseStream(listener, cid, poll_op);
                # Tell the async controller to manage this socket as an SSE stream
                async_response_out.code = rv."code";
                async_response_out.hdr = rv.hdr;
                async_response_out.streaming = True;
                async_response_out.stream_type =
                    HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpConnectionState::Sse;
                async_response_out.stream_operation = poll_op;
                # the controller calls it exactly once when the stream has ended, for any reason
                poll_op.setCloseCallback(getSseStreamEndCallback(
                    (handler instanceof HttpServer::AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface)
                    ? cast<HttpServer::AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface>(handler)
                    : NOTHING, cid, sse_run));
                # the event source pushes the events of the stream from now on; they are sent after the
                # response headers
                if (sse_run) {
                    sse_run.start(new HttpServerAsyncIo::Http1SseConnection(poll_op));
                }
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                listener.logError("cid %d: error setting up async SSE connection for handler %y: %s: %s",
                    cx.id, cx.handler_name, ex.err, ex.desc);
            }
        }
    }

    #! Takes the event source of a response out of it and returns its run
    /** Called by sendReply() only for a response with an event source; the work is done here so that the frame of
        sendReply(), which every response runs, does not change (qore#5758).

        @param rv the response; the caller removes its \c sse_source, and its \c send_callback too if the source is
        used
        @param listener the listener
        @param cx the request context
        @param use @ref True if the response is an event stream that the server sends on the async I/O controller

        @return the run of the source; a source that is not used (see SseEventSourceRun::isUsed()) has been stopped,
        unless the response's \c send_callback still sends its events

        @throw SSE-HANDLER-ERROR the \c sse_source is not an @ref HttpServerAsyncIo::AbstractSseEventSource
    */
    private Priv::SseEventSourceRun takeSseSource(hash<auto> rv, HttpListener listener, hash<auto> cx, bool use) {
        if (!(rv.sse_source instanceof HttpServerAsyncIo::AbstractSseEventSource)) {
            string err = sprintf("handler %y returned an sse_source of class %y; expecting "
                "HttpServerAsyncIo::AbstractSseEventSource", cx.handler_name, rv.sse_source.className());
            listener.logError("cid %d: %s", cx.id, err);
            throw "SSE-HANDLER-ERROR", err;
        }
        Priv::SseEventSourceRun run(rv.sse_source, listener, cx.id, cx.handler_name, getHandlerPoolSubmitter(), use);
        if (!use && !rv.send_callback) {
            # a source that is not used at all is stopped at once, so that it releases what it registered
            run.stopHere();
        }
        return run;
    }

    #! Raises an exception if the \c sse_max_buffer_size listener option is not positive
    private static checkSseMaxBufferSize(hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> info) {
        if (info.sse_max_buffer_size <= 0) {
            throw "HTTPSERVER-ADDLISTENER-ERROR", sprintf("listener option \"sse_max_buffer_size\" must be > 0; got "
                "%d", info.sse_max_buffer_size);
        }
    }

    #! Applies the listener's bound and warning log to an HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 SSE stream that its handler did not
    #! configure
    private static configureSseStream(HttpListener listener, string cid, HttpServerAsyncIo::AbstractSseConnection sse) {
        if (!sse.hasMaxBufferSize()) {
            sse.setMaxBufferSize(listener.getSseMaxBufferSize());
        }
        if (!sse.hasWarningCallback()) {
            sse.setWarningCallback(getSseWarningCallback(listener, cid));
        }
    }

    #! Applies the listener's bound and warning log to an HTTP/1.x SSE stream that its handler did not configure
    private static configureSseStream(HttpListener listener, string cid,
            HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpSseStreamPollOperation sse) {
        if (!sse.hasMaxBufferSize()) {
            sse.setMaxBufferSize(listener.getSseMaxBufferSize());
        }
        if (!sse.hasWarningCallback()) {
            sse.setWarningCallback(getSseWarningCallback(listener, cid));
        }
    }

    #! Returns the callback that logs the warning of an SSE stream whose client is dropped in the listener's log
    private static code<nothing(string)> getSseWarningCallback(HttpListener listener, string cid) {
        return sub (string msg) {
            listener.logWarn("SSE connection %y: %s", cid, msg);
        };
    }

    #! Returns the code that is called when an SSE stream has ended: by the async I/O controller, or, for a client dropped for its bound, on the thread that queued the data
    /** Deregisters a socket handler's connection, and ends the run of the stream's event source, if any.  Created
        here so that the frame of sendReply() keeps no locals for it.
    */
    private code<nothing()> getSseStreamEndCallback(*AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface sse_handler, string cid,
            *Priv::SseEventSourceRun run) {
        return sub () {
            if (sse_handler) {
                sse_handler.deregisterConnection(cid);
            }
            if (run) {
                run.end();
            }
        };
    }

    #! Executes an async poll operation using the global async I/O controller
    /** @par Internal Method
        This method is for internal use only and should not be called directly by user code.

        This method sends a response via the shared AsyncSocketIoController for efficient
        I/O multiplexing. It blocks until the response is fully sent, but the underlying I/O is
        handled asynchronously by the controller's I/O thread.

        Used for both HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 (QUIC) poll operations.

        @param s the socket to send on
        @param op the poll operation to execute

        @throw any exception raised during the I/O operation
    */
    execAsyncPollOperation(Socket s, AbstractPollOperation op) {
        # Wrap the operation with a DelegatingPollOperation that signals a Counter
        # when complete.  The handler thread waits on the Counter — this has no
        # dependency on the I/O thread's ability to process commands (only on it
        # completing the poll operation), eliminating deadlock risk.
        Counter done(1);
        *hash<auto> op_ex;
        auto wrapped_op = new DelegatingPollOperation(op,
            sub (hash<SocketPollResultInfo> result) {
                if (result.ex) {
                    op_ex = result.ex;
                }
                done.dec();
            });
        AsyncSocketIo::AsyncSocketIoController ctrl = AsyncSocketIo::getGlobalAsyncIoController();
        ctrl.submit(<SocketPollOperationInfo>{
            "sock": s,
            "spop": wrapped_op,
            "to": 30s,
            "owner": self.uniqueHash(),
        });
        done.waitForZero();
        if (op_ex) {
            throw op_ex.err, op_ex.desc, op_ex.arg;
        }
    }

    # rv is "hash" to strip types
    # If async_response_out is provided, capture response info instead of sending
    # Assumes stream yields homogeneous chunk types (string or binary) per read loop.
    private:internal data readAllStream(InputStream is, *int chunk_size) {
        int size = chunk_size ?? 8192;
        *string sout;
        *binary bout;
        while (True) {
            *data chunk = is.read(size);
            if (!chunk || !chunk.size()) {
                break;
            }
            if (chunk.typeCode() == NT_STRING) {
                if (sout) {
                    sout += chunk;
                } else {
                    sout = chunk;
                }
            } else {
                binary b = binary(chunk);
                if (bout) {
                    bout += b;
                } else {
                    bout = b;
                }
            }
        }
        return sout ? sout : (bout ? bout : "");
    }

    # chunked_allowed: False for a response to an HTTP/1.0 request, where a streamed message body is delimited by
    # closing the connection instead of chunked transfer encoding
    # log_info: if True, the request's INFO messages are logged (RequestHelper::log_info)
    private:internal doResponse(HttpListener listener, Socket s, hash<auto> cx, hash rv, bool chunked_allowed,
            *ResponseAfterSendNotifier after_send, *reference<hash<HttpAsyncResponseInfo>> async_response_out,
            bool log_info) {
        # Handle send_callback responses (e.g. DataStream protocol)
        if (rv.send_callback) {
            # Capture callback for the response-sending code
            async_response_out = <HttpAsyncResponseInfo>{
                "code": rv."code",
                "hdr": rv.hdr ?? {},
                "send_callback": rv.send_callback,
                "send_timeout": rv.send_timeout,
                "close": cx.close ?? False,
                "cx": cx,
                "listener": listener,
                "after_send": after_send,
            };
            # the response is logged by the code that sends it (translateToRequestResult(), sendResponseSync(),
            # sendMultiplexedResponse(), sendMultiplexedStreamingResponseInline()); logging it here logged it twice
            return;
        }

        # RFC 9113 §8.2.2 / RFC 9114 §4.2: Transfer-Encoding is prohibited in HTTP/2 and HTTP/3;
        # strip it before the chunked check to prevent the body from being needlessly converted
        # to a BinaryInputStream (which would trigger the streaming response path)
        if (cx."header-info".http2 || cx."header-info".http3) {
            remove rv.hdr."Transfer-Encoding";
        }

        bool chunked = rv.chunked_body || (rv.body && (rv.hdr."Transfer-Encoding"
            || (rv.body instanceof InputStream && !rv.hdr."Content-Length")));
        auto body = rv.body;

        # text is sent in the character encoding that the response declares
        string text_encoding = getResponseTextEncoding(rv.hdr."Content-Type", cx."response-encoding"
            ?? s.getEncoding());
        if (body.typeCode() == NT_STRING && !HttpServer::sameEncoding(body.encoding(), text_encoding)) {
            body = convert_encoding(body, text_encoding);
        }

        if (chunked) {
            body = rv.chunked_body ?? body;
            if (body instanceof StringInputStream) {
                StringInputStream input_stream = cast<StringInputStream>(body);
                string stream_encoding = input_stream.getEncoding();
                if (!HttpServer::sameEncoding(stream_encoding, text_encoding)) {
                    body = new EncodingConversionInputStream(input_stream: input_stream,
                        source_encoding: stream_encoding, target_encoding: text_encoding);
                }
            } else if ((body instanceof string) || (body instanceof binary)) {
                body = new BinaryInputStream(body);
            }
            if (chunked_allowed) {
                rv.hdr."Transfer-Encoding" = "chunked";
            } else {
                # the stream is sent as-is, and sendReply() has set the close flag to delimit it
                chunked = False;
            }
        }

        # Capture response info for async sending
        # Preserve InputStream so the controller can send it
        # with proper chunked framing; the controller handles thread reassignment
        *InputStream is;
        if (body instanceof InputStream) {
            is = body;
            # Architectural rule: the I/O thread must NEVER call readHelper()
            # on a non-I/O-thread-safe InputStream.  Enforcement happens
            # downstream: sendMultiplexedResponse() and the async I/O
            # controller's handleHttp{2,3}MultiplexedResult() spawn a
            # @ref StreamResponseProducer "StreamResponseProducer" on a
            # dedicated worker thread for any non-I/O-thread-safe stream,
            # so read() is always invoked on a worker — never on the I/O
            # thread.  Here we only unassign thread affinity so the
            # downstream worker can claim the stream via reassignThread().
            is.unassignThread();
            body = NOTHING;
        }
        # the message is built only if it is logged; whether it is was decided when the request started
        if (log_info) {
            listener.log("doResponse: capturing async response code=%d body_size=%d has_stream=%y",
                rv."code", body ? body.size() : 0, exists is);
        }
        async_response_out = <HttpAsyncResponseInfo>{
            "code": rv."code",
            "hdr": rv.hdr ?? {},
            "body": body,
            "input_stream": is,
            "chunked": chunked,
            "close": cx.close ?? False,
            "cx": cx,
            "listener": listener,
            "after_send": after_send,
        };
        # the response is logged by the code that sends it, as above; an event stream and a protocol upgrade are sent by
        # sendReply() itself, which does not log them
        if (rv."code" == 101 || rv.hdr."Content-Type" == MimeTypeSse || (rv.sse_source && rv.sse_source.isUsed())) {
            listener.logResponse(cx, rv);
        }
    }
    #! @endcond
}

class HttpPersistentHandlerInfo {
    public {
        *DynamicHandlerHelper dhh;
        *HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler handler;
    }

    destructor() {
        try {
            clear();
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            # ignore PROGRAM-ERROR exceptions in the destructor
            if (ex.err != "PROGRAM-ERROR") {
                rethrow;
            }
        }
    }

    assign(*DynamicHandlerHelper n_dhh, HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler n_handler) {
        dhh = n_dhh;
        handler = n_handler;
    }

    clear() {
        if (handler) {
            # This object is assigned only after the handler has opted into
            # persistence.  Always deliver its close notification: thread
            # resource cleanup or a final request can clear the handler's
            # transient persistence flag before this owner-thread teardown
            # runs, but that must not suppress the already-registered cleanup
            # callback.  persistentClosed() is a no-op when the notification
            # was explicitly removed.
            handler.persistentClosed();
            # Clear any remaining per-thread persistence map entry so a reused
            # pool thread cannot appear persistent on this handler.
            handler.clearPersistentThread();
            remove handler;
            remove dhh;
        }
    }
}

#! Implements listeners for the @ref HttpServer::HttpServer "HttpServer" class
public class HttpListener inherits Qore::Socket, HttpServer::HttpListenerInterface {
    public {
        # TID of the background listener thread
        int tid;

        # default handler info
        *HandlerInfo defaultHandler;
    }

    private {
        HttpServer serv;
        #! private request-handling method from the server
        code<nothing(...)> handleRequest;
        Sequence ss;

        *hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> opts;

        #*SSLCertificate cert;
        #*SSLPrivateKey key;
        bool ssl = False;
        auto socket;
        hash socket_info;

        # connection WaitGroup for normal connection threads
        WaitGroup cThreads();
        # connection WaitGroup for dedicated socket threads
        WaitGroup dThreads();

        bool exit = False;
        bool stopped = False;
        #! set once the listener's dedicated socket handlers have been signalled to stop
        /** Registrations are refused from this point on.  Without it, a dedicated socket registered
            after signalHandlersToStop() has taken its snapshot is never signalled, and the handler
            pool's stopWait() then waits for a task that will never exit.  This cannot reuse \c exit,
            because \c exit also makes HttpListener::stop() return immediately, which would skip the
            listener socket shutdown.
        */
        bool no_new_dedicated = False;
        int id;

        # socket handler hash
        hash<string, AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface> shh;

        # mutex
        Mutex m();

        #! listener-specific logger; if not present, then the server's logger will be used
        *LoggerInterface logger;

        # stop notification closure
        *code<nothing(string)> stopc;

        string name;

        # log recv headers flag
        bool log_recv_headers = False;

        # log recv body flag
        bool log_recv_body = False;

        # log send headers flag
        bool log_send_headers = False;

        # log send body flag
        bool log_send_body = False;

        #! get remote certificates
        bool get_remote_certs = False;

        #! SSL verify flags
        int ssl_verify_flags = SSL_VERIFY_NONE;

        #! accept all certificates
        bool ssl_accept_all_certs = True;

        const PollInterval = 250ms;
        const ListenQueue = 1024;
        const BodyLogLimit = 40;

        # handler ref count
        int handler_ref_cnt = 0;

        # listener-specific handlers
        HttpHandlerList handlers();

        #! map for converting ssl verify flags to strings
        const hash<string, string> SslVerifyMap = {
            SSL_VERIFY_PEER: "SSL_VERIFY_PEER",
            SSL_VERIFY_FAIL_IF_NO_PEER_CERT: "SSL_VERIFY_FAIL_IF_NO_PEER_CERT",
            SSL_VERIFY_CLIENT_ONCE: "SSL_VERIFY_CLIENT_ONCE",
        };
    }

    #! Creates the object with the given parameters
    /**
        @param server the HTTP server
        @param handleRequest the server's request handling code reference
        @param id the listener ID
        @param ss the listener connection sequence
        @param opts listener options
    */
    constructor(HttpServer server, code<nothing(...)> handleRequest,int id, Sequence ss,
            *hash<HttpListenerOptionInfo> opts) {
        if (!opts.name.val()) {
            throw "HTTPLISTENER-CONSTRUCTOR-ERROR", "Missing required listener option: name";
        }
        self.name = opts.name;
        self.handleRequest = handleRequest;
        if (opts.cert_loc && !opts.cert) {
            opts += http_get_ssl_objects(opts.cert_loc, opts.key_loc, opts.key_password);
        }
        self.opts = opts;

        # we originally set for utf-8 and then per-connection the encoding is set according to the client's
        # content-type header (with iso-8859-1 as the default)
        # according to RFC 2616 section 3.7.1 (http://tools.ietf.org/html/rfc2616#section-3.7.1)
        setEncoding("utf-8");

        # save a weak reference to the server object
        serv := server;

        # set other params
        self.id = id;
        self.ss = ss;
        self.logger = opts.logger;
        self.stopc = opts.stopc;

        if (opts.handler_info) {
            addHandlers(opts.handler_info);
        }

        # create SSL certificate and private key objects from PEM files if passed
        # Don't configure SSL on the listener socket because async I/O uses
        # startPollAccept() which doesn't properly handle client certificate
        # verification. Instead, each accepted connection is manually upgraded to SSL.
        if (opts.cert) {
            if (opts.get_remote_certs) {
                # issue #3512: enable remote certificates to be retrieved by default for HTTPS listeners
                # get certificates from peers
                ssl_verify_flags = opts.ssl_verify_flags |= SSL_VERIFY_PEER;
                get_remote_certs = True;
            }
            if (opts.ssl_verify_flags) {
                ssl_verify_flags = opts.ssl_verify_flags;
            }
            if (exists opts.ssl_accept_all_certs) {
                ssl_accept_all_certs = opts.ssl_accept_all_certs;
            }
            ssl = True;
        }

        if (opts.no_bind) {
            # QUIC listener mode: skip bind/listen (network I/O uses a separate UDP socket)
            socket = opts.name;
            socket_info = {
                "ssl": True,
                "name": name,
                "id": id,
            } + opts.ext_info;
            socket_info.desc = name;
        } else {
            # make INET (tcp) bind
            if (exists opts.service) {
                bindINET(opts.node, opts.service, True, opts.family);
                socket = sprintf("%s:%s", opts.node ?? "*", opts.service);
            } else {
                socket = opts.node;
                if (opts.node =~ /^\//) {
                    # try to unlink the file first if it's a UNIX domain socket
                    unlink(opts.node);
                    bindUNIX(opts.node);
                } else {
                    if (bind(opts.node, True)) {
                        throw "HTTPLISTENER-BIND-ERROR", strerror();
                    }
                }
            }

            # save socket port/path
            socket_info = getSocketInfo(False) + {
                "ssl": ssl,
                "name": name,
                "id": id,
            } + opts.ext_info;

            # make 'desc' key for logging
            socket_info.desc = socket_info.address_desc;
            if (socket_info.port) {
                socket_info.desc += ":" + socket_info.port;
            }

            # set listening state on socket
            if (listen(ListenQueue)) {
                throw "HTTP-LISTEN-ERROR", sprintf("listen error %d on socket %s: %s", errno(), socket, strerror());
            }
        }

    }

    #! Returns a hash of extra headers to be added to all responses from this listener
    *hash<auto> getDefaultResponseHeaders() {
        return serv.getDefaultResponseHeaders();
    }

    #! Reloads the HTTPS certificate from the original location
    /** Subsequent connections will use the new certificate definition; the listener must have been started with the
        location information for the X.509 certificate and private key, or a REFRESH-CERTIFICATE error is raised

        @throw REFRESH-CERTIFICATE-ERROR not an HTTPS listener or certificate location information not present

        @since HttpServer 1.1
    */
    reloadCertificate() {
        if (!ssl) {
            throw "REFRESH-CERTIFICATE-ERROR", sprintf("listener %y (id %d) is not an HTTPS listener", name, id);
        }
        if (!opts.cert_loc) {
            throw "REFRESH-CERTIFICATE-ERROR", sprintf("listener %y (id %d) cannot reload the certificate from the "
                "source location as no source location was provided for the current certificate; the certificate was "
                "provided in binary format when the listener was started; in order to reload certificates at "
                "runtime, make sure to use APIs that provide the certificate location when starting the listener",
                name, id);
        }

        on_error rethrow "REFRESH-CERTIFICATE-ERROR", sprintf("%s: %s", $1.err, $1.desc);
        hash<HttpCertInfo> info = http_get_ssl_objects(opts.cert_loc, opts.key_loc, opts.key_password);
        # update the listener Socket's own cert/key so any code path that reads them
        # via the Socket inheritance gets the new values
        setCertificateAndPrivateKey(info.cert, info.key);
        # also keep the new objects on the listener-options snapshot so getInfo()
        # / getSslCertificateObject() agree with what the async controller will use
        opts.cert = info.cert;
        opts.key = info.key;
        # in async-I/O mode every replenished accept poll operation reads the
        # cert/key out of the controller's listeners map; without this push the
        # controller keeps handing out the original cert/key references
        # forever and TLS handshakes for new connections quietly stall
        # (HTTPCLIENT-NEGOTIATE-TIMEOUT after 20 s).
        *HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpAsyncSocketIoController async_ctrl = serv ? serv.getHttpAsyncIo() : NOTHING;
        if (async_ctrl) {
            async_ctrl.reloadListenerCertificate(self, info.cert, info.key);
        }
    }

    #! add handlers to the listener
    addHandlers(hash<string, hash<HttpHandlerConfigInfo>> handler_info) {
        AutoLock al(m);
        #printf("HttpListener::addHandlers() hi: %y\n", hi);
        bool def = handlers.empty();
        map handlers.setHandler($1.key, $1.value), handler_info.pairIterator();
        map ++handler_ref_cnt, handler_info.iterator();
        if (def) {
            setDefaultHandler(handler_info.firstKey());
        }
    }

    /** Remove handler from the listener.
        @throw INVALID-HANDLER-ERROR handler is not present in the listener's handler list
    */
    removeHandler(string handler_name) {
        AutoLock al(m);
        --handler_ref_cnt;

        if (defaultHandler && defaultHandler.name == handler_name) {
            remove defaultHandler;
        }
        handlers.removeHandler(handler_name);
    }

    /** Remove handler from the listener.
        @throw INVALID-HANDLER-ERROR handler is not present in the listener's handler list
    */
    removeHandler(HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler handler) {
        AutoLock al(m);
        --handler_ref_cnt;

        if (defaultHandler && defaultHandler.obj == handler) {
            remove defaultHandler;
        }
        handlers.removeHandler(handler);
    }

    setDefaultHandler(string name) {
        if (!handlers.handlers{name})
            throw "HTTPLISTENER-SETDEFAULTHANDLER-ERROR", sprintf("handler %y is unknown (known handlers: %y)", name,
                handlers.handlers.keys());

        defaultHandler = handlers.handlers{name};
    }

    *HandlerInfo findHandler(hash<auto> hdr, reference<int> score, bool finalv = False, *reference<string> root_path) {
        return handlers.findHandler(hdr, \score, finalv, root_path ? \root_path : NOTHING);
    }

    bool hasHandlers() {
        return !handlers.empty();
    }

    #! Returns the handler for inline check (no side effects)
    /** Looks up the handler for the given request headers without creating
        DynamicHandlerHelper objects or modifying any state.

        @param hdr parsed HTTP headers
        @return the handler if found, NOTHING otherwise, including for a request that the server rejects without
        calling a handler (see @ref HttpServer::acceptsHttpMethod() "HttpServer::acceptsHttpMethod()")

        @note Only returns permanent (non-dynamic) handlers. Dynamic handlers
        require lifecycle tracking that is incompatible with the inline path.

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    *AbstractHttpRequestHandler findHandlerForInlineCheck(hash<auto> hdr) {
        # the full path rejects the HTTP/2 connection preface and unsupported methods before calling a handler
        if (!serv.acceptsHttpMethod(hdr.method)) {
            return;
        }
        int score = 0;
        string root_path;
        *HandlerInfo hi;
        if (hasHandlers()) {
            hi = findHandler(hdr, \score, True, \root_path);
            if (!hi) {
                hi = defaultHandler;
            }
        } else {
            hi = serv.findGlobalHandler(hdr);
        }
        if (hi) {
            return hi.obj;
        }
        return NOTHING;
    }

    #! Lightweight inline request processing — no context setup, logging, auth
    /** This method processes a request on the async callback dispatcher worker, bypassing
        the full processNativeRequest() path. It is only called when the handler's
        canHandleInline() returns True.

        @param sock the client socket
        @param cx the connection context (peer info only)
        @param hdr parsed HTTP headers
        @param handler the handler to use
        @param header_info header parsing info including the \c close flag for connection semantics

        @return result info for the controller to act on

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    hash<HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo> processInlineRequest(Socket sock, hash<auto> cx,
            hash<auto> hdr, AbstractHttpRequestHandler handler, hash<auto> header_info) {
        try {
            hash<HttpResponseInfo> resp = handler.handleInlineRequest(cx, hdr, NOTHING);
            if ((resp.body instanceof InputStream) || resp.chunked_body || resp.send_callback) {
                throw "INLINE-HANDLER-ERROR", "the handler returned a streamed message body; an inline handler must "
                    "return the message body as a string or binary value";
            }
            # the handler can close the connection, but it cannot keep open one that the request requires to close
            # (RFC 9112 section 9.3); "close" is never NOTHING in a hash<HttpResponseInfo>
            bool close = resp.close || header_info.close.toBool() || !sock.isOpen();
            *hash<auto> resp_hdr = http_canonicalize_reply_header_names(resp.hdr);
            # the message body is in memory, so it is sent with a Content-Length
            remove resp_hdr."Transfer-Encoding";
            # as with http_set_reply_headers(), tell the client whether the connection persists unless the handler
            # did; an HTTP/1.0 client assumes that it does not without "Connection: Keep-Alive"
            if (!resp_hdr.Connection) {
                resp_hdr.Connection = close ? "close" : "Keep-Alive";
            }
            # a response to a HEAD request and a 1xx, 204, or 304 response have no message body (RFC 9110 section
            # 6.4.1)
            *data body = resp.body;
            if (hdr.method == "HEAD" || resp.code < 200 || resp.code == 204 || resp.code == 304) {
                body = NOTHING;
            }
            # the controller sends the response and reports its transmission result
            *code<nothing(bool)> after_send;
            if (resp.after_send) {
                ResponseAfterSendNotifier notifier(resp.after_send, cx, handler, self);
                after_send = sub (bool sent) {
                    notifier.notify(sent);
                };
            }
            return <HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo>{
                "code": resp.code,
                "status_message": HttpCodes{resp.code},
                "hdr": resp_hdr,
                "body": body,
                "close": close,
                "return_to_idle": !close,
                "ttl": serv.getTtl(),
                "after_send": after_send,
            };
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            string err = sprintf("inline handler %s: %s: %s", handler.className(), ex.err, ex.desc);
            logError("%s", err);
            return <HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo>{
                "code": 500,
                "status_message": HttpCodes{500},
                "hdr": {
                    "Content-Type": MimeTypeText,
                    "Connection": "close",
                },
                "body": hdr.method == "HEAD" ? NOTHING : err,
                "close": True,
            };
        }
    }

    #! stop listeners if there are no handlers
    /** @param listener listener to stop
        @return whether listener was stopped
    */
    static bool stopIfNoHandlers(HttpServer::HttpListener listener) {
        listener.m.lock();
        on_error listener.m.unlock();

        if (listener.handler_ref_cnt == 0) {
            listener.m.unlock();
            listener.stop();
            return True;
        }
        listener.m.unlock();
        return False;
    }

    hash getListenerSocketInfo() {
        return socket_info;
    }

    copy() {
        throw "COPY-ERROR", "this object cannot be copied";
    }

    destructor() {
        stop();
    }

    logRecvHeaders(softbool flag = True) {
        log_recv_headers = flag;
    }

    logRecvBody(softbool flag = True) {
        log_recv_body = flag;
    }

    logSendHeaders(softbool flag = True) {
        log_send_headers = flag;
    }

    logSendBody(softbool flag = True) {
        log_send_body = flag;
    }

    # Returns True if informational messages of this listener will be logged
    private bool isLogInfoEnabled() {
        return logger ? logger.isEnabledFor(LoggerLevel::INFO) : serv.isLogInfoEnabled();
    }

    doLogRecvBody(*data body, hash<auto> cx, hash<auto> hdr, hash<auto> info) {
        # INVARIANT: arguments of a log message are masked and formatted only if it will be logged
        if (!isLogInfoEnabled()) {
            return;
        }
        if (body) {
            string http_ver = hdr.http_version =~ /^HTTP\// ? hdr.http_version : "HTTP/" + hdr.http_version;
            string lstr = sprintf("cid %d src %y: %s %s %s body (%db): ", cx.id, cx."peer-info".address_desc,
                hdr.method, cx.masked_path, http_ver, body.size());
            if (body.typeCode() == NT_STRING) {
                try {
                    string bstr = body.size() > BodyLogLimit ? body.substr(0, BodyLogLimit) + "..." : body;
                    # attempt to mask sensitive data in the message body
                    bstr = serv.maskData(bstr);
                    bstr = replace(bstr, "\n", "\\n");
                    bstr = replace(bstr, "\r", "\\r");
                    bstr = replace(bstr, "\t", "\\t");
                    lstr += sprintf("'%s'", bstr);
                } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                    if (ex.err == "INVALID-ENCODING") {
                        lstr += sprintf("error logging msg body: %s: %s", ex.err, ex.desc);
                        string etxt = serv.getDebug()
                            ? sprintf("%s: %s: %s", get_ex_pos(ex), ex.err, ex.desc)
                            : get_exception_string(ex);
                        string str = sprintf("%s: received from %s via %s", etxt, info.address_desc,
                            socket_info.address_desc);
                        logError("%s", str);
                        logError("hdr=%y", http_mask_value(hdr));
                    }
                }
            } else {
                lstr += "<bin>";
            }
            log("%s", lstr);
        }

        if (log_recv_body) {
            if (body && (hdr."content-type" == MimeTypeOctetStream || hdr."content-encoding")) {
                log("cid %d: RECV BODY: <%s>", cx.id, body.toHex());
            } else {
                log("cid %d: RECV BODY: %s", cx.id, getMaskedBodyLogString(body));
            }
        }
    }

    hash<auto> getLogOptions() {
        return {
            "recv_headers": log_recv_headers,
            "recv_body": log_recv_body,
            "send_headers": log_send_headers,
            "send_body": log_send_body,
        };
    }

    string getName() {
        return name;
    }

    auto getAddress() {
        return socket;
    }

    int getID() {
        return id;
    }

    bool isSecure() {
        return ssl;
    }

    #! Returns the effective maximum request body size for this listener
    /** Returns the listener-specific limit if set, otherwise the server-level default.

        @return the maximum body size in bytes; 0 means unlimited

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    int getMaxRequestBodySize() {
        int rv = opts.max_request_body_size ?? 0;
        if (!rv) {
            rv = serv.getMaxRequestBodySize();
        }
        return rv;
    }

    #! Returns the bound in bytes of the data held for the client of each Server-Sent Events stream of the listener
    /** @see @ref HttpServer::HttpListenerOptionInfo::sse_max_buffer_size "HttpListenerOptionInfo::sse_max_buffer_size"

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    int getSseMaxBufferSize() {
        return opts.sse_max_buffer_size ?? HttpServerAsyncIo::DefaultSseMaxBufferSize;
    }

    #! Logs a warning in the listener's log
    /** @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    logWarn(string fmt, ...) {
        softlist<auto> args = fmt;
        if (argv) {
            args += argv;
        }
        args[0] = sprintf("%y (%s): %s", name, socket_info.desc, args[0]);
        if (logger) {
            try {
                logger.logArgs(LoggerLevel::WARN, "HTTP " + args[0], args[1..]);
                return;
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                # falls back to the server's error log
            }
        }
        if (serv) {
            args[0] = "WARNING: " + args[0];
            try {
                serv.logErrorArgs(args);
            } catch () {
                # ignore if server was deleted
            }
        }
    }

    #! Returns the effective maximum size in bytes of a decompressed request body for this listener
    /** Returns the listener-specific limit if set, otherwise the server-level limit if set, otherwise the effective
        maximum request body size of the listener if set, otherwise
        @ref HttpServer::DefaultMaxDecodedBodySize "DefaultMaxDecodedBodySize".

        @return the maximum size in bytes of a decompressed request body; a negative value means no limit

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    int getMaxDecodedBodySize() {
        int rv = opts.max_decoded_body_size ?? 0;
        if (!rv) {
            rv = serv.getMaxDecodedBodySize();
        }
        if (!rv) {
            rv = getMaxRequestBodySize();
        }
        return rv ?: DefaultMaxDecodedBodySize;
    }

    #! Returns the SSL certificate object if configured
    /** @return the SSL certificate or NOTHING if not an SSL listener
    */
    *SSLCertificate getSslCertificate() {
        return opts.cert;
    }

    #! Returns the SSL private key object if configured
    /** @return the SSL private key or NOTHING if not an SSL listener
    */
    *SSLPrivateKey getSslPrivateKey() {
        return opts.key;
    }

    hash<auto> getInfo() {
        return {
            "name": name,
         } + socket_info + {
            "proto": ssl ? "https" : "http",
            "id": id,
            "bind": socket,
            "get_remote_certs": get_remote_certs,
            "ssl_verify_flags": getSslVerifyModeList(),
            "ssl_accept_all_certs": ssl_accept_all_certs,
         };
    }

    private setupSslIntern() {
        if (!opts.key) {
            throw "HTTP-ADDLISTENER-ERROR", sprintf("SSL certificate given without private key for listener %y",
                name);
        }
        # set SSL cert and key
        setCertificate(opts.cert);
        setPrivateKey(opts.key);
    }

    private softlist<string> getSslVerifyModeList() {
        return (map $1.value, SslVerifyMap.pairIterator(), ssl_verify_flags & $1.key.toInt()) ?* "SSL_VERIFY_NONE";
    }

    #! Returns the SSL verify flags
    /** @return the SSL verification flags (e.g. SSL_VERIFY_PEER)

        @since HttpServer 1.5
    */
    int getSslVerifyFlags() {
        return ssl_verify_flags;
    }

    #! Returns the SSL certificate object
    /** @return the SSL certificate object, or NOTHING if not configured

        @since HttpServer 1.5
    */
    *SSLCertificate getSslCertificateObject() {
        return opts.cert;
    }

    #! Returns the SSL private key object
    /** @return the SSL private key object, or NOTHING if not configured

        @since HttpServer 1.5
    */
    *SSLPrivateKey getSslPrivateKeyObject() {
        return opts.key;
    }

    #! Returns whether to accept all client certificates (including self-signed)
    /** @return True if all client certificates should be accepted

        @since HttpServer 1.5
    */
    bool getSslAcceptAllCerts() {
        return ssl_accept_all_certs;
    }

    #! Returns whether remote (client) certificate capture is enabled
    /** @return True if remote certificates should be captured during the SSL handshake

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    bool getGetRemoteCerts() {
        return get_remote_certs;
    }

    #! Returns whether this listener is configured for SSL/TLS
    /** @return True if this listener uses SSL/TLS

        @note This returns the configured SSL status, not whether the socket has SSL configured.
        SSL is configured per-connection rather than on the listener socket, so this method
        should be used instead of isSecure() for checking SSL status.

        @since HttpServer 1.5
    */
    bool isSsl() {
        return ssl;
    }

    #! Returns the HTTP version mode for this listener
    /** @return the HTTP version mode: "auto", "1.0", "1.1", "2.0", or "3.0"

        @note If get_global_http2_mode() returns "disabled", HTTP/2 is not offered even if the listener is
        configured with "auto" or "2.0"

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    string getHttpVersion() {
        string http_version = opts.http_version ?? "auto";
        # Check global HTTP/2 mode - if disabled, don't offer HTTP/2
        if (http_version == "auto" || http_version == "2.0" || http_version == "2") {
            if (get_global_http2_mode() == "disabled") {
                return "1.1";
            }
        }
        return http_version;
    }

    auto removeUserThreadContext(*string k) {
        if (!exists k) {
            remove_thread_data("uctx");
            return;
        }
        *hash<auto> h = get_thread_data("uctx");
        auto rv = remove h{k};
        save_thread_data("uctx", h);
        return rv;
    }

    addUserThreadContext(hash<auto> uctx) {
        save_thread_data("uctx", get_thread_data("uctx") + uctx);
    }

    #! Force-closes the persistent session for the given connection
    /** Override of @ref HttpServer::HttpListenerInterface::signalPersistentSessionClose();
        delegates to the underlying async-I/O controller's
        @ref HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpAsyncSocketIoController::signalPersistentQueuesForConnection(),
        which pushes a \c "close" marker into the dedicated persistent
        handler queue identified by @c uh, unblocking the dedicated thread
        if it is parked in @c Queue::get().  The dedicated thread's
        persistent loop then exits and its @c on_exit runs the normal
        cleanup path (@c removeUserThreadContext() + @c persistentClosed()),
        releasing any handler-attached user thread context on the correct
        (listener) thread.  This method returns only after that cleanup
        completes, preventing the close-notification program or its resources
        from being unloaded while the callback is still queued.

        Safe to call from any thread; idempotent (no-op if no queue is
        registered under @c uh, e.g. when the dedicated thread has already
        exited).

        @param uh the connection unique hash identifying the persistent
            session to close
    */
    signalPersistentSessionClose(softstring uh) {
        *HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpAsyncSocketIoController ctrl = serv
            ? serv.getHttpAsyncIo() : NOTHING;
        if (ctrl) {
            Queue close_ack(1);
            if (ctrl.signalPersistentQueuesForConnection(uh, close_ack)) {
                close_ack.get();
            }
        }
    }

    stopNoWait() {
        background stop();
    }

    stop(*bool silent) {
        {
            m.lock();
            on_exit m.unlock();

            if (stopped) {
                return;
            }

            if (exit) {
                return;
            }

            exit = True;
        }

        # wait for all connection threads to terminate
        cThreads.wait();

        # Cancel async accept operations BEFORE closing the listener socket.
        #
        # The I/O controller has pending accept operations on this
        # listener's fd.  If we close the fd first, the I/O thread sees errors on
        # the closed fd and fires error callbacks that attempt to replenish accept
        # ops on the dead socket.  Those replenishments fail, but the error path
        # runs on the shared I/O thread and can interfere with operations for OTHER
        # listeners on the same controller.
        #
        # By cancelling first, the callbacks fire with canceled=True (clean path,
        # no replenishment), and the fd is removed from the event loop before close.
        # listenerStopped() will skip the redundant removeListener() call.
        if (serv) {
            serv.removeAsyncListener(self);
        }

        # Close the listener socket to release the bound port.
        shutdown();
        close();

        # stop all dedicated socket connections
        {
            # their protocol's closing handshake runs first, on all connections at the same time
            closeHandlerConnections(getDedicatedSocketHandlers(), id, silent ? NOTHING : sub (string fmt, ...) {
                logError("%s", vsprintf(fmt, argv));
            });

            *hash<string, AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface> shh_copy;
            {
                m.lock();
                on_exit m.unlock();

                shh_copy = remove shh;
            }

            foreach AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface handler in (shh_copy.iterator()) {
                try {
                    handler.stop(id);
                } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                    if (!silent) {
                        logError("error stopping handler on listener %d: %s: %s", id, ex.err, ex.desc);
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        # wait for all dedicated connection threads to terminate
        dThreads.wait();

        stopped = True;

        # Notify the server BEFORE logging, because log() may access the server
        # via the weak 'serv' reference, and if the server's last strong reference
        # has been dropped externally (e.g. stopNoWait() + caller scope exit), the
        # log call can trigger server destruction.  The server destructor calls
        # stop() -> c.wait(), so c.done() (inside listenerStopped) must complete
        # first to avoid a deadlock.
        if (serv) {
            serv.listenerStopped(self);
            if (stopc) {
                serv.callListenerStopCallback(stopc, name, socket_info);
            }
        }

        if (!silent) {
            log("stopped listener");
        }
    }

    string getResponseLogString(hash<auto> cx, int status_code, *data body, *hash<auto> hdr) {
        # do log msg
        string http_ver = cx."header-info".http3 ? "HTTP/3"
            : cx."header-info".http2 ? "HTTP/2" : "HTTP/1.1";
        string lstr = sprintf("cid %d: %s %d %s", cx.id, http_ver, status_code, HttpServer::HttpCodes{status_code});
        if (cx.handler_name)
            lstr += sprintf(" handler: %y", cx.handler_name);
        if (cx.user)
            lstr += sprintf(" user: %y", cx.user);
        if (body)
            lstr += sprintf(" size: %d", body.size());
        if (hdr."Content-Type")
            lstr += sprintf(" CT: %y", hdr."Content-Type");
        return lstr;
    }

    # returns a message body for a log message
    /* INVARIANT: message headers and bodies are logged only after masking; see http_mask_value().  This method does
       not throw: a log message must not make a request fail
    */
    private string getMaskedBodyLogString(auto body) {
        try {
            return serv.maskData(sprintf("%y", http_mask_value(body)));
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            return sprintf("<masked: %s>", ex.err);
        }
    }

    logResponse(hash<auto> cx, int status_code, *data body, *hash<auto> hdr) {
        if (!isLogInfoEnabled()) {
            return;
        }
        # do log msg
        string lstr = getResponseLogString(cx, status_code, body, hdr);
        log("%s", lstr);

        if (log_send_headers)
            log("cid %d: SEND HDR: HTTP code %d: %y", cx.id, status_code, http_mask_value(hdr));
        if (log_send_body)
            log("cid %d: SEND BODY: %s", cx.id, getMaskedBodyLogString(body));
    }

    logResponse(hash<auto> cx, int code, InputStream body, *hash<auto> hdr) {
        if (!isLogInfoEnabled()) {
            return;
        }
        # do log msg
        string http_ver = cx."header-info".http3 ? "HTTP/3"
            : cx."header-info".http2 ? "HTTP/2" : "HTTP/1.1";
        string lstr = sprintf("cid %d: %s %d %s", cx.id, http_ver, code, HttpServer::HttpCodes{code});
        if (cx.handler_name)
            lstr += sprintf(" handler: %y", cx.handler_name);
        if (cx.user)
            lstr += sprintf(" user: %y", cx.user);
        if (hdr."Content-Type")
            lstr += sprintf(" CT: %y", hdr."Content-Type");
        log("%s", lstr);

        if (log_send_headers)
            log("cid %d: SEND HDR: HTTP code %d: %y", cx.id, code, http_mask_value(hdr));
        if (log_send_body)
            log("cid %d: SENT CHUNKED BODY (%s)", cx.id, body.className());
    }

    logResponse(hash<auto> cx, hash<auto> rv) {
        #  "rv" can be a "HttpHandlerResponseInfo", so we can't access "chunked_body" directly
        if (rv.body || !rv.hasKeyValue("chunked_body")) {
            logResponse(cx, rv."code", rv.body, rv.hdr);
        } else {
            logResponse(cx, rv."code", rv.chunked_body, rv.hdr);
        }
    }

    # Called when an HTTP response could not be sent
    logResponseError(hash<auto> cx, hash<auto> rv, hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
        # do log msg
        string lstr = sprintf("ERROR: %s: Could not send response: %s",
            getResponseLogString(cx, rv."code", rv.chunked_body ?? rv.body, rv.hdr),
            !serv.getDebug()
                ? sprintf("%s: %s: %s", get_ex_pos(ex), ex.err, ex.desc)
                : get_exception_string(ex));
        log("%s", lstr);

        if (log_send_headers) {
            log("ERROR: cid %d: FAILED TO SEND HDR: HTTP code %d: %y", cx.id, rv."code",
                http_mask_value(rv.hdr));
        }
        if (log_send_body) {
            log("ERROR: cid %d: FAILED TO SEND BODY: %s", cx.id, getMaskedBodyLogString(rv.chunked_body ?? rv.body));
        }
    }

    log(string fmt, ...) {
        # this is called several times for every request: the message is built only if it is logged, by the
        # listener's logger or, for a listener without one, by the server's
        if (logger ? !logger.isEnabledFor(LoggerLevel::INFO) : (!serv || !serv.isLogInfoEnabled())) {
            return;
        }
        softlist<auto> args = fmt;
        if (argv) {
            args += argv;
        }
        args[0] = sprintf("%y (%s): %s", name, socket_info.desc, args[0]);
        if (logger) {
            args[0] = "HTTP" + (ssl ? "S" : "") + " " + args[0];
            try {
                logger.logArgs(LoggerLevel::INFO, args[0], args[1..]);
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                if (serv) {
                    args[0] = sprintf("cannot write to listener log: %s: %s: msg: %s", ex.err, ex.desc, args[0]);
                    try {
                        serv.logErrorArgs(args);
                    } catch () {
                        # ignore if server was deleted
                    }
                }
            }
        } else if (serv) {
            try {
                serv.logArgs(args);
            } catch () {
                # ignore if server was deleted
            }
        }
    }

    private static string getSslLogSuffix(Socket s, *hash<auto> header_info) {
        string cipher_name;
        string cipher_version;
        if (header_info && header_info.session_id) {
            try {
                cipher_name = s.getQuicSSLCipherName(header_info.session_id) ?? "";
                cipher_version = s.getQuicSSLCipherVersion(header_info.session_id) ?? "";
            } catch () {
                cipher_name = "";
                cipher_version = "";
            }
        }
        if (!cipher_name.val() && !cipher_version.val()) {
            cipher_name = s.getSSLCipherName() ?? "";
            cipher_version = s.getSSLCipherVersion() ?? "";
        }
        trim cipher_name;
        trim cipher_version;
        if (cipher_name !~ /[[:alnum:]]/) {
            cipher_name = "";
        }
        if (cipher_version !~ /[[:alnum:]]/) {
            cipher_version = "";
        }
        if (cipher_name.val() && cipher_version.val()) {
            return sprintf(" %s %s", cipher_name, cipher_version);
        }
        if (cipher_name.val()) {
            return " " + cipher_name;
        }
        if (cipher_version.val()) {
            return " " + cipher_version;
        }
        return "";
    }

    logError(string fmt, ...) {
        softlist<auto> args = fmt;
        if (argv) {
            args += argv;
        }
        args[0] = sprintf("%y (%s): %s", name, socket_info.desc, args[0]);
        if (logger) {
            args[0] = "HTTP " + args[0];
            try {
                logger.logArgs(LoggerLevel::ERROR, args[0], args[1..]);
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                if (serv) {
                    args[0] = sprintf("cannot write to listener log: %s: %s: msg: %s", ex.err, ex.desc, args[0]);
                    try {
                        serv.logErrorArgs(args);
                    } catch () {
                        # ignore if server was deleted
                    }
                }
            }
        } else if (serv) {
            try {
                serv.logErrorArgs(args);
            } catch () {
                # ignore if server was deleted
            }
        }
    }

    #! Returns the dedicated socket handlers of the listener's connections, keyed by their unique hashes
    hash<string, AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface> getDedicatedSocketHandlers() {
        hash<string, AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface> rv = {};
        m.lock();
        on_exit m.unlock();
        foreach AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface h in (shh.iterator()) {
            rv{h.uniqueHash()} = h;
        }
        return rv;
    }

    #! Closes the connections of dedicated socket handlers with their protocol's closing handshake
    /** All the handlers start the handshake on their connections, then the latest of their deadlines is waited for,
        so the wait is at most one handshake timeout in total.

        @param handlers the handlers, keyed by their unique hashes
        @param lid the listener ID, or @ref nothing for all listeners
        @param log_error the error logging function, if any
    */
    static closeHandlerConnections(hash<string, AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface> handlers, *softstring lid,
            *code log_error) {
        *date deadline;
        foreach AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface h in (handlers.iterator()) {
            try {
                *date d = h.startClosingConnections(lid);
                if (d && (!deadline || d > deadline)) {
                    deadline = d;
                }
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                if (log_error) {
                    log_error("error closing dedicated connections: %s: %s", ex.err, ex.desc);
                }
            }
        }
        if (!deadline) {
            return;
        }
        foreach AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface h in (handlers.iterator()) {
            try {
                h.waitForConnectionsClosed(deadline, lid);
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                if (log_error) {
                    log_error("error waiting for dedicated connections to close: %s: %s", ex.err, ex.desc);
                }
            }
        }
    }

    bool registerDedicatedSocket(RequestHelper request_helper, softstring id,
            HttpServer::AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface h) {
        m.lock();
        on_exit m.unlock();

        # a socket registered after the handlers have been signalled would never be told to stop
        if (exit || no_new_dedicated) {
            return False;
        }

        # handover counter value and set dedicated flag
        request_helper.setDedicated();

        # save handler
        shh{id} = h;

        return True;
    }

    removeExternalDedicatedSocket(softstring id) {
        removeDedicatedSocket(id, True);
    }

    removeDedicatedSocket(softstring id, *bool managed_externally) {
        m.lock();
        on_exit m.unlock();

        remove shh{id};
    }

    #! Signals all active dedicated socket handlers to stop without closing the listener
    /** Called during async I/O controller shutdown to ensure streaming handler tasks
        (SSE, WebSocket) exit their polling loops before the handler pool is stopped.

        Unlike @ref stop(), this does not shut down the listener socket or wait for
        connection threads; it only sets the handler-level stop flags.

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    signalHandlersToStop() {
        *hash<string, AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface> shh_copy;
        {
            m.lock();
            on_exit m.unlock();
            # refuse new dedicated sockets before snapshotting, so that nothing can be registered
            # after this point and therefore miss the stop signal below; the handler pool is stopped
            # immediately afterwards and waits for every handler task to exit
            no_new_dedicated = True;
            shh_copy = shh;
        }
        foreach AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface handler in (shh_copy.iterator()) {
            try {
                handler.stop(id);
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                log("signalHandlersToStop: error stopping handler: %s: %s", ex.err, ex.desc);
            }
        }
    }

    #! Returns the context template for new connections
    /** @param info peer info hash with "address", "address_desc", "port" keys
        @return context hash for new connection
    */
    hash<auto> getContextTemplate(hash<auto> info) {
        hash<auto!> cx = {
            "socket": socket,
            "socket-info": socket_info,
            "peer-info": info,
            "id": ss.next(),
            "ssl": ssl,
            "listener-id": id,
            "listener-name": name,
        };
        # Every request context refers to the process-wide async I/O controller, which never refers to a request
        # context, so the reference is opaque: an ordinary one made every scan of a request's data also walk the
        # shared controller and its logger, which every other request's scans walk too
        cx.async_ctrl @= serv.getGlobalAsyncCtrl();
        return cx;
    }

    #! Returns the listener ID
    int getId() {
        return id;
    }

    #! URL-decodes the resource path while preserving the raw query string
    private string decodeRequestPath(string path) {
        int i = path.find("?");
        if (i == -1) {
            return decode_url(path);
        }
        return decode_url(path.substr(0, i)) + path.substr(i);
    }

    #! Returns a masked copy of the request path without changing handler-visible path semantics
    private string maskRequestPath(string raw_path) {
        # Mask sensitive query values before URL decoding so an encoded '&' in
        # a token cannot become a separator and expose the rest of the value.
        string path = mask_sensitive_url_query_values(raw_path);
        try {
            path = decode_url(path);
        } catch () {
            # Keep log masking best-effort; request validation happens on the handler-visible path.
        }
        return serv.maskData(path);
    }

    #! Processes an HTTP request natively for async I/O
    /** This method performs context setup, calls handleRequest() with async response capture,
        and translates the result to HttpRequestResultInfo for the controller to send.

        Response data is returned in the result for the controller to send (HTTP/1.x or
        HTTP/2); this method does not send responses itself.

        @param s the client socket
        @param cx the connection context (will be modified)
        @param hdr the parsed HTTP header (will be modified)
        @param header_info header parsing info

        @return result info for the controller to act on

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    hash<HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo> processNativeRequest(Socket s, hash<auto> cx,
            hash<auto> hdr, hash<auto> header_info) {
        cThreads.add();
        RequestHelper request_helper(cThreads, dThreads);
        request_helper.log_info = isLogInfoEnabled();

        bool is_multiplexed = (header_info.http2 ?? False) || (header_info.http3 ?? False);
        string sock_key;
        HttpPersistentHandlerInfo phi();
        if (is_multiplexed) {
            # HTTP/3 uses "hash:quic:session_id" to isolate each QUIC session;
            # the UDP listener socket is shared by all sessions
            sock_key = header_info.http3
                ? s.uniqueHash() + ":quic:" + header_info.session_id
                : s.uniqueHash();
        } else {
            # HTTP/1.x persistent handlers need this key while the initial
            # request is being dispatched, before the queued persistent loop
            # is registered below.  Deferring uniqueHash() until after
            # handleRequest() left cx.sock_key unset for handler-created
            # thread-affine resources, so an external abort could not signal
            # their owner queue and had to wait for connection close.
            sock_key = s.uniqueHash();
        }

        # Expose the per-connection unique hash on @c cx so handler code can
        # match against the framework's persistent-queue key (e.g. to pass to
        # @ref HttpListenerInterface::signalPersistentSessionClose()).  For
        # HTTP/3 this includes the QUIC session_id suffix matching the
        # multiplexed sock_key derivation above; for HTTP/1.x and HTTP/2 it
        # is the underlying socket's uniqueHash().  Computed and assigned
        # before dispatch so every handler sees the same key the framework
        # uses for its persistent queue.
        cx.sock_key = sock_key;

        # Get peer info for handleRequest
        hash<auto> info = cx."peer-info";
        info.desc = info.address_desc;
        if (info.port) {
            info.desc += ":" + info.port;
        }

        # Process path: decode URL-encoded characters in the resource path only
        cx.raw_path = hdr.path;
        hdr.path = decodeRequestPath(hdr.path);
        cx += {
            "orig_path": hdr.path,
            "masked_path": maskRequestPath(cx.raw_path),
        };

        # Remove leading / if present in path
        if (hdr.path =~ /^\//) {
            splice hdr.path, 0, 1;
        }

        # Set authorization if present
        if (hdr.authorization) {
            cx.auth = hdr.authorization;
        }

        # Save original content-type and process content-type header
        if (hdr."content-type") {
            hdr."_qore_orig_content_type" = hdr."content-type";
        }
        # only overwrite content-type if body-content-type is set (HTTP/2 may not set it)
        if (header_info."body-content-type".val()) {
            hdr."content-type" = header_info."body-content-type";
            if (hdr."content-type".typeCode() == NT_LIST) {
                hdr."content-type" = hdr."content-type"[0];
            }
        }

        # Copy headers to the context
        cx.hdr = hdr;

        # Set encoding info
        cx.char_encoding = s.getEncoding();
        cx."response-encoding" = header_info."accept-charset";

        # Check if we need to close the connection (from HTTP version negotiation)
        cx.close = header_info.close;

        # Log the request; the message is built only if it is logged
        if (request_helper.log_info) {
            string http_ver = hdr.http_version =~ /^HTTP\// ? hdr.http_version : "HTTP/" + hdr.http_version;
            string lstr = sprintf("cid %d src %y: %s %s %s (agent: %y", cx.id,
                cx."peer-info".address_desc, hdr.method, cx.masked_path, http_ver, hdr."user-agent");
            if (hdr."transfer-encoding" == "chunked") {
                lstr += " body: chunked";
            }
            if (hdr."_qore_orig_content_type") {
                lstr += sprintf(" Content-Type: %y", hdr."_qore_orig_content_type");
            }
            if (ssl) {
                lstr += getSslLogSuffix(s, header_info);
            }
            lstr += ")";
            log("%s", lstr);
        }

        # Set user context and header info
        cx.uctx = get_thread_data("uctx");
        # For body_streaming, expose the socket so handlers can read body data
        # incrementally via readQuicStreamDataBlock()
        if (header_info.body_streaming) {
            header_info.sock = s;
        }
        cx."header-info" = header_info;

        # Add client certificate if requested and available
        if (get_remote_certs) {
            if (header_info.http3) {
                # For HTTP/3 (QUIC), the TLS handshake is in the QuicSession, not
                # the listener socket — use getQuicPeerCertificate() with the session ID
                cx."client-cert" = s.getQuicPeerCertificate(header_info.session_id);
            } else {
                cx."client-cert" = s.getRemoteCertificate();
            }
        }

        # Capture the response for the controller to send
        hash<HttpAsyncResponseInfo> async_response = <HttpAsyncResponseInfo>{};

        # Call handleRequest with async capture
        handleRequest(request_helper, self, s, \cx, hdr, hdr.method == "HEAD", phi, info, \async_response);

        # Manage persistent handler state after request processing
        if (is_multiplexed && phi.handler && phi.handler.isPersistent()
                && !request_helper.isDedicated()) {
            # Stream-scoped persistent sessions (issue #5351): multiplexed
            # transports (HTTP/2, HTTP/3) no longer support a connection-scoped
            # multi-request persistent session with a parked dedicated handler
            # thread.  On a pooled/multiplexed connection the connection-close
            # that ends an HTTP/1.x session never arrives, so such a session
            # could only be released by a best-effort end-of-session request;
            # if that was lost the dedicated thread stayed parked forever and
            # its thread-local resources (e.g. an open DB transaction) leaked.
            #
            # A persistent session on a multiplexed transport must instead be a
            # single bidirectional streaming request: the handler opts into
            # body streaming, holds its thread-local resource for the lifetime
            # of that one stream, and reads framed messages from the stream's
            # data queue until the peer half-closes (END_STREAM) or the stream
            # is reset / the connection closes — all transport-deterministic and
            # reliably delivered.  That path runs entirely within the handler's
            # request dispatch and does NOT reach here.
            #
            # Here we therefore only send the initial response (inline for
            # thread-affine streaming responses, mirroring the non-persistent
            # multiplexed path below) and release the thread.  The one-shot
            # streamed-SELECT case clears its own persistence via the
            # RestStreamRequest destructor once the response refs are dropped;
            # any handler still reporting persistent afterwards requested an
            # unsupported connection-scoped session and is torn down here so it
            # cannot leak.
            if (async_response.code) {
                if (async_response.send_callback || async_response.input_stream) {
                    sendMultiplexedStreamingResponseInline(s, header_info,
                        async_response);
                } else {
                    sendMultiplexedResponse(s, header_info, async_response);
                }
            }
            # Drop response refs so any framework objects captured by
            # send_callback / input_stream (e.g. RestStreamRequest) are
            # destroyed and run their destructors before the persistence
            # re-check below — the destructor is what clears persistence for a
            # one-shot streamed SELECT (isPersistent() returns False once
            # send_done is set).
            remove async_response.send_callback;
            remove async_response.input_stream;
            if (header_info.body_streaming) {
                cleanupMultiplexedBodyStream(s, header_info);
            }
            if (phi.handler.isPersistent()) {
                log("cid %d: connection-scoped persistent session is not "
                    "supported on multiplexed transports (HTTP/2, HTTP/3); "
                    "releasing — use a single bidirectional streaming request "
                    "instead", cx.id);
                # Tear down the handler's thread-local context now so the
                # would-be session cannot leak (mirrors the on_exit teardown of
                # the H1 dedicated loop).
                phi.clear();
                removeUserThreadContext();
                remove_thread_data("svc_listener");
            } else {
                log("cid %d: persistence released during initial response "
                    "(multiplexed)", cx.id);
            }
            return <HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo>{};
        } else if (!is_multiplexed && phi.handler && phi.handler.isPersistent()
                && !request_helper.isDedicated()) {
            # For HTTP/1.x, keep this handler thread dedicated for thread
            # affinity, but hand subsequent socket reads back to async I/O.  The
            # controller routes matching requests to the Queue below.
            log("cid %d: handler opted into persistence for %s %s, entering queued persistent mode",
                cx.id, hdr.method, cx.masked_path);
            if (async_response.code) {
                sendResponseSync(s, async_response);
            }
            # See multiplexed branch above: drop response refs so the underlying
            # framework objects (e.g. RestStreamRequest) can run their destructors
            # before the persistent queue loop is entered, releasing handler
            # persistence whose only condition was the in-flight stream.
            remove async_response.send_callback;
            remove async_response.input_stream;
            if (!phi.handler.isPersistent()) {
                log("cid %d: persistence released during initial response, "
                    "skipping queued persistent mode", cx.id);
                return <HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo>{};
            }
            if (cx.close || !s.isOpen()) {
                # the response was sent, and the connection closes after it
                return s.isOpen()
                    ? <HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo>{"close": True}
                    : <HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo>{};
            }

            Queue pq();
            code<nothing(string, Queue)> register_fn =
                header_info.register_persistent_queue;
            # Both explicit session termination and exception cleanup can deregister this queue. Bind
            # cleanup to its identity so a delayed exit cannot remove a new session on the same socket.
            code<nothing(string)> deregister_fn = sub (string key) {
                header_info.deregister_persistent_queue(key, pq);
            };
            register_fn(sock_key, pq);
            on_exit deregister_fn(sock_key);
            if (!cx.close && s.isOpen()) {
                serv.returnConnectionToAsyncIo(s, cx, self);
            }
            # Continue handling queued requests on this thread until persistence ends
            handleQueuedPersistentConnection(request_helper, pq, s, \cx, phi, info,
                deregister_fn, sock_key);
            # Persistent handler managed the socket lifecycle; return empty result
            return <HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo>{};
        }

        # If handler consumed the response (e.g., WebSocket/SSE upgrade sent the 101
        # response directly and deleted async_response), the connection is now managed
        # by a dedicated handler
        if (!async_response) {
            if (request_helper.isDedicated()) {
                return <HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo>{"dedicated": True};
            }
            # Response was sent directly by the handler
            return <HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo>{};
        }

        if (is_multiplexed && async_response.code
                && (async_response.send_callback
                    || (async_response.input_stream
                        && async_response.input_stream instanceof CallbackInputStream))
                && !request_helper.isDedicated()) {
            # send_callback / CallbackInputStream is Qore handler code and
            # may close over thread-local iterators or reservations.  Drive
            # it on the request thread; plain InputStream responses still use
            # StreamResponseProducer below unless the handler is persistent.
            sendMultiplexedStreamingResponseInline(s, header_info, async_response);
            remove async_response.send_callback;
            remove async_response.input_stream;
            if (header_info.body_streaming) {
                cleanupMultiplexedBodyStream(s, header_info);
            }
            return <HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo>{};
        }

        # Translate result to HttpRequestResultInfo for the controller
        return translateToRequestResult(async_response, request_helper, cx, s);
    }

    #! Translates async response data into HttpRequestResultInfo for the controller
    /** @param async_response the captured async response data
        @param request_helper the request lifecycle helper
        @param cx the connection context
        @param s the client socket

        @return result info for the controller to act on
    */
    private hash<HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo> translateToRequestResult(
            hash<HttpAsyncResponseInfo> async_response, RequestHelper request_helper,
            hash<auto> cx, Socket s) {
        bool is_multiplexed = (cx."header-info".http2 ?? False) || (cx."header-info".http3 ?? False);

        # a response without a message exchange to send reports that it was not sent
        on_exit if (async_response.after_send) {
            async_response.after_send.notify(False);
        }

        # Handler set up a streaming connection (SSE or WebSocket)
        if (async_response.streaming) {
            return <HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo>{
                "streaming": True,
                "stream_type": async_response.stream_type,
                "stream_operation": async_response.stream_operation,
                "code": async_response.code,
                "status_message": HttpServer::HttpCodes{async_response.code},
                "hdr": async_response.hdr,
            };
        }

        if (async_response.code && !request_helper.isDedicated()) {
            # Normal response - return response data for controller to send
            hash<auto> hdr = async_response.hdr ?? {};
            if (is_multiplexed) {
                # HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 use DATA frames, not chunked Transfer-Encoding
                remove hdr."Transfer-Encoding";
            }
            if (async_response.body && !hdr."Content-Length" && !hdr."Transfer-Encoding") {
                hdr."Content-Length" = string(async_response.body.size());
            }
            # Log the response
            if (request_helper.log_info) {
                logResponse(cx, async_response);
            }

            # For multiplexed protocols (HTTP/2, HTTP/3), don't close the connection
            # based on cx.close — the stream is closed independently
            bool do_close = !is_multiplexed && (async_response.close || !s.isOpen());
            # the controller sends the response and reports its transmission result
            *code<nothing(bool)> after_send;
            if (*ResponseAfterSendNotifier notifier = remove async_response.after_send) {
                if (is_multiplexed) {
                    watchMultiplexedResponseSend(cx."header-info", notifier);
                } else {
                    after_send = sub (bool sent) {
                        notifier.notify(sent);
                    };
                }
            }
            # a response to a HEAD request has no message body, also an error message that was built with one
            bool is_head = !is_multiplexed && cx.hdr.method == "HEAD";
            return <HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo>{
                "head": is_head,
                "code": async_response.code,
                "status_message": HttpServer::HttpCodes{async_response.code},
                "hdr": hdr,
                "body": is_head ? NOTHING : async_response.body,
                "input_stream": is_head ? NOTHING : async_response.input_stream,
                "send_callback": is_head ? NOTHING : async_response.send_callback,
                "send_timeout": async_response.send_timeout,
                "chunked": async_response.chunked && !is_multiplexed && !is_head,
                "close": do_close,
                "return_to_idle": !do_close,
                "ttl": serv.getTtl(),
                "after_send": after_send,
            };
        }

        if (request_helper.isDedicated()) {
            # Connection was handed to a dedicated handler (WebSocket/SSE)
            return <HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo>{"dedicated": True};
        }

        # No response captured - handler may have sent response directly or failed
        bool response_sent = cx."response-sent" ?? False;
        if (!response_sent) {
            # Handler failed to send response - send 500 error
            string err = sprintf("handler %y returned without sending a response", cx.handler_name);
            logError("%s", err);
            if (is_multiplexed) {
                # Multiplexed: return the error response for the controller to send asynchronously
                # Do NOT call sendHttpError synchronously - it races with the async I/O controller
                hash<HttpAsyncResponseInfo> err_resp = serv.buildAsyncErrorResponse(500, err, cx,
                    self, s, False);
                if (err_resp.body && !err_resp.hdr."Content-Length") {
                    err_resp.hdr."Content-Length" = string(err_resp.body.size());
                }
                logResponse(cx, err_resp);
                return <HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo>{
                    "code": 500,
                    "status_message": HttpServer::HttpCodes{500},
                    "hdr": err_resp.hdr,
                    "body": err_resp.body,
                    "close": False,
                    "return_to_idle": True,
                    "ttl": serv.getTtl(),
                };
            }
            cx.close = True;
            serv.sendHttpError(self, cx, s, 500, err, NOTHING, NOTHING, cx."response-encoding", False);
        }

        if ((!cx.close || is_multiplexed) && s.isOpen()) {
            return <HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo>{
                "return_to_idle": True,
                "ttl": serv.getTtl(),
            };
        }
        return <HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo>{"close": True};
    }

    #! Sends a response synchronously (used when switching from async to sync mode for persistent handlers)
    private sendResponseSync(Socket s, hash<HttpAsyncResponseInfo> response) {
        @assert(response.code, "response.code must be set");
        # an HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 response is written by the I/O thread after it has been submitted here, so its
        # transmission is watched; an HTTP/1.x response has been written when the send call returns
        *ResponseAfterSendNotifier after_send = remove response.after_send;
        if (after_send && (response.cx."header-info".http2 || response.cx."header-info".http3)) {
            watchMultiplexedResponseSend(response.cx."header-info", after_send);
            remove after_send;
        }
        @debug {
            auto body_size = response.body ? response.body.size() : 0;
            log("DEBUG: sendResponseSync: code=%d body_size=%d chunked=%y http2=%y http3=%y",
                response.code, body_size, response.chunked, response.cx."header-info".http2,
                response.cx."header-info".http3);
        }
        try {
            if (response.cx."header-info".http2) {
                # HTTP/2: send via HTTP/2 DATA frames
                int stream_id = response.cx."header-info".stream_id;
                hash<auto> hdr = response.hdr ?? {};
                remove hdr."Transfer-Encoding";
                AbstractPollOperation op;
                if (response.input_stream) {
                    # Use streaming response to avoid memory buffering
                    # Stream was unassigned in doResponse(); reassign to current thread so
                    # startPollSendHttp2StreamingResponse() can access it (it will unassign again)
                    response.input_stream.reassignThread();
                    op = s.startPollSendHttp2StreamingResponse(stream_id, response.code, hdr,
                        response.input_stream);
                } else {
                    if (response.body && !hdr."Content-Length") {
                        hdr."Content-Length" = string(response.body.size());
                    }
                    op = s.startPollSendHttp2Response(stream_id, response.code, hdr,
                        response.body);
                }
                serv.execAsyncPollOperation(s, op);
            } else if (response.cx."header-info".http3) {
                # HTTP/3: send via QUIC stream
                int session_id = response.cx."header-info".session_id;
                int stream_id = response.cx."header-info".stream_id;
                hash<auto> hdr = response.hdr ?? {};
                remove hdr."Transfer-Encoding";
                if (response.input_stream) {
                    # HTTP/3: stream InputStream response incrementally
                    # Stream was unassigned in doResponse(); reassign to current thread so
                    # startPollQuicSendStreamingResponse() can access it (it will unassign again)
                    response.input_stream.reassignThread();
                    AbstractPollOperation op = s.startPollQuicSendStreamingResponse(
                        session_id, stream_id, response.code, hdr, response.input_stream);
                    serv.execAsyncPollOperation(s, op);
                } else {
                    s.submitQuicResponse(session_id, stream_id, response.code, hdr,
                        response.body);
                }
            } else if (response.send_callback) {
                if (response.cx."header-info".http2) {
                    # HTTP/2: wrap send_callback in CallbackInputStream and stream via DATA frames
                    int stream_id = response.cx."header-info".stream_id;
                    hash<auto> hdr = response.hdr ?? {};
                    remove hdr."Transfer-Encoding";
                    auto cb = response.send_callback;
                    InputStream is = new CallbackInputStream(
                        auto sub() { return cb(); }, response.cx.socketobject);
                    AbstractPollOperation op = s.startPollSendHttp2StreamingResponse(
                        stream_id, response.code, hdr, is);
                    serv.execAsyncPollOperation(s, op);
                } else {
                    # HTTP/1.x: send the callback's data framed as the headers declare; with chunked transfer
                    # encoding, each return value is one chunk (e.g. DataStream protocol)
                    http_send_streamed_response(s, response.code, HttpServer::HttpCodes{response.code},
                        response.hdr ?? {}, response.send_callback, response.send_timeout ?? -1);
                }
            } else if (response.input_stream) {
                # HTTP/1.x: send the InputStream framed as the headers declare
                InputStream is = response.input_stream;
                is.reassignThread();
                http_send_streamed_response(s, response.code, HttpServer::HttpCodes{response.code},
                    response.hdr ?? {}, auto sub () { return is.read(65536); }, response.send_timeout ?? -1);
            } else if (response.cx.hdr.method == "HEAD" && response.code >= 200) {
                # HTTP/1.x: a response to a HEAD request has no Content-Length added (RFC 9110 section 9.3.2)
                serv.execAsyncPollOperation(s, s.startPollSendHttpResponse(response.code,
                    HttpServer::HttpCodes{response.code}, "1.1", response.hdr ?? {}, NOTHING, True));
            } else {
                # HTTP/1.x: send normal response
                s.sendHTTPResponse(response.code, HttpServer::HttpCodes{response.code}, "1.1",
                    response.hdr ?? {}, response.body);
            }
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            log("ERROR: Failed to send response synchronously: %s: %s", ex.err, ex.desc);
            if (after_send) {
                after_send.notify(False);
            }
            rethrow;
        }
        if (after_send) {
            after_send.notify(True);
        }
        logResponse(response.cx, response);
    }

    #! Watches the transmission of a multiplexed response so that its after_send callback is called
    /** Must be called before the response is submitted on the stream.

        @param header_info the request header info, whose \c watch_response_send closure the async I/O controller
        provides for HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 streams
        @param after_send the response's after_send notifier
    */
    private watchMultiplexedResponseSend(hash<auto> header_info, ResponseAfterSendNotifier after_send) {
        *code<nothing(code<nothing(bool)>)> watch = header_info.watch_response_send;
        if (!watch) {
            logError("cannot report the transmission result of the response on stream %d to its after_send callback: "
                "the connection does not support it", header_info.stream_id);
            after_send.notify(False);
            return;
        }
        watch(sub (bool sent) {
            after_send.notify(sent);
        });
    }

    #! Sends a multiplexed response from a handler pool thread using the protocol-appropriate submit API
    /** Uses the thread-safe submit APIs that queue data for the I/O thread, avoiding conflicts
        with the I/O thread's poll operation.  Supports both HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 (QUIC).

        @param s the socket
        @param header_info the request header info containing protocol details (http2/http3,
            stream_id, session_id, io_wake)
        @param response the async response data
    */
    private sendMultiplexedResponse(Socket s, hash<auto> header_info,
            hash<HttpAsyncResponseInfo> response) {
        if (response.after_send) {
            watchMultiplexedResponseSend(header_info, response.after_send);
        }
        int stream_id = header_info.stream_id;
        code<nothing()> io_wake = header_info.io_wake;
        hash<auto> hdr = response.hdr ?? {};
        remove hdr."Transfer-Encoding";

        # Convert send_callback to InputStream
        if (response.send_callback && !response.input_stream) {
            auto cb = response.send_callback;
            response.input_stream = new CallbackInputStream(
                auto sub() { return cb(); }, s);
        }

        # InputStream response body delivery.  The C++ vtable's
        # isIoThreadSafe() check (inside submit*StreamingResponseWithStream)
        # is the authoritative gate — Qore subclass overrides of
        # isIoThreadSafe() do NOT propagate to the C++ vtable.  So:
        #
        #   1. Try the fast path (submit the stream for I/O-thread drain)
        #   2. If the gate returns 1 (rejected → stream is truly unsafe),
        #      spawn a @ref StreamResponseProducer "StreamResponseProducer"
        #      to drain it on a worker thread.
        #
        # This preserves streaming semantics for both paths: memory-backed
        # streams stream directly from the I/O thread; user-defined streams
        # stream from a dedicated producer worker.  Backpressure is driven
        # by the peer's flow-control window in both cases.  read() never
        # runs on the async I/O thread.
        if (header_info.http3) {
            int session_id = header_info.session_id;
            if (response.input_stream) {
                int rv = s.submitQuicStreamingResponseWithStream(
                    session_id, stream_id, response.code, hdr, response.input_stream);
                if (rv) {
                    # C++ gate rejected — spawn a producer.
                    s.submitQuicResponseStreaming(session_id, stream_id, response.code, hdr);
                    response.input_stream.unassignThread();
                    StreamResponseProducer producer(s, stream_id, session_id,
                        response.input_stream, io_wake);
                    producer.start();
                }
                io_wake();
            } else {
                s.submitQuicResponse(session_id, stream_id, response.code, hdr, response.body);
                io_wake();
            }
        } else {
            # HTTP/2
            if (response.input_stream) {
                int rv = s.submitHttp2StreamingResponseWithStream(
                    stream_id, response.code, hdr, response.input_stream);
                if (rv) {
                    # C++ gate rejected — spawn a producer.
                    s.submitHttp2StreamingResponseHeaders(stream_id, response.code, hdr);
                    response.input_stream.unassignThread();
                    StreamResponseProducer producer(s, stream_id, NOTHING,
                        response.input_stream, io_wake);
                    producer.start();
                }
                io_wake();
            } else {
                s.submitHttp2Response(stream_id, response.code, hdr, response.body);
                io_wake();
            }
        }
        logResponse(response.cx, response);
    }

    #! Sends a streaming response inline on the current thread
    /** Used by \c handleQueuedPersistentConnection() to drive \c send_callback
        or \c input_stream responses on the dedicated handler thread, preserving
        thread affinity for thread-local resources (e.g. DatasourcePool
        reservations).

        The response headers are sent first, then the body is produced by
        calling the callback (or reading the stream) in a loop on the current
        thread, sending each chunk as an HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 DATA frame with
        backpressure handling.

        @param s the socket
        @param header_info the request header info (contains stream_id, session_id, io_wake, etc.)
        @param response the response to send

        @since HttpServer 1.x
    */
    private sendMultiplexedStreamingResponseInline(Socket s, hash<auto> header_info,
            hash<HttpAsyncResponseInfo> response) {
        if (response.after_send) {
            watchMultiplexedResponseSend(header_info, response.after_send);
        }
        int stream_id = header_info.stream_id;
        code<nothing()> io_wake = header_info.io_wake;
        hash<auto> hdr = response.hdr ?? {};
        remove hdr."Transfer-Encoding";

        # Build the data-producing callback: either from send_callback
        # directly, or by wrapping an InputStream
        code<*binary()> producer;
        *InputStream reassigned_stream;
        on_exit if (reassigned_stream) {
            try {
                reassigned_stream.unassignThread();
            } catch () {
            }
        }
        if (response.send_callback) {
            auto cb = response.send_callback;
            producer = *binary sub () {
                auto d = cb();
                if (!exists d) {
                    return;
                }
                return d.typeCode() == NT_BINARY ? d : binary(d);
            };
        } else {
            InputStream is = response.input_stream;
            is.reassignThread();
            reassigned_stream = is;
            producer = *binary sub () {
                return is.read(65536);
            };
        }

        # Send response headers (no body, no end_stream)
        if (header_info.http3) {
            int session_id = header_info.session_id;
            s.submitQuicResponseStreaming(session_id, stream_id, response.code, hdr);
            io_wake();

            # Drive the producer loop inline
            while (True) {
                *binary chunk = producer();
                if (!exists chunk) {
                    sendQuicDataWithBackpressure(s, session_id, stream_id,
                        binary(), True, io_wake);
                    break;
                }
                sendQuicDataWithBackpressure(s, session_id, stream_id,
                    chunk, False, io_wake);
            }
        } else {
            # HTTP/2
            s.submitHttp2StreamingResponseHeaders(stream_id, response.code, hdr);
            io_wake();

            while (True) {
                *binary chunk = producer();
                if (!exists chunk) {
                    sendHttp2DataWithBackpressure(s, stream_id, binary(),
                        True, io_wake);
                    break;
                }
                sendHttp2DataWithBackpressure(s, stream_id, chunk, False,
                    io_wake);
            }
        }
        logResponse(response.cx, response);
    }

    #! Sends HTTP/2 DATA with backpressure handling
    private sendHttp2DataWithBackpressure(Socket s, int stream_id, binary data,
            bool end_stream, code<nothing()> io_wake) {
        int retries = 0;
        while (True) {
            bool backpressure = False;
            try {
                s.sendHttp2StreamData(stream_id, data, end_stream);
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                if (ex.err == "HTTP2-FLOW-CONTROL") {
                    backpressure = True;
                } else {
                    rethrow;
                }
            }
            io_wake();
            if (!backpressure) {
                return;
            }
            if (++retries > 60) {
                throw "STREAM-BACKPRESSURE-TIMEOUT",
                    sprintf("stream %d buffer still full after %d drain attempts",
                        stream_id, retries);
            }
            s.waitForHttp2StreamDrain(stream_id, 30000);
        }
    }

    #! Sends HTTP/3 (QUIC) DATA with backpressure handling
    private sendQuicDataWithBackpressure(Socket s, int session_id, int stream_id,
            binary data, bool end_stream, code<nothing()> io_wake) {
        int retries = 0;
        while (True) {
            int rv = s.submitQuicStreamData(session_id, stream_id, data,
                end_stream);
            if (rv == 1) {
                io_wake();
                if (++retries > 60) {
                    throw "STREAM-BACKPRESSURE-TIMEOUT",
                        sprintf("stream %d buffer still full after %d drain "
                            "attempts", stream_id, retries);
                }
                s.waitForQuicStreamDrain(session_id, stream_id, 30000);
                continue;
            }
            if (rv != 0) {
                throw "STREAM-SEND-ERROR",
                    sprintf("unexpected rv=%d from submitQuicStreamData on "
                        "stream %d", rv, stream_id);
            }
            io_wake();
            return;
        }
    }

    #! Cleans up a multiplexed request body stream after handler dispatch
    private cleanupMultiplexedBodyStream(Socket s, hash<auto> header_info) {
        on_exit if (header_info.io_wake) {
            header_info.io_wake();
        }
        if (header_info.http3) {
            s.cleanupQuicStream(header_info.session_id, header_info.stream_id);
        } else {
            s.cleanupHttp2Stream(header_info.stream_id);
        }
    }

    #! Resumes queued HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 reads after the persistent handler has
    #! consumed a queued request
    private resumeQueuedPersistentEntry(hash<auto> entry) {
        if (!entry.hasKey("resume_reading")) {
            return;
        }

        *code<nothing()> resume_reading = entry{"resume_reading"};
        if (!resume_reading) {
            return;
        }

        try {
            resume_reading();
        } catch () {
            # already resumed — ignore
        }
    }

    #! Handles persistent connections via a dedicated handler pool thread
    /** This method is called when a handler opts into persistence.  It enters a loop reading
        subsequent requests from a Queue (populated by the I/O controller) and processes them
        on the current thread, ensuring
        thread affinity for thread-local resources like database transactions.

        @param request_helper the request lifecycle helper
        @param pq the Queue to receive subsequent requests from
        @param s the socket
        @param cx the connection context (reference, modified per request)
        @param phi the persistent handler info
        @param info peer info for logging
        @param deregister_fn callback to deregister the persistent queue
        @param sock_key the socket unique hash identifying the connection
    */
    private handleQueuedPersistentConnection(RequestHelper request_helper, Queue pq, Socket s,
            reference<hash<auto>> cx, HttpPersistentHandlerInfo phi, hash<auto> info,
            code<nothing(string)> deregister_fn, string sock_key) {
        @assert(phi.handler, "phi.handler must be set when entering queued persistent mode");
        @assert(phi.handler.isPersistent(), "handler must be persistent when entering "
            "queued persistent mode");
        log("cid %d: entering queued persistent loop", cx.id);

        # Static listener context template
        hash<auto> cx_listener = getContextTemplate(info);
        *Queue close_ack;

        # Clean up persistent handler state and thread-local data on exit
        on_exit {
            # Register all remaining cleanup as exception-safe on-exit actions.
            # The acknowledgement is deliberately registered first so it runs
            # last: signalPersistentSessionClose() must not return until the
            # close callback and both thread-local removals have completed.
            on_exit if (close_ack) {
                close_ack.push(True);
            }
            on_exit remove_thread_data("svc_listener");
            on_exit removeUserThreadContext();
            phi.clear();
        }

        # Some handlers report persistence that legitimately toggles
        # per-request (e.g. a handler that pins a thread only for the
        # duration of an open transaction) while the underlying client
        # connection continues to carry further persistent requests
        # multiplexed over the same HTTP/2 stream set or HTTP/3 QUIC
        # session.  For such handlers, tearing the dedicated thread down
        # (and deregistering the persistent queue) on every per-request
        # persistence clear races the next in-flight request on the same
        # multiplexed connection: it is dispatched to a fresh pool thread
        # that has none of the dedicated thread's thread-local resources.
        # When the handler asks to keep the connection dedicated for its
        # full lifetime, the dedicated thread is released only on
        # connection close, an explicit end-of-session request, or the
        # idle TTL (the contract documented in the QUEUE-TIMEOUT branch
        # below).  The persistent-queue filter (getPersistentQueueFilter())
        # already restricts which requests are routed to the dedicated
        # thread, so unrelated requests on the same connection are
        # unaffected.  Default handler behaviour (one-shot persistence) is
        # unchanged: the flag stays False and the dedicated thread is
        # released as soon as persistence clears.
        bool keep_connection_dedicated =
            phi.handler && phi.handler.keepPersistentConnectionDedicated();

        try {
            while (!exit && s.isOpen()) {
                # Before blocking on the queue, check whether the handler still
                # reports itself persistent.  A handler may release its
                # persistence context between the previous request's completion
                # and the start of the next loop iteration — persistence
                # depends on whatever thread-pinning context the handler cares
                # about (an open SQL statement, a bidi message exchange, a
                # transient lock, etc.), not on any one specific stream shape.
                # Without this check the loop would idle until the TTL
                # (default 75 s) expires, making client-side close latency
                # visibly bad for every handler that naturally terminates its
                # persistence on request completion.
                #
                # Handlers that opt into connection-lifetime dedication
                # (keepPersistentConnectionDedicated()) are excepted: their
                # persistence legitimately toggles per-request while the
                # connection keeps carrying further persistent requests, so
                # the loop must keep waiting for the next request here and
                # release the dedicated thread only on connection close, an
                # explicit end-of-session request, or the idle TTL (handled
                # in the QUEUE-TIMEOUT branch below).
                if ((!phi.handler || !phi.handler.isPersistent())
                        && !keep_connection_dedicated) {
                    log("cid %d: handler no longer persistent, exiting "
                        "dedicated mode immediately", cx.id);
                    deregister_fn(sock_key);
                    drainOrphanedPersistentRequests(request_helper, pq, s,
                        \cx, cx_listener, phi, info, \close_ack);
                    return;
                }
                # Wait for next request from Queue with idle timeout
                date ttl = serv.getTtl();
                *hash<auto> entry;
                try {
                    entry = pq.get(ttl);
                } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                    if (ex.err == "QUEUE-TIMEOUT") {
                        if (!phi.handler || !phi.handler.isPersistent()) {
                            # Non-persistent handler timed out; exit the loop.
                            # For persistent handlers, the client must
                            # explicitly end the session (recognised by the
                            # handler via isPersistentSessionEndRequest()),
                            # or TCP connection close triggers cleanup via
                            # signalPersistentQueuesForConnection().
                            log("cid %d: queued persistent idle timeout, "
                                "exiting dedicated mode", cx.id);
                            deregister_fn(sock_key);
                            drainOrphanedPersistentRequests(request_helper, pq,
                                s, \cx, cx_listener, phi, info, \close_ack);
                            return;
                        }
                        continue;
                    }
                    rethrow;
                }

                # Check for close signal or empty entry
                if (!entry || entry.type == "close") {
                    if (entry) {
                        close_ack = entry.close_ack;
                    }
                    log("cid %d: queued persistent connection closed", cx.id);
                    return;
                }

                # Extract request data from queue entry
                hash<auto> conn_info = entry.conn_info;
                hash<auto> req_hdr = conn_info.hdr;
                bool is_multiplexed = (conn_info.header_info.http2 ?? False)
                    || (conn_info.header_info.http3 ?? False);

                # Check for a handler-defined end-of-session signal — the
                # client is explicitly ending the persistent session (e.g.,
                # before clearing its client-side persistent flag).  The
                # server does not interpret any application header itself;
                # it asks the handler (isPersistentSessionEndRequest()).
                # Send a 200 OK response and exit the loop; the on_exit
                # block will call phi.clear() → persistentClosed() →
                # release any handler-held context.
                if (phi.handler
                        && phi.handler.isPersistentSessionEndRequest(req_hdr)) {
                    log("cid %d: received end-persistent signal, terminating "
                        "persistent session", cx.id);
                    hash<auto> end_cx = cx_listener;
                    end_cx."header-info" = conn_info.header_info;
                    end_cx.close = conn_info.header_info.close;
                    hash<HttpAsyncResponseInfo> end_response = <HttpAsyncResponseInfo>{
                        "code": 200,
                        "hdr": {"content-type": "text/plain"},
                        "body": "OK",
                        "cx": end_cx,
                    };
                    if (is_multiplexed) {
                        sendMultiplexedResponse(s, conn_info.header_info, end_response);
                    } else {
                        sendResponseSync(s, end_response);
                    }
                    resumeQueuedPersistentEntry(entry);
                    deregister_fn(sock_key);
                    drainOrphanedPersistentRequests(request_helper, pq, s,
                        \cx, cx_listener, phi, info, \close_ack);
                    if (!is_multiplexed && s.isOpen()) {
                        if (end_cx.close) {
                            serv.closeConnectionAfterResponse(s, end_cx, self);
                        } else {
                            serv.returnConnectionToAsyncIo(s, end_cx, self);
                        }
                    }
                    return;
                }

                # Reset context for new request; whether its INFO messages are logged is decided for each request
                cx = cx_listener;
                request_helper.log_info = isLogInfoEnabled();

                # Context setup — mirrors processNativeRequest()
                cx.raw_path = req_hdr.path;
                req_hdr.path = decodeRequestPath(req_hdr.path);
                cx += {
                    "orig_path": req_hdr.path,
                    "masked_path": maskRequestPath(cx.raw_path),
                };
                if (req_hdr.path =~ /^\//) {
                    splice req_hdr.path, 0, 1;
                }
                if (req_hdr.authorization) {
                    cx.auth = req_hdr.authorization;
                }
                if (req_hdr."content-type") {
                    req_hdr."_qore_orig_content_type" = req_hdr."content-type";
                }
                if (conn_info.header_info."body-content-type".val()) {
                    req_hdr."content-type" = conn_info.header_info."body-content-type";
                    if (req_hdr."content-type".typeCode() == NT_LIST) {
                        req_hdr."content-type" = req_hdr."content-type"[0];
                    }
                }
                cx.hdr = req_hdr;
                cx.char_encoding = s.getEncoding();
                cx."response-encoding" = conn_info.header_info."accept-charset";
                cx.close = conn_info.header_info.close;

                # Log the request; the message is built only if it is logged
                if (request_helper.log_info) {
                    string http_ver = req_hdr.http_version =~ /^HTTP\//
                        ? req_hdr.http_version : "HTTP/" + req_hdr.http_version;
                    string lstr = sprintf("cid %d src %y: %s %s %s (agent: %y", cx.id,
                        cx."peer-info".address_desc, req_hdr.method, cx.masked_path,
                        http_ver, req_hdr."user-agent");
                    if (req_hdr."_qore_orig_content_type") {
                        lstr += sprintf(" Content-Type: %y", req_hdr."_qore_orig_content_type");
                    }
                    if (ssl) {
                        lstr += getSslLogSuffix(s, conn_info.header_info);
                    }
                    lstr += is_multiplexed ? " [persistent-mux]" : " [persistent-h1]";
                    lstr += ")";
                    log("%s", lstr);
                }

                cx.uctx = get_thread_data("uctx");
                if (conn_info.header_info.body_streaming) {
                    conn_info.header_info.sock = s;
                }
                cx."header-info" = conn_info.header_info;

                if (get_remote_certs) {
                    if (conn_info.header_info.http3) {
                        cx."client-cert" = s.getQuicPeerCertificate(
                            conn_info.header_info.session_id);
                    } else {
                        cx."client-cert" = s.getRemoteCertificate();
                    }
                }

                # Process request
                hash<HttpAsyncResponseInfo> async_response = <HttpAsyncResponseInfo>{};
                # Ensure h2op is resumed even if handleRequest() or response
                # sending throws — otherwise the connection is stuck.
                on_exit {
                    resumeQueuedPersistentEntry(entry);
                }
                handleRequest(request_helper, self, s, \cx, req_hdr,
                    req_hdr.method == "HEAD", phi, info, \async_response);

                # If handler took over the socket (dedicated), we're done
                if (request_helper.isDedicated()) {
                    return;
                }

                # Send response.  For send_callback
                # responses, drive the callback inline on the current
                # (dedicated) thread to preserve thread affinity for
                # thread-local resources (e.g. DSP reservations).
                if (async_response && async_response.code) {
                    if (is_multiplexed) {
                        if (async_response.send_callback || async_response.input_stream) {
                            sendMultiplexedStreamingResponseInline(s,
                                conn_info.header_info, async_response);
                        } else {
                            sendMultiplexedResponse(s, conn_info.header_info,
                                async_response);
                        }
                    } else {
                        sendResponseSync(s, async_response);
                    }
                }
                # Drop response refs so any framework objects captured by the
                # send_callback / input_stream (e.g. RestStreamRequest) can run
                # their destructors before the persistence re-check below.  See
                # the matching block in the initial-response path for rationale.
                if (async_response) {
                    remove async_response.send_callback;
                    remove async_response.input_stream;
                }

                # For body_streaming requests where the handler returned early
                # (e.g., error before reading all client data), clean up the
                # dispatched stream now that the handler is done.  The response
                # has already been queued via sendMultiplexedResponse() which
                # uses nghttp2's internal state, not our streams map.
                if (is_multiplexed && conn_info.header_info.body_streaming) {
                    cleanupMultiplexedBodyStream(s, conn_info.header_info);
                }

                if (!is_multiplexed && (cx.close || !s.isOpen())) {
                    # the response was sent, and the connection closes after it
                    if (s.isOpen()) {
                        serv.closeConnectionAfterResponse(s, cx, self);
                    }
                    return;
                }

                # Check if persistence was cleared by this request (e.g. commit/rollback).
                # Multiplexed requests also check user thread context, since
                # persistenceThreadTerminate() clears the uctx but not the handler's
                # internal persistence flag.  Plain H1 persistent handlers may not
                # use uctx at all, so their handler flag remains authoritative.
                *hash<auto> uctx = get_thread_data("uctx");
                bool persistence_data_cleared = is_multiplexed && !uctx.persistent_data;
                # Handlers that opt into connection-lifetime dedication keep
                # the dedicated thread bound across per-request persistence
                # clears (e.g. between sequential transactions multiplexed on
                # the same connection); the loop instead waits for the next
                # request and releases only on connection close, an explicit
                # end-of-session request, or the idle TTL.
                if ((!phi.handler || !phi.handler.isPersistent()
                            || persistence_data_cleared)
                        && !keep_connection_dedicated) {
                    log("cid %d: persistence cleared after request, exiting "
                        "dedicated mode", cx.id);
                    # Deregister the persistent queue BEFORE returning to prevent
                    # a race condition: the I/O thread could receive and dispatch
                    # a new request to this queue between our return and the
                    # caller's on_exit deregistration.  After deregistration, any
                    # new requests go through the normal handler pool.
                    deregister_fn(sock_key);
                    # Process any requests that were pushed to the queue during the
                    # brief window between the last pq.get() and the deregistration
                    drainOrphanedPersistentRequests(request_helper, pq, s, \cx,
                        cx_listener, phi, info, \close_ack);
                    if (!is_multiplexed && !cx.close && s.isOpen()) {
                        serv.returnConnectionToAsyncIo(s, cx, self);
                    }
                    return;
                }

                if (!is_multiplexed && s.isOpen()) {
                    serv.returnConnectionToAsyncIo(s, cx, self);
                }
            }
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            string etxt = serv.getDebug()
                ? sprintf("%s: %s: %s", get_ex_pos(ex), ex.err, ex.desc)
                : get_exception_string(ex);
            logError("cid %d: queued persistent handler error: %s", cx.id, etxt);
            # Drain any requests routed onto the persistent queue between
            # the last pq.get() and this error.  Without this drain, a
            # request queued while we were processing the failing request
            # (e.g. an end-of-session cleanup sent in parallel with another
            # request, or any second request on the same connection that
            # matched the persistence filter) is stranded on the queue:
            # deregister stops future dispatches but does not drain what
            # was already enqueued.  The client then waits for its full
            # request_timeout (typically 120 s) before retrying on a
            # fresh connection.
            deregister_fn(sock_key);
            drainOrphanedPersistentRequests(request_helper, pq, s, \cx,
                cx_listener, phi, info, \close_ack);
        }
    }

    #! Drains and processes any orphaned requests from a persistent queue
    /** Called after the persistent queue has been deregistered to handle requests
        that were pushed during the brief race window between the persistent loop's
        last \c pq.get() and the deregistration.  After deregistration, no new items
        can be added, so this loop is bounded.

        Orphaned requests are processed as non-persistent requests since persistence
        has already been cleared.

        @param request_helper the request lifecycle helper
        @param pq the persistent queue to drain
        @param s the socket
        @param cx the connection context (reference, modified per request)
        @param cx_listener the listener context template
        @param phi the persistent handler info (persistence already cleared)
        @param info peer info for logging
        @param close_ack receives the completion queue from an orphaned close request
    */
    private drainOrphanedPersistentRequests(RequestHelper request_helper, Queue pq,
            Socket s, reference<hash<auto>> cx, hash<auto> cx_listener,
            HttpPersistentHandlerInfo phi, hash<auto> info,
            reference<*Queue> close_ack) {
        while (True) {
            *hash<auto> entry;
            try {
                entry = pq.get(-1);
            } catch () {
                # QUEUE-TIMEOUT — queue is empty
                break;
            }
            if (!entry) {
                break;
            }
            if (entry.type == "close") {
                close_ack = entry.close_ack;
                break;
            }

            log("cid %d: processing orphaned request from persistent queue race window",
                cx.id);

            # Context setup — mirrors the persistent loop processing
            hash<auto> conn_info = entry.conn_info;
            hash<auto> req_hdr = conn_info.hdr;

            cx = cx_listener;
            # whether the request's INFO messages are logged is decided for each request
            request_helper.log_info = isLogInfoEnabled();
            cx.raw_path = req_hdr.path;
            req_hdr.path = decodeRequestPath(req_hdr.path);
            cx += {
                "orig_path": req_hdr.path,
                "masked_path": maskRequestPath(cx.raw_path),
            };
            if (req_hdr.path =~ /^\//) {
                splice req_hdr.path, 0, 1;
            }
            if (req_hdr.authorization) {
                cx.auth = req_hdr.authorization;
            }
            if (req_hdr."content-type") {
                req_hdr."_qore_orig_content_type" = req_hdr."content-type";
            }
            if (conn_info.header_info."body-content-type".val()) {
                req_hdr."content-type" = conn_info.header_info."body-content-type";
                if (req_hdr."content-type".typeCode() == NT_LIST) {
                    req_hdr."content-type" = req_hdr."content-type"[0];
                }
            }
            cx.hdr = req_hdr;
            cx.char_encoding = s.getEncoding();
            cx."response-encoding" = conn_info.header_info."accept-charset";
            cx.close = conn_info.header_info.close;

            # Log the request; the message is built only if it is logged
            if (request_helper.log_info) {
                string http_ver = req_hdr.http_version =~ /^HTTP\//
                    ? req_hdr.http_version : "HTTP/" + req_hdr.http_version;
                string lstr = sprintf("cid %d src %y: %s %s %s (agent: %y",
                    cx.id, cx."peer-info".address_desc, req_hdr.method,
                    cx.masked_path, http_ver, req_hdr."user-agent");
                if (req_hdr."_qore_orig_content_type") {
                    lstr += sprintf(" Content-Type: %y",
                        req_hdr."_qore_orig_content_type");
                }
                if (ssl) {
                    lstr += getSslLogSuffix(s, conn_info.header_info);
                }
                lstr += " [orphaned-persistent-drain]";
                lstr += ")";
                log("%s", lstr);
            }

            cx.uctx = get_thread_data("uctx");
            if (conn_info.header_info.body_streaming) {
                conn_info.header_info.sock = s;
            }
            cx."header-info" = conn_info.header_info;

            if (get_remote_certs) {
                if (conn_info.header_info.http3) {
                    cx."client-cert" = s.getQuicPeerCertificate(
                        conn_info.header_info.session_id);
                } else {
                    cx."client-cert" = s.getRemoteCertificate();
                }
            }

            # Process request
            hash<HttpAsyncResponseInfo> async_response = <HttpAsyncResponseInfo>{};
            on_exit {
                resumeQueuedPersistentEntry(entry);
            }

            try {
                handleRequest(request_helper, self, s, \cx, req_hdr,
                    req_hdr.method == "HEAD", phi, info, \async_response);

                if (request_helper.isDedicated()) {
                    return;
                }

                bool is_multiplexed = (conn_info.header_info.http2 ?? False)
                    || (conn_info.header_info.http3 ?? False);
                if (async_response && async_response.code) {
                    if (is_multiplexed) {
                        if (async_response.send_callback
                                || async_response.input_stream) {
                            sendMultiplexedStreamingResponseInline(s,
                                conn_info.header_info, async_response);
                        } else {
                            sendMultiplexedResponse(s, conn_info.header_info,
                                async_response);
                        }
                    } else {
                        sendResponseSync(s, async_response);
                    }
                }
                if (async_response) {
                    remove async_response.send_callback;
                    remove async_response.input_stream;
                }

                if (is_multiplexed && conn_info.header_info.body_streaming) {
                    cleanupMultiplexedBodyStream(s, conn_info.header_info);
                }
            } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
                string etxt = serv.getDebug()
                    ? sprintf("%s: %s: %s", get_ex_pos(ex), ex.err, ex.desc)
                    : get_exception_string(ex);
                logError("cid %d: error processing orphaned request: %s", cx.id,
                    etxt);
            }
        }
    }
}
}

#! Private namespace
namespace Priv {
#! a running server's logger set on the global async I/O controller; see HttpServer::setControllerLogger()
hashdecl ControllerLoggerInfo {
    #! the server's unique hash
    string owner;
    #! the server's logger
    LoggerInterface logger;
    #! the controller's logger that the server's replaced, restored when the server stops
    *LoggerInterfaceBase prev;
}

#! the information about the names of the fields of the configured response headers of a server (HttpServer::hdr_info)
hashdecl ConfiguredHeaderInfo {
    #! the configured response headers that the information is about
    hash<auto> hdr;
    #! @ref True if the names of the fields managed by the server are canonical in \c hdr
    bool canonical;
    #! the lower-case names of the fields in \c hdr, each between newlines
    list<string> merge_names;
}

# class containing handler info
class HandlerInfo {
    public {
        string name;
        HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler obj;
        string path;
        bool isregex;
        # content type map
        *hash<string, bool> content_type_map;
        *list<string> special_header_list;
    }

    #! Creates the object from the parameters
    /** @throw HANDLER-ERROR the handler is missing from the \a info argument
    */
    constructor(string name, hash<HttpHandlerConfigInfo> info) {
        self.name = name;
        if (!info.handler) {
            throw "HANDLER-ERROR", sprintf("missing \"handler\" from handler config: %y", info);
        }
        obj = info.handler;
        path = info.path;
        isregex = info.isregex;
        content_type_map = map {$1: True}, info.content_types;
        special_header_list = map $1.lwr(), info.headers;
    }

    bool matchContentType(string ct) {
        return content_type_map{ct} ? True : False;
    }

    #! called when matching a request; returns a code giving the match level; 0 = no match, 1 = only the Content-Type matches, 2 = special header matches, 3 = URL matches
    int matchRequest(hash<auto> hdr, int score) {
        if (path && hdr.path && isregex && regex(hdr.path, path)) {
            return 3;
        }
        # the case where isregex is False was already handled by HttpHandlerList::findHandler using TreeMap
        # we get here only if the path does not match

        if (score >= 2) {
            return 0;
        }

        if (hdr{special_header_list}) {
            return 2;
        }

        if (score || !content_type_map) {
            return 0;
        }

        return content_type_map.(hdr."content-type") ? 1 : 0;
    }

    stop() {
        # stop handler if it is a dedicated socket handler
        if (obj instanceof HttpServer::AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface) {
            cast<HttpServer::AbstractHttpSocketHandlerInterface>(obj).stop();
        }
    }
}

# class to implement handler-handling (private)
class HttpHandlerList {
    public {
        hash<string, HandlerInfo> handlers;
        TreeMap treeMap();
    }

    /*
    private static checkSpecialHeaders(reference sh) {
        foreach auto h in (\sh) {
            if (h.typeCode() != NT_STRING)
                throw "SETHANDLER-ERROR", sprintf("entry %d in the special header list is not a string; type: %s", $#, h.type());
            # make sure header is in lower case for matching
            h = tolower(h);
        }
    }
    */

    #! sets a request handler according to the arguments given
    setHandler(string name, hash<HttpHandlerConfigInfo> info) {
        if (handlers{name}) {
            throw "SETHANDLER-ERROR", sprintf("HTTP request handler %y already exists", name);
        }
        # paths are passed without the leading "/", so they have to be like this in the treemap
        if (info.path =~ /^\//) {
            info.path = info.path.substr(1);
        }
        # for backwards-compatibility, isregex = True if not set
        if (!exists info.isregex) {
            info.isregex = True;
        }

        HandlerInfo handlerInfo(name, info);
        handlers{name} = handlerInfo;

        if (!info.isregex && info.path) {
            treeMap.put(info.path, handlerInfo);
        }
    }

    #! remove handler from the list by its name
    /**
        @throw INVALID-HANDLER-ERROR invalid handler name passed
    */
    removeHandler(string handler_name) {
        if (!handlers.hasKey(handler_name)) {
            throw "INVALID-HANDLER-ERROR", sprintf("handler with name %y is not present", handler_name);
        }

        HandlerInfo hi = handlers{handler_name};
        treeMap.take(hi.path);
        remove handlers{handler_name};
        hi.stop();
    }

    #! remove handler from the list
    /**
        @throw INVALID-HANDLER-ERROR invalid handler passed
    */
    removeHandler(HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler handler) {
        *string name;
        foreach hash<auto> h in (handlers.pairIterator()) {
            if (h.value.obj == handler) {
                name = h.key;
                break;
            }
        }
        if (name == NOTHING) {
            throw "INVALID-HANDLER-ERROR", "handler not present";
        }

        HandlerInfo hi = handlers{name};
        treeMap.take(hi.path);
        remove handlers{name};
        hi.stop();
    }

    # matches a handler to the request
    *HandlerInfo findHandler(hash<auto> hdr, reference<int> score, bool finalv = False,
            *reference<string> root_path) {
        if (!handlers)
            return;

        # if there is only one handler and final is true, then return the only handler
        if (finalv && handlers.size() == 1)
            return handlers.firstValue();

        *HandlerInfo handlerInfo = treeMap.get(hdr.path);
        if (handlerInfo) {
            # must set score to 3 to stop searching; a path match trumps all other matches
            score = 3;
            root_path = handlerInfo.path;
            return handlerInfo;
        }

        # find a handler for the request
        HandlerInfo rv;

        foreach HandlerInfo hi in (handlers.iterator()) {
            int scr = hi.matchRequest(hdr, score);
            #printf("findHandler() path: %s ct: %y %y score: %d (regex: %y)\n", hdr.path, hdr."content-type", hi.name,
            #    scr, hi.isregex);
            if (scr > score) {
                rv = hi;
                score = scr;
                if (scr == 3)
                    break;
            }
        }
        return rv;
    }

    bool empty() {
        return handlers.empty();
    }

    int size() {
        return handlers.size();
    }
}

# class containing dynamic handler info
class DynamicHandlerInfo inherits HandlerInfo {
    public {
        WaitGroup counter();
    }

    constructor(string name, hash<HttpHandlerConfigInfo> info) : HandlerInfo(name, info) {
    }
}

# maintains the request count for dynamic handlers (private)
class DynamicHandlerHelper {
    public {}

    private {
        WaitGroup c;
    }

    constructor(WaitGroup n_c) {
        c = n_c;
        c.add();
    }

    destructor() {
        c.done();
    }
}

# for dynamic handler-handling (private)
class DynamicHttpHandlerList inherits HttpHandlerList {
    private {
        RWLock dhl();

        # issue #4173: allow dynamic handlers to be disabled before being removed
        hash<string, bool> disable_map;
    }

    #! sets a dynamic request handler according to the arguments given
    setHandler(string name, hash<HttpHandlerConfigInfo> info) {
        # paths are passed without the leading "/", so they have to be like this in the treemap
        if (info.path =~ /^\//) {
            info.path = info.path.substr(1);
        }
        # for backwards-compatibility, isregex = True if not set
        if (!exists info.isregex) {
            info.isregex = True;
        }

        DynamicHandlerInfo dhi(name, info);

        {
            dhl.writeLock();
            on_exit dhl.writeUnlock();

            if (handlers{name}) {
                throw "SETHANDLER-ERROR", sprintf("dynamic HTTP request handler %y already exists (dynamic "
                    "handlers: %y)", name, keys handlers);
            }

            handlers{name} = dhi;
        }

        if (!info.isregex && info.path) {
            treeMap.put(info.path, dhi);
        }
    }

    #! remove dynamic handler
    /** @throw REMOVEHANDLER-ERROR if the given dynamic handler does not exist
    */
    removeHandler(string name, *bool force_immediate) {
        WaitGroup c;
        {
            # grab exclusive lock
            dhl.writeLock();
            on_exit dhl.writeUnlock();

            # if no such handler exists, throw an exception
            if (!handlers{name}) {
                throw "REMOVEHANDLER-ERROR", sprintf("dynamic request handler %y does not exist", name);
            }

            # remove the handler from the list
            DynamicHandlerInfo dhi = remove handlers{name};

            # remove handler from treemap if applicable
            if (!dhi.isregex && exists dhi.path)
                treeMap.take(dhi.path);

            # take a copy of a reference to the WaitGroup
            c = dhi.counter;

            # stop handler if it is a dedicated socket handler
            dhi.stop();

            # issue #4173: remove disabled entry, if any
            remove disable_map{name};
        }

        # wait for the call counter to reach zero
        if (!force_immediate) {
            c.wait();
        }
    }

    #! Disable dynamic handler
    /** @throw DISABLEHANDLER-ERROR if the given dynamic handler does not exist
    */
    disableHandler(string name) {
        # grab exclusive lock
        dhl.writeLock();
        on_exit dhl.writeUnlock();

        # if no such handler exists, throw an exception
        if (!handlers{name}) {
            throw "DISABLEHANDLER-ERROR", sprintf("dynamic request handler %y does not exist", name);
        }

        disable_map{name} = True;
    }

    *DynamicHandlerInfo findHandler(hash<auto> hdr, reference<int> score, reference<DynamicHandlerHelper> dhh,
            *reference<string> root_path) {
        dhl.readLock();
        on_exit dhl.readUnlock();

        *DynamicHandlerInfo h = HttpHandlerList::findHandler(hdr, \score, False, \root_path);
        if (!h || disable_map{h.name}) {
            return;
        }

        dhh = new DynamicHandlerHelper(h.counter);
        return h;
    }
}

class HttpServerCallbackAppender inherits LoggerAppenderWithLayout {
    private {
        code<nothing(string)> logfunc;
    }

    constructor(code<nothing(string)> logfunc) : LoggerAppenderWithLayout("HttpServer", new LoggerLayoutPattern("%d T%t [%p]: %m")) {
        self.logfunc = logfunc;
        open();
    }

    processEventImpl(int type, auto params) {
        switch (type) {
            case EVENT_LOG:
                call_function(logfunc, params);
                break;
        }
    }
}

#! Native async target implementing HttpAsyncTarget for the callback-based HttpAsyncSocketIoController
/** Request dispatch uses a two-path model:
    -# @ref tryInlineRequest() runs on an async callback dispatcher worker for lightweight requests
       (HTTP/1.x only, no body, handler opts in via
       @ref HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler::canHandleInline() "canHandleInline()"); returns the response immediately without a thread hop
    -# If @ref tryInlineRequest() returns @ref nothing, the request is dispatched to the
       handler thread pool via @ref onHttpRequest() which calls
       HttpListener::processNativeRequest() for full context setup, body reading, and handler
       dispatch

    @since HttpServer 1.6
    @private
*/
class HttpServerNativeAsyncTarget inherits HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpAsyncTarget {
    private {
        #! Logger interface
        *LoggerInterface logger;
    }

    #! Creates the native async target
    /** @param logger optional logger interface
    */
    constructor(*LoggerInterface logger) {
        self.logger = logger;
    }

    #! Attempts to handle a request on the async callback dispatcher worker
    /** Looks up the handler and, if it supports inline processing and the request
        has no body, processes it directly without thread pool dispatch.

        Only for HTTP/1.x — HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 always return @ref nothing. Requests with a
        body (Content-Length or Transfer-Encoding) are rejected because the inline
        path does not read the body; unread bytes would corrupt the next header
        parse on keep-alive connections.

        @param info connection and request information
        @return result hash if handled inline, or @ref nothing to dispatch to thread pool

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    *hash<HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo> tryInlineRequest(
            hash<HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpConnectionInfo> info) {
        # Only for HTTP/1.x — HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 always use thread pool
        if (info.header_info.http2 || info.header_info.http3) {
            return NOTHING;
        }
        # Reject requests with a body — the inline path does not read the body
        if (info.hdr."content-length" || info.hdr."transfer-encoding") {
            return NOTHING;
        }
        HttpListener listener = cast<HttpListener>(info.listener);
        *AbstractHttpRequestHandler handler = listener.findHandlerForInlineCheck(info.hdr);
        if (!handler || !handler.canHandleInline(info.cx, info.hdr)) {
            return NOTHING;
        }
        return listener.processInlineRequest(info.sock, info.cx, info.hdr, handler, info.header_info);
    }

    #! Called when an HTTP request is ready (header fully parsed)
    /** Dispatches the request to HttpListener.processNativeRequest() which handles
        context setup, request processing, and returns response data for the controller
        to send.

        @param info connection and request information
        @return instructions for connection handling including response data
    */
    hash<HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpRequestResultInfo> onHttpRequest(hash<HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpConnectionInfo> info) {
        HttpListener listener = cast<HttpListener>(info.listener);
        # Full path — build proper connection context using the listener's template
        # The controller's cx only has "peer" and "socket" keys; the listener's
        # getContextTemplate() builds the full context with "peer-info", "id",
        # "ssl", "listener-id", "listener-name", "async_ctrl"
        hash<auto> cx = listener.getContextTemplate(info.cx.peer);
        if (exists info.cx{"peer-info-future"}) {
            cx{"peer-info-future"} = info.cx{"peer-info-future"};
        }
        return listener.processNativeRequest(info.sock, cx, info.hdr, info.header_info);
    }

    #! Called when an idle connection times out
    /** Closes the socket when the keep-alive TTL expires.

        @param sock the socket that timed out
        @param listener the listener that accepted this connection
        @param cx connection context
    */
    onIdleTimeout(Socket sock, *HttpServer::HttpListenerInterface listener, hash<auto> cx) {
        log(LoggerLevel::DEBUG, "idle timeout for connection %y", sock.uniqueHash());
        try {
            sock.close();
        } catch () {
            # Ignore close errors
        }
    }

    #! Called on I/O errors
    /** Logs the error and closes the socket.

        @param sock the socket where the error occurred
        @param ex exception information
        @param listener the listener for this connection
        @param cx connection context
    */
    onError(Socket sock, hash<ExceptionInfo> ex, *HttpServer::HttpListenerInterface listener, *hash<auto> cx) {
        log(LoggerLevel::ERROR, "async I/O error for %y: %s: %s", sock.uniqueHash(), ex.err, ex.desc);
        try {
            sock.close();
        } catch () {
            # Ignore close errors
        }
    }

    #! Called when an SSE client disconnects
    /** Closes the socket.

        @param sock the socket that disconnected
        @param listener the listener for this connection
        @param cx connection context
    */
    onSseDisconnect(Socket sock, *HttpServer::HttpListenerInterface listener, hash<auto> cx) {
        log(LoggerLevel::DEBUG, "SSE disconnect for %y", sock.uniqueHash());
        try {
            sock.close();
        } catch () {
            # Ignore close errors
        }
    }

    #! Called when a WebSocket frame is received
    /** Not used in the current HttpServer flow - WebSocket connections are managed
        by dedicated handler threads, not by the async I/O controller.
    */
    onWebSocketFrame(Socket sock, hash<HttpServerAsyncIo::WebSocketFrameInfo> frame,
            *HttpServer::HttpListenerInterface listener, hash<auto> cx) {
        # WebSocket frames are handled by dedicated handler threads
    }

    #! Called when a WebSocket connection closes
    /** Closes the socket.

        @param sock the WebSocket socket
        @param code close status code
        @param reason close reason text
        @param listener the listener for this connection
        @param cx connection context
    */
    onWebSocketClose(Socket sock, int code, string reason, *HttpServer::HttpListenerInterface listener, hash<auto> cx) {
        log(LoggerLevel::DEBUG, "WebSocket close for %y: code=%d reason=%y", sock.uniqueHash(), code, reason);
        try {
            sock.close();
        } catch () {
            # Ignore close errors
        }
    }

    #! Logs a message
    private log(int level, string fmt) {
        # the message is formatted only if it is logged: this is called several times for every request
        if (logger && logger.isEnabledFor(level)) {
            # use "%s" wrapper so the already-formatted message is treated as a
            # literal — re-passing it as Logger::log()'s format string would
            # re-interpret any embedded "%XY" (e.g. URL-encoded "%2F") and
            # collapse pure "%s" results to empty output
            logger.log(level, "%s", vsprintf(fmt, argv));
        }
    }
}

#! Internal hashdecl for async response data
/** Used to capture response data for async sending instead of synchronous send.
    @private
*/
hashdecl HttpAsyncResponseInfo {
    #! HTTP status code
    int code;

    #! Response headers
    hash<auto> hdr;

    #! Response body (NOTHING if no body)
    *data body;

    #! InputStream for streaming response body (used for HTTP/2 streaming)
    /** When set, the body is read from this stream incrementally instead of
        being buffered in memory. Takes precedence over \c body for HTTP/2 responses.

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    *InputStream input_stream;

    #! Whether this is a chunked response
    bool chunked = False;

    #! Whether to close the connection after sending
    bool close = False;

    #! Connection context (for keep-alive)
    hash<auto> cx;

    #! Listener reference
    HttpListener listener;

    #! callback for sending chunked response data where each callback return is one HTTP chunk
    /** @since HttpServer 1.6

        @see send_timeout
    */
    *code<auto()> send_callback;

    #! timeout for send_callback responses
    /** @since HttpServer 1.6

        @see send_callback
    */
    *timeout send_timeout;

    #! Whether this is a streaming response already sent by the handler
    /** Set when a handler sends a streaming response (e.g., HTTP/3 SSE) directly
        via protocol-specific APIs and sets \c reply_sent in the response.
        The controller should not send a response — it just acknowledges and continues.

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    bool streaming = False;

    #! The type of streaming connection: "sse" or "websocket"
    /** Only used when \c streaming is @ref True.

        @since HttpServer 1.6
    */
    *string stream_type;

    #! The poll operation for the streaming connection
    /** Only used when \c streaming is @ref True. Set to an
        @ref HttpServerAsyncIo::HttpSseStreamPollOperation for H1 SSE connections.

        @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    *AbstractPollOperation stream_operation;

    #! Reports the transmission result of the response to the handler's after_send callback
    /** @since HttpServer 1.7
    */
    *ResponseAfterSendNotifier after_send;
}

#! Calls the after_send callback of a response exactly once
/** @see HttpServer::HttpResponseInfo::after_send

    @since HttpServer 1.7
*/
class ResponseAfterSendNotifier {
    private:internal {
        #! the callback
        code<nothing(hash<auto>, bool)> callback;
        #! the request context
        hash<auto> cx;
        #! the handler whose thread-local data the callback runs under
        *HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler handler;
        #! the listener for logging errors
        HttpListener listener;
        #! protects called
        Mutex m();
        #! True once the result has been reported
        bool called = False;
    }

    #! Creates the object
    constructor(code<nothing(hash<auto>, bool)> callback, hash<auto> cx,
            *HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler handler, HttpListener listener) {
        self.callback = callback;
        self.cx = cx;
        self.handler = handler;
        self.listener = listener;
    }

    #! Reports that the response was not sent if no result was reported
    /** Every path that sends a response reports its result; this ensures that the callback is called even if the
        response is discarded without being sent.  The callback runs in a new thread, as the destructor may run on
        any thread.
    */
    destructor() {
        if (called) {
            return;
        }
        called = True;
        try {
            background ResponseAfterSendNotifier::call(callback, cx, handler, listener, False);
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            listener.logError("cid %d: %s: cannot call the after_send callback: %s: %s", cx.id, cx.handler_name,
                ex.err, ex.desc);
        }
    }

    #! Reports the transmission result of the response; only the first call has any effect
    /** Must not be called on an I/O thread, as the callback runs on the calling thread.

        @param sent @ref True "True" if the response has been completely sent, @ref False "False" if not
    */
    notify(bool sent) {
        {
            AutoLock al(m);
            if (called) {
                return;
            }
            called = True;
        }
        ResponseAfterSendNotifier::call(callback, cx, handler, listener, sent);
    }

    #! Calls the callback under the handler's thread-local data; exceptions are logged
    private:internal static call(code<nothing(hash<auto>, bool)> callback, hash<auto> cx,
            *HttpServer::AbstractHttpRequestHandler handler, HttpListener listener, bool sent) {
        try {
            if (handler) {
                *hash<auto> tld_save = handler.saveThreadLocalData();
                on_exit handler.restoreThreadLocalData(tld_save);
                callback(cx, sent);
            } else {
                callback(cx, sent);
            }
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            listener.logError("cid %d: %s: error in after_send callback: %s", cx.id, cx.handler_name,
                get_exception_string(ex));
        }
    }
}
} # namespace Priv

namespace Priv {
#! Runs the event source of a Server-Sent Events stream: starts it with the stream and stops it exactly once
/** The stream's end (the controller's close callback on HTTP/1.x, the peer-close notification on HTTP/2 and HTTP/3,
    or a close by the source itself) calls @ref end(), which runs the source's stop on a thread of the handler pool;
    a source whose stream could not be set up is stopped by the request's thread (@ref stopHere()).  See qore#5758.
*/
class SseEventSourceRun {
    private {
        #! the source; released when it has been stopped, as it can refer to its stream, which refers to this object
        *HttpServerAsyncIo::AbstractSseEventSource source;
        HttpListener listener;
        softstring cid;
        *string handler_name;
        #! runs code on a thread of the handler pool (see HttpServer::getHandlerPoolSubmitter())
        code<nothing(code<nothing()>)> submitter;
        Mutex m();
        bool started;
        bool stopped;
        #! True if the source pushes the events of a stream of the async I/O controller
        bool used;
    }

    constructor(HttpServerAsyncIo::AbstractSseEventSource source, HttpListener listener, softstring cid,
            *string handler_name, code<nothing(code<nothing()>)> submitter, bool used) {
        self.source = source;
        self.listener = listener;
        self.cid = cid;
        self.handler_name = handler_name;
        self.submitter = submitter;
        self.used = used;
    }

    #! A used source whose stream was never set up (the run is no longer referenced before start() was called) is
    #! stopped here, so that it always releases what it registered
    /** A source that is not used is stopped by HttpServer::takeSseSource(), unless the response's \c send_callback
        still sends its events after this object is gone.
    */
    destructor() {
        if (used && !started) {
            stopHere();
        }
    }

    #! Returns @ref True if the source pushes the events of a stream of the async I/O controller
    bool isUsed() {
        return used;
    }

    #! Returns @ref True once @ref start() has been called
    bool isStarted() {
        AutoLock al(m);
        return started;
    }

    #! Starts the source with the stream; never throws
    /** An exception of the source is logged and closes the stream, whose end stops the source.
    */
    start(HttpServerAsyncIo::AbstractSseConnection conn) {
        {
            AutoLock al(m);
            started = True;
        }
        *HttpServerAsyncIo::AbstractSseEventSource src;
        {
            AutoLock al(m);
            src = source;
        }
        # stopped already (the stream ended before it was started)
        if (!src) {
            return;
        }
        SseEventSourceStream stream(conn, self);
        try {
            src.start(stream);
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            listener.logError("cid %s: the SSE event source of handler %y failed to start; closing the stream: %s",
                cid, handler_name, get_exception_string(ex));
            stream.close();
        }
    }

    #! Called when the stream has ended; stops the source once, on a thread of the handler pool
    end() {
        if (!claim()) {
            return;
        }
        try {
            submitter(sub () {
                stopIntern();
            });
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            # the stop must still run once
            stopIntern();
        }
    }

    #! Stops the source once in the calling thread; used when its stream was never set up
    stopHere() {
        if (!claim()) {
            return;
        }
        stopIntern();
    }

    private bool claim() {
        AutoLock al(m);
        if (stopped) {
            return False;
        }
        stopped = True;
        return True;
    }

    private stopIntern() {
        *HttpServerAsyncIo::AbstractSseEventSource src;
        {
            AutoLock al(m);
            src = remove source;
        }
        if (!src) {
            return;
        }
        try {
            src.stop();
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            listener.logError("cid %s: the SSE event source of handler %y could not be stopped: %s", cid,
                handler_name, get_exception_string(ex));
        }
    }
}

#! The stream given to an SSE event source: a close or abort by the source also ends the source's run
/** On HTTP/2 and HTTP/3 a stream that the server ends itself is not reported by the peer-close notification, so
    the source's own close ends its run here; the other end paths are reported by the transport.
*/
class SseEventSourceStream inherits HttpServerAsyncIo::AbstractSseConnection {
    private {
        HttpServerAsyncIo::AbstractSseConnection conn;
        SseEventSourceRun run;
    }

    constructor(HttpServerAsyncIo::AbstractSseConnection conn, SseEventSourceRun run) {
        self.conn = conn;
        self.run = run;
    }

    string getProtocol() {
        return conn.getProtocol();
    }

    queueEvent(*string event_type, string data, *string id, *int retry) {
        conn.queueEvent(event_type, data, id, retry);
    }

    queueRawEvent(hash<auto> event) {
        conn.queueRawEvent(event);
    }

    queueRawString(string data) {
        conn.queueRawString(data);
    }

    setHeartbeat(int interval_ms, string msg = HttpServerAsyncIo::DefaultSseHeartbeatMsg) {
        conn.setHeartbeat(interval_ms, msg);
    }

    setMaxBufferSize(int size) {
        conn.setMaxBufferSize(size);
    }

    int getMaxBufferSize() {
        return conn.getMaxBufferSize();
    }

    bool hasMaxBufferSize() {
        return conn.hasMaxBufferSize();
    }

    setWarningCallback(*code<nothing(string)> cb) {
        conn.setWarningCallback(cb);
    }

    bool hasWarningCallback() {
        return conn.hasWarningCallback();
    }

    bool isOpen() {
        return conn.isOpen();
    }

    bool isDisconnected() {
        return conn.isDisconnected();
    }

    int getQueueSize() {
        return conn.getQueueSize();
    }

    close() {
        on_exit run.end();
        conn.close();
    }

    abort() {
        on_exit run.end();
        conn.abort();
    }
}

#! Holds the request helper of a dedicated connection that its handler manages externally until it has ended
/** The request helper counts the connection in the listener's dedicated connections (which a stop of the listener
    waits for) until it is destroyed; the holder is released by the server's end of the connection when the
    connection has ended, or by the server at once when the handler does not manage the connection externally.
*/
class DedicatedConnectionHolder {
    private {
        Mutex m();
        *RequestHelper rh;
        #! counts the connection from accept() until release()
        ExternalConnectionKeeper keeper;
        bool claimed;
        bool accepted;
    }

    constructor(RequestHelper rh, ExternalConnectionKeeper keeper) {
        self.rh = rh;
        self.keeper = keeper;
    }

    #! Called by the handler when it takes over the end of the connection (it runs the server's end when the
    #! connection has ended)
    /** From here on the connection keeps the Program of the server alive until it has ended (see
        @ref ExternalConnectionKeeper)
    */
    accept() {
        AutoLock al(m);
        if (accepted || !rh) {
            return;
        }
        accepted = True;
        keeper.connectionOpened();
    }

    #! Returns @ref True if the handler took over the end of the connection
    bool isAccepted() {
        AutoLock al(m);
        return accepted;
    }

    #! Claims the end of the connection; returns @ref True for the first call only
    bool claim() {
        AutoLock al(m);
        if (claimed) {
            return False;
        }
        claimed = True;
        return True;
    }

    #! Releases the request helper, which then no longer counts the connection, once the connection has ended
    release() {
        *RequestHelper r;
        bool counted;
        {
            AutoLock al(m);
            r = remove rh;
            counted = r && accepted;
        }
        # the connection is no longer counted, also if the destruction of the helper raises an exception
        on_exit if (counted) {
            keeper.connectionEnded();
        }
        # the helper is destroyed here if this was its last reference, outside the lock
        r = NOTHING;
    }
}

#! Keeps the Program of a server alive while a connection that its handler manages externally is open
/** A connection whose handler manages it externally (an asynchronous WebSocket connection, see
    \c WebSocketHandler::startImpl()) has no thread that waits for it, so nothing keeps the Program that runs the
    server from ending while it is open: once the Program has ended (all its threads have), it is deleted, the end of
    the connection, which runs the connection's close callbacks in the Program, can no longer run, and the objects that
    those callbacks release (often ones that hold the connection's context, and with it the listener and the server)
    keep the server and the threads of its handler pool alive, so the process never ends (qore#5752; seen with
    \c qdbg-server, whose debugged program ends before its debugger's connection closes).  When a thread of the
    handler pool waited for each connection, the Program waited for the connection as it waits for its threads.

    One thread, which is not a thread of the handler pool, waits while the server has such connections: the Program
    then waits for them as before, and their ends always run in it.  A stop of the server ends the connections, and
    with them the thread.
*/
class ExternalConnectionKeeper {
    private {
        Mutex m();
        Condition cond();
        #! the number of open connections
        int count;
        #! True while the waiting thread runs
        bool waiting;
    }

    #! Counts an open connection; starts the waiting thread if it does not run
    connectionOpened() {
        AutoLock al(m);
        ++count;
        if (waiting) {
            return;
        }
        try {
            background waitForConnections();
            waiting = True;
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            # no thread can be started (a thread limit): the connection works, but the Program does not wait for it
        }
    }

    #! Ends the count of a connection that has ended
    connectionEnded() {
        AutoLock al(m);
        @assert(count > 0, "ExternalConnectionKeeper::connectionEnded() without connectionOpened()");
        if (!--count) {
            cond.broadcast();
        }
    }

    #! The waiting thread: returns when no connection is open
    private waitForConnections() {
        # a cancellation of this thread while it takes the lock leaves waiting set, so that no thread waits for the
        # server's connections any more; the Program then ends as it did without this class
        AutoLock al(m);
        # a later connection starts a new thread (the lock is still held when this runs)
        on_exit waiting = False;
        try {
            while (count) {
                cond.wait(m);
            }
        } catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
            # the thread was cancelled: the Program no longer waits for the connections
        }
    }
}

#! Helper class for managing HTTP request lifecycle and thread counter tracking
/**
    This class manages the lifecycle of HTTP connections and ensures proper counter
    management for graceful server shutdown. It tracks two types of connections:

    1. <b>Normal connections (cThreads)</b>: Regular HTTP request/response connections
       that are handled by the thread pool and released after the response is sent.

    2. <b>Dedicated connections (dThreads)</b>: Long-lived connections like WebSocket
       or SSE that take over the socket for bidirectional communication.

    ## WaitGroup Management

    The server uses WaitGroups to track active connections so that shutdown can wait
    for all connections to complete. The flow is:

    1. When a request starts: <tt>cThreads.add()</tt> is called (before RequestHelper is created)
    2. RequestHelper stores a reference to cThreads in <tt>c</tt>
    3. If the connection becomes dedicated (WebSocket/SSE):
       - <tt>setDedicated()</tt> is called: adds to dThreads, marks cThreads done, switches <tt>c</tt> to dThreads
    4. When RequestHelper is destroyed: <tt>c.done()</tt> decrements the appropriate WaitGroup

    ## Dedicated External Mode

    When a handler takes full control of the socket (e.g., WebSocket handler returns True
    from start()), <tt>setDedicatedExternal()</tt> is called. This:
    - Sets <tt>dedicated_external = True</tt> so the destructor won't close the socket
    - The handler is responsible for socket cleanup
    - The WaitGroup is still decremented in the destructor

    ## Important: WaitGroup Balance

    The WaitGroup add() happens BEFORE RequestHelper is created (in processNativeRequest
    or similar). The done() happens in the destructor. This ensures:
    - Every add() has a matching done()
    - Server shutdown can wait for all WaitGroups to reach zero

    @see HttpListener::cThreads
    @see HttpListener::dThreads
*/
class RequestHelper {
    public {
        #! if True, the request's INFO messages are logged
        /** decided once when the request starts, as the check costs a call to the logger for each message; a change
            of the log level applies from the next request
        */
        bool log_info = True;
    }

    private {
        #! WaitGroup for normal connection threads (decremented if we stay in normal mode)
        WaitGroup cThreads;
        #! WaitGroup for dedicated connection threads (decremented if we switch to dedicated mode)
        WaitGroup dThreads;
        #! Reference to the active WaitGroup (either cThreads or dThreads) - decremented in destructor
        WaitGroup c;
        #! Socket to manage (may be null for async connections where socket is managed elsewhere)
        Socket s;
        #! If True, socket is managed externally (e.g., by WebSocket handler) and won't be closed in destructor
        bool dedicated_external = False;
    }

    /**
        @param cThreads the counter for normal connection threads
        @param dThreads the counter for dedicated connection threads (WebSocket, SSE)
        @param s optional socket to manage (not passed for async connections where socket lifecycle
               is managed by the async I/O controller)

        @note The caller must have already called cThreads.add() before creating this object.
              The destructor will call c.done() to balance this.
    */
    constructor(WaitGroup cThreads, WaitGroup dThreads, *Socket s) {
        @assert(cThreads.getCount() > 0, "cThreads must be incremented before creating RequestHelper");
        self.cThreads = cThreads;
        self.dThreads = dThreads;
        c = cThreads;
        if (s) {
            self.s = s;
        }
    }

    #! Destructor ensures counter is decremented and socket is cleaned up if needed
    /**
        - If not dedicated_external and socket exists and is open: shutdown and close the socket
        - Always decrements the active counter (c) to signal this connection is complete

        This allows HttpListener::stop() to wait for all connections to finish via
        cThreads.wait() and dThreads.wait().

        @note We check isOpen() to avoid double-close if the socket was already closed
        (e.g., by the async I/O controller)
    */
    destructor() {
        if (s && !dedicated_external && s.isOpen()) {
            try {
                s.shutdown();
                s.close();
            } catch () {
                # Ignore close errors - socket may already be in error state
            }
        }
        c.done();
    }

    #! Switches from normal connection mode to dedicated connection mode
    /**
        Called when a connection is upgraded to a dedicated socket handler (WebSocket, SSE).
        This transfers the counter tracking from cThreads to dThreads so that:
        - Normal requests can complete (cThreads reaches zero)
        - Dedicated connections are tracked separately (dThreads)
        - Server shutdown waits for dedicated connections after normal connections

        @note Must only be called once, when c === cThreads (asserted)
    */
    setDedicated() {
        @assert(c === cThreads);
        dThreads.add();
        cThreads.done();
        c = dThreads;
    }

    #! Marks this connection as dedicated and externally managed
    /**
        Called when a dedicated socket handler (e.g., WebSocket) takes full control
        of the connection and will manage the socket lifecycle itself.

        This method:
        1. Switches to dedicated mode if not already (handles race where setDedicated()
           may have been called first in registerDedicatedSocket)
        2. Sets dedicated_external = True so destructor won't close the socket

        The handler that called this is responsible for:
        - Closing the socket when done
        - The connection counter is still decremented in destructor

        @note This fixes a bug where calling setDedicatedExternal() after setDedicated()
              would double-increment dThreads. The check <tt>c === cThreads</tt> prevents this.
    */
    setDedicatedExternal() {
        # Only adjust counters if not already in dedicated mode
        # (setDedicated() may have been called first in registerDedicatedSocket)
        if (c === cThreads) {
            dThreads.add();
            cThreads.done();
            c = dThreads;
        }
        dedicated_external = True;
    }

    #! Returns True if this connection is in dedicated mode (WebSocket, SSE)
    /**
        @return True if setDedicated() or setDedicatedExternal() was called

        @note This returns True for BOTH internally and externally managed dedicated connections.
              To check specifically for external management, use isDedicatedExternal().
              When checking shutdown behavior, isDedicatedExternal() is usually what you want.
    */
    bool isDedicated() {
        return c === dThreads;
    }

    #! Returns True if the socket is managed externally by the dedicated handler
    /**
        @return True if setDedicatedExternal() was called

        @note If this returns True, isDedicated() also returns True.
              This method specifically indicates the socket won't be closed by the destructor.
    */
    bool isDedicatedExternal() {
        return dedicated_external;
    }
}
}
